INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (13, 2, 'Simulation foundations: math, units, bodies, orbits', 700, 'Part 1 of `plan 2: masterplan`: the simulation''s ground, four files that every later part builds on and that never call the HAL or SDL. `math.lisp` gives double vectors and quaternions and the one single-float boundary, `units.lisp` the constants, the calendar and display conversions, `bodies.lisp` the Sun, Earth and Moon with their rotations and ephemerides, `orbits.lisp` osculating elements, Kepler propagation, apsides and radius crossings. `plan 2`''s part 2 (ship, integrator, flight computer) and part 3 (clock, hazards, prediction) depend on it; nothing here integrates the ship.

## Sources and decisions
- Files and load order: `docs/architecture.md` "Files in load order" (`decision 12: Module layout and package split`): `math`, `units`, `bodies`, `orbits`, right after `package` and before `shaders` in `luv-in-space.asd`; tests `tests/math.lisp`, `tests/units.lisp`, `tests/bodies.lisp`, `tests/orbits.lisp` in the same order after `tests/package.lisp` in `luv-in-space/tests`.
- Units, time, frames: `docs/architecture.md` "Units", "Time", "Frames", "Floating-origin pitfalls"; `decision 5: Precision of positions`, `decision 14: Units and time epoch`, `decision 15: Coordinate frames`.
- Ephemerides and gravity constants: "Ephemerides", "Gravity model"; `decision 17: Gravity model`, `decision 18: Ephemerides`.
- Orbits: "Rails", "Hyperbolic rails", "Removal of objects" (the radius crossing); `decision 46: Rails across spheres, hyperbolas and re-entry` (milestone 4 builds the rails on what this part writes).
- Crash radii: `decision 34: How death works`; start orbit: `decision 38: Start orbit: polar`, `docs/design.md` "The start".
- Recorded for this plan: `decision 65: Vectors and quaternions in math.lisp`, `decision 66: Calendar dates on the 1/64 s grid`, `decision 67: Ephemeris cache and the Moon''s series frame`, `decision 68: Orbit elements: one struct for every conic`.
- Tests: `docs/architecture.md` "Testing" → "Rules for tests", "Rails, orbits and frames"; this part''s tests together stay under 0.5 s.

## Rules for every piece
- Pure functions on `double-float` (`decision 14`), typed and fast enough for the integrator (part 2 calls `bodies` four times per step); no mutable globals except the ephemeris cache (`decision 67`); tunables `defparameter`, constants `defconstant` only for numbers that never change (μ, radii) or `defparameter` where `./sly eval` should reach them.
- Every constant cites its source in a short comment beside it (`docs/architecture.md` "Units"); reference values in tests come from closed forms or committed sources, never from a previous run.
- Each piece adds its files to `luv-in-space.asd` (both systems), passes `scripts/test`, and updates `docs/architecture.md` where it refines it (the line given per piece). `AGENTS.md` "Layout": the first piece changes the `src/` bullet from "so far the walking skeleton" to the skeleton plus the simulation files built so far, citing "Files in load order"; later pieces keep it true.
- No luv gap is expected (luv''s `vec3` and `luv/arithmetic` are not used, `decision 65`); one met goes to `docs/luv-capabilities.md` and `docs/luv-wishlist.md`.

## Pieces, in the order they run

### 1. `math.lisp`: vectors, quaternions, the single-float boundary
- `decision 65`: vec3 `(simple-array double-float (3))`, quaternion `(simple-array double-float (4))` as (w x y z); constructors `vec3`, `quat`; `vec3-add`, `-sub`, `-scale`, `-dot`, `-cross`, `-norm`, `-normalize`, `-lerp`, `axpy`-style `vec3-add-scaled`, each with an in-place `!` twin (destination first); 3×3 matrices as `(simple-array double-float (9))` row-major with `mat3*vec3`, transpose, product, inverse of a symmetric matrix (for the inertia tensor in part 2), `mat3-from-quat`.
- Quaternions: product, conjugate, normalise in double, `quat-rotate` (v_inertial = q v_body q*), `quat-from-axis-angle`, `quat-slerp` (shortest arc, for render interpolation), `quat-derivative` q̇ = ½ q ⊗ (0, ω) with ω in body axes (`docs/architecture.md` "Integrator"), a frame from three axes (`quat-from-basis`, for holds and cameras).
- Boundary: `(relative-single p origin &optional out (index 0))`: subtract in double, then coerce, into a single-float array at `index` ("Floating-origin pitfalls").
- Tests `tests/math.lisp` (moved here from "Frame transforms", which names `tests/physics.lisp`): body → inertial → body round trip within 1e-15 for random unit quaternions from a fixed seed list; FRD signs: integrating `quat-derivative` (small fixed steps, or the exact exponential) with a positive pitch rate turns body +x towards inertial −z when q = 1, a positive yaw rate towards +y, a positive roll rate +y towards +z; `quat-slerp` at 0, ½ and 1; `relative-single` of two points 6.8×10^6 m from the origin and 1.25 m apart returns 1.25 exactly while coercing each first does not; symmetric inverse × matrix = identity within 1e-15.
- Docs: `docs/architecture.md` "Frame transforms" points the quaternion tests at `tests/math.lisp`; "Files in load order" for `math.lisp` names the layout of `decision 65`.
- Done: the tests pass; `./sly eval ''(quat-rotate (quat-from-axis-angle (vec3 0 1 0) 0.1d0) (vec3 1 0 0))'' --package LUV-IN-SPACE` gives a vector with negative z.

### 2. `units.lisp`: constants, calendar, display conversions
- Constants with sources ("Units"): μ Sun 1.32712440041279419×10^20, Earth 3.98600435507×10^14, Moon 4.902800118×10^12 m³/s² (DE440, Park et al. 2021; piece 4 verifies the digits against the paper); radii Sun 6.957×10^8 m (IAU 2015 B3), Earth equatorial 6,378,136.6 m, Moon mean 1,737,400 m (IAU WGCCRE 2015); Earth''s J2 1.08263×10^-3 (EGM2008); g₀ 9.80665 m/s²; obliquity ε 84381.448″; the AU 149,597,870,700 m (IAU 2012 B2); TT − UTC 69.184 s; day 86,400 s, Julian century 36,525 days; the fine step 1/64 s as `+fine-step+`.
- Calendar (`decision 66`): `(utc->t year month day hour minute second)` by Fliegel–Van Flandern with integer days and seconds kept apart; `(utc->grid-t …)` rounds to the nearest 1/64 s; `(t->utc t)` returns the six fields (seconds as a double, truncation to whole seconds is the caller''s); `*start-date*` = `(2040 1 1 0 0 0)` and `(start-t)`; `(t->ut1-days t)` = JD_UT1 − 2451545.0 for Earth''s rotation; `(t->julian-centuries t)` for the series (TDB = TT, "Time").
- Display conversions (instruments only, "Units"): metres to km, m/s to km/s, radians to degrees and back, a duration in seconds to fields (days, hours, minutes, seconds) for countdowns, `format-utc` as `2040-01-01 00:00:00`; the readout''s row layout is part 9''s.
- Tests `tests/units.lisp` ("Units" in "Rails, orbits and frames"): J2000.0 (2000-01-01 11:58:55.816 UTC) gives `t` within 1e-9 s of 0; round trips date → `t` → date for J2000.0, the start date and 2040-02-29 12:34:56; `(start-t)` is a multiple of 1/64 s and within 1/128 s of the exact `utc->t`; `format-utc` of `(start-t)` is `2040-01-01 00:00:00`; one day after the start formats as 2040-01-02; the start date lies inside 1800–2050 (`decision 18`); a degree/radian round trip.
- Docs: `docs/architecture.md` "Time" states the grid rounding and `(start-t)` (`decision 66`).
- Done: the tests pass; `units.lisp` cites a source beside every constant.

### 3. `bodies.lisp`, part one: bodies, rotations, body-fixed frames
- A `body` struct and a fixed list (no hash-table iteration): `:sun`, `:earth`, `:moon` with name, μ, radius, crash radius (`decision 34`: Earth R + 100 km, Moon R + 0, Sun R), parent (`:sun` for Earth, `:earth` for the Moon), the parent orbit''s mean semi-major axis and the Laplace sphere a (m/M)^(2/5) (Earth ~9.25×10^8 m, Moon ~6.6×10^7 m; used from milestone 4, here only defined), J2 for Earth only; `(body key)` looks one up.
- Earth''s rotation ("Frames"): Earth Rotation Angle θ = 2π (0.7790572732640 + 1.00273781191135448 D_U), D_U from `t->ut1-days`, the fraction reduced before multiplying by 2π (keep the integer part of D_U apart for precision); inertial → Earth-fixed and back as rotations about ICRF z.
- The Moon''s rotation: IAU WGCCRE 2015 leading terms, α₀ = 269.9949° + 0.0031° T, δ₀ = 66.5392° + 0.0130° T, W = 38.3213° + 13.17635815° d − 1.4×10^-12° d² (T centuries, d days from J2000 TDB), inertial → body-fixed R_z(W) R_x(90° − δ₀) R_z(90° + α₀); the periodic terms are left out until milestone 4 textures the Moon (a `PLACEHOLDER` is not needed: nothing visible depends on them).
- `(body-fixed-matrix body t)` (a mat3, double; the GPU''s single copy is made by the renderer), `(latitude-longitude body r t)` for a body-centred inertial position, `(body-texture-uv direction)` ("Frames": u = 0.5 + atan2(y, x)/(2π), v = 0.5 − asin(z)/π) for the crash marker and the tests.
- Tests `tests/bodies.lisp` ("Frame transforms"): θ at J2000.0 UT1 is 0.7790572732640 rev within 1e-12 rev and advances 1.00273781191135448 rev per UT1 day within 1e-12; body-fixed x maps to (u, v) = (0.5, 0.5), the north pole to v = 0; an inertial point over latitude 0, longitude 0 when θ = 0 is at longitude 90.246° W (0.25 × 1.00273781191135448 rev) 6 UT1 hours later, within 1e-9°; the Laplace spheres within 1 % of the figures above; the Moon''s synchronous rotation is checked in piece 4, which brings its ephemeris.
- Docs: none beyond keeping "Files in load order" true.
- Done: the tests pass.

### 4. `bodies.lisp`, part two: ephemerides and the Hermite cache (`--web`: Horizons vectors and the DE440 digits)
- Models (`decision 18`, `decision 67`): Sun fixed at the heliocentric origin; the Earth–Moon barycentre from Standish Table 1 (its EMB row only; the planets are milestone 4''s), Kepler''s equation by Newton to 1e-14 rad, analytic velocity, ecliptic → ICRF by ε; the Moon from Meeus ch. 47 (tables 47.A and 47.B in full, mean geometric, no nutation), precessed to the J2000 ecliptic by Meeus eq. 21.5, then ICRF; Moon velocity by central difference ±60 s; Earth = EMB − μ_Moon/(μ_Earth + μ_Moon) × Moon.
- Cache (`decision 67`): nodes every 4,096 s of (r, v) for the EMB and the geocentric Moon, each a pure function of its index; cubic Hermite position and its derivative for velocity; direct-mapped, 64 slots per quantity.
- API: `(body-state key t &optional (origin :earth))` → `(values r v)` in the origin-centred ICRF frame, with an in-place variant `(body-state-into r-out v-out key t origin)` for part 2''s gravity; origins `:sun` and `:earth` now (`:moon` too: the geocentric Moon negated plus nothing heliocentric), built by `decision 67`''s rule; `(body-state-series …)` bypasses the cache for tests.
- Tests `tests/bodies.lisp` ("Ephemeris spot checks"):
  - JPL Horizons vectors (geometric, ICRF, TDB) committed as constants with the query in a comment (target, centre, the date fetched): the EMB from the Sun (target 3, centre 500@10) and the Moon from Earth (target 301, centre 500@399), at 2026-01-01, 2040-01-01 and 2049-06-01 00:00 TDB; bounds: EMB direction within 1′ and distance within 1e-4 relative; Moon direction within 1′, distance within 50 km. Mars and Jupiter come with the planets in milestone 4.
  - The Sun from Earth at the start date near right ascension 281°, declination −23° within 0.5° (`docs/design.md` "The start").
  - The cache: Hermite positions match the series within 2 cm for the Moon (and the EMB) over 200 random times in 2040 from a fixed list; a node is bit for bit the same whether computed first or after 100 others (clear the cache between).
  - Earth heliocentric minus EMB is μ_Moon/(μ_Earth + μ_Moon) times the geocentric Moon within 1e-6 m; the Moon from the Sun equals Earth from the Sun plus the Moon from Earth within 1e-4 m.
  - The Moon''s prime meridian (body-fixed +x) within 10° of the direction to Earth at the three dates (piece 3''s rotation).
- Also: check μ of Sun, Earth and Moon digit by digit against Park et al. 2021 (DE440) and the IAU radii; fix `units.lisp` and say so in the done note if one differs.
- Docs: `docs/architecture.md` "Ephemerides": the cached quantities, the global cache and the precession of `decision 67` (replacing "kept in a small table in the `game` object"); "Ephemeris spot checks" notes Mars and Jupiter wait for milestone 4.
- Done: the tests pass with every Horizons query reproducible from its comment; the cost of a cached `body-state-into` is measured (a few hundred ns at most) and written in "Ephemerides".

### 5. `orbits.lisp`: ellipses, Kepler, apsides, radius crossings
- `decision 68`''s `orbit` struct; `(state->orbit r v mu t)` and `(orbit-state orbit t)` → `(values r v)`, through the argument of latitude and true longitude where ω or Ω are conventional; elliptic Kepler''s equation by Newton from a robust start (E₀ = M + e sin M, or π for e > 0.8), to 1e-15 relative.
- Derived: a, p, n, period, apoapsis and periapsis radii, `orbit-energy`, `(orbit-next-apsis orbit t :periapsis|:apoapsis)` times, `(orbit-radius-crossings orbit r t)`: the next inbound and outbound times at radius r after t in closed form ("Removal of objects": θ = ∓arccos((p/r − 1)/e), its eccentric and mean anomaly), nil when r is out of [q, Q].
- `(orbit-j2-rates orbit body)`: Ω̇ = −(3/2) n J2 (R/p)² cos i, ω̇ = (3/4) n J2 (R/p)² (5 cos² i − 1), Ṁ − n = (3/4) n J2 (R/p)² √(1 − e²)(3 cos² i − 1); used by rails from milestone 3 and part 3''s node test.
- Tests `tests/orbits.lisp` ("Kepler round trip"):
  - state → orbit → state within 1e-9 relative (position and velocity) for circular equatorial, circular polar (the start orbit of `decision 38`: a = 6,778,136.6 m, i 90°, Ω 270°, u 0), retrograde i = 150°, e = 0.5 and e = 0.9, each at eight anomalies; orbit → state → orbit likewise in the non-singular combinations of `decision 68`.
  - Kepler propagation t₀ → t₁ → t₀ returns the state within 1e-9 relative; the residual of Kepler''s equation below 1e-14 for e up to 0.99.
  - The start orbit''s period 92.6 min and speed 7,669 m/s (`docs/design.md` "The start") within 0.1 min and 1 m/s; apsides of an 800 × 90 km orbit (radii R + h).
  - Radius crossing: Derelict K-17''s 800 × 90 km orbit (a = 6,823,136.6 m, e = 0.052029) crosses R + 100 km 2,603.07 s after apogee at θ = −14.35° within 0.01 s and 0.01° (the reference of "Removal of objects"; its 1e-6 s test on rails is milestone 3''s); none for periapsis 1 m above the radius.
  - J2 rates: Ω̇ for 400 km at 51.6° about −5.0°/day within 0.1°/day and equal to the closed form within 1e-12 relative; |Ω̇| < 1e-15 rad/s at i = 90°.
  - The start''s lighting (needs piece 4): with the Sun from Earth at `(start-t)`, the start orbit''s β is −10° within 1° and the ship is 25° from the Sun within 1° (`docs/design.md` "The start").
- Docs: `docs/architecture.md` "Rails" names the `orbit` struct (`decision 68`); "Kepler round trip" lists the hyperbolic cases as piece 6''s.
- Done: the tests pass; `./sly eval ''(orbit-period (state->orbit …))''` works on a hand-made state.

### 6. `orbits.lisp`: hyperbolas for the osculating display of an escape
- The same `orbit` struct for e > 1 (`decision 46`, "Hyperbolic rails"): state ↔ orbit, the hyperbolic Kepler equation M = e sinh H − H by Newton from H₀ = sign(M) ln(2|M|/e + 1.8) to 1e-14 relative; position from the perifocal forms of "Hyperbolic rails"; periapsis only (apoapsis and period nil, so the readout shows "—"); `orbit-radius-crossings` for r ≥ q; `(orbit-points orbit t-start t-end n)` for both conics, the map''s ribbon vertices in double around the body (part 8 draws them).
- Milestone 4 keeps: the near-parabolic series for |H| < 0.5, the |e − 1| < 1e-6 nudge, segments across spheres and the passing rock''s tests; until then a state with |e − 1| < 1e-6 is treated as e = 1 + 1e-6 or 1 − 1e-6 on its side without changing the state (display only).
- Tests `tests/orbits.lisp`: round trips for e = 1.5 and e = 3 at H = 0, ±0.5, ±2 within 1e-9 relative; propagation there and back; Kepler''s residual below 1e-14 for |M| from 1e-6 to 1e3; speed at periapsis √(v∞² + 2μ/q) within 1e-12 relative; `orbit-points` of an ellipse lie on it within 1e-6 m of the conic''s radius.
- Docs: `docs/architecture.md` "Hyperbolic rails" says which forms milestone 2 built and which wait for milestone 4.
- Done: the tests pass; the whole part''s tests run in under 0.5 s in `scripts/test`.

## What later parts take from here
- Part 2 (`physics.lisp`, `ship.lisp`): `math` in place forms, `body-state-into` for the Sun and the Moon, μ, J2 and R; the drift tests'' exact Kepler solution from `orbit-state`.
- Part 3 (`clock.lisp`, `hazards.lisp`, `prediction.lisp`): `+fine-step+`, `(start-t)`, crash radii, `orbit-radius-crossings` and `orbit-next-apsis` for events, `latitude-longitude` for the impact point, `orbit-j2-rates` for the polar node test.
- Parts 4–9: `relative-single`, `body-fixed-matrix`, `body-state` for the Sun''s direction and the Moon, `orbit-points`, display conversions and `format-utc`.', '2026-10-14T22:03:09Z', 72);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (14, 2, 'Ship, integrator and flight computer', 680, 'Part 2 of `plan 2: masterplan`: the ship as a rigid body under Newtonian physics and the flight computer that flies it. `physics.lisp` holds the ship''s state, the gravity model, the RK4 step and the step rule; `ship.lisp` the Mule''s parts, mass properties, engine and RCS layout; `controls.lisp` the control state that input, scripts and the autopilot write, the flight computer (direct, rate damping, holds, fine control) and the RCS mixer, and one function that advances the ship by a step. It builds on `plan 13: Simulation foundations: math, units, bodies, orbits` (math, units, bodies, orbits) and is what `plan 2`''s part 3 (clock, warp, hazards, prediction) drives; nothing here knows warp levels, frames or keys.

## Sources and decisions
- Files and load order: `docs/architecture.md` "Files in load order" (`decision 12: Module layout and package split`): `physics`, `ship`, `controls` after `orbits` and before `shaders` in `luv-in-space.asd`; tests `tests/physics.lisp`, `tests/ship.lisp`, `tests/controls.lisp` in the same order after `tests/orbits.lisp` in `luv-in-space/tests`.
- Integrator and gravity: `docs/architecture.md` "Integrator", "Measured drift", "Gravity model"; `decision 16: Integrator and simulation clock`, `decision 17: Gravity model`, `decision 15: Coordinate frames` (FRD, q body → inertial, ω in body axes).
- Warp''s demands on the integrator: `decision 19: Time-warp rules` (kinematic attitude above ×4, thrust cap), `decision 31: Warp choices within the time-warp rules`.
- The ship: `docs/design.md` "The ship(s)" ("Body axes and reference point", "Parts", "Propulsion", "Mass budget and Δv", "Inertia", "RCS forces, torques and accelerations"); `decision 22: The player''s ship and its propulsion`. Running dry does not kill (`decision 34: How death works`).
- Controls: `docs/design.md` "Controls" (the key rules), "Control modes"; `decision 29: Control scheme: key map, control modes, gamepad`; the start''s prograde hold (`decision 38: Start orbit: polar`).
- From part 1 (`plan 13`): `decision 65: Vectors and quaternions in math.lisp` (vec3, quat, mat3, in-place `!` twins, `quat-derivative`, `quat-from-basis`), `body-state-into` (`decision 67: Ephemeris cache and the Moon''s series frame`), μ, R, J2, g₀ and `+fine-step+` from `units.lisp`, `orbit-state` for exact Kepler references (`decision 68: Orbit elements: one struct for every conic`).
- Recorded for this plan: `decision 69: Ship state, step rule and kinematic attitude in physics.lisp`, `decision 70: Flight computer: per-step control laws, holds and the RCS mixer`, `decision 71: The Mule''s mass model in ship.lisp`.
- Tests: `docs/architecture.md` "Testing" → "Rules for tests", "Drift bounds" (`decision 21: Physics test bounds and flight scripts`); this part''s tests together stay under 3 s, leaving part 3 its share of the ~5 s physics budget.

## Rules for every piece
- Simulation files: no HAL, SDL or `*game*`; pure functions on `double-float`, typed and declaimed inline where hot; the step path (gravity, RK4, mass properties, flight computer, mixer) conses nothing (checked by a test with `sb-ext:get-bytes-consed`).
- State only in structs that part 3 copies into its simulation state (`ship-state`, `controls`); each has a deep copy function and `equalp` compares two bit for bit. No mutable globals except caches that are pure functions of their key (the ephemeris cache of `plan 13`).
- Tunables (`5°/s`, `0.5 s`, `0.8`, the settle thresholds) are `defparameter`s with the design section cited beside them; the Mule''s figures are data in `ship.lisp`, each part''s row citing "Parts".
- Each piece adds its files to `luv-in-space.asd` (both systems), passes `scripts/test`, and keeps `AGENTS.md` "Layout" (the `src/` bullet lists the simulation files built so far) and `docs/architecture.md` "Files in load order" true.
- No luv gap is expected (nothing here touches luv); one met goes to `docs/luv-capabilities.md` and `docs/luv-wishlist.md`.

## Pieces, in the order they run

### 1. `physics.lisp`: ship state, gravity, the step rule
- `ship-state` struct (`decision 69`): `r`, `v` (vec3, Earth-centred ICRF in milestone 2), `q` (quat body → inertial), `w` (vec3, body rad/s), `m-p` (propellant kg), `dv` (odometer, m/s); `copy-ship-state` deep; a constructor from (r, v, q, w, m-p).
- `(gravity-into! a r t model)`: `model` `:none`, `:point` (Earth μ), `:j2` (Earth with J2 = 1.08263×10⁻³ and R⊕ equatorial, the pole along ICRF z, in closed form in the frame''s own axes), `:full` (`:j2` plus Sun and Moon as third bodies, μ_b ((d − r)/|d − r|³ − d/|d|³), d from `body-state-into … :earth` at `t`; "Gravity model"). Earth frame only (`plan 2`''s rules); an `ecase` on the central body leaves room for milestone 4''s frames.
- `(coarse-step r t thrusting)`: h = η min over Earth, Moon, Sun of √(d³/μ), η = 1/256, rounded down to a power of two, clamped to [1/64 s, 16,384 s], and ≤ 1 s when thrusting ("Integrator"); `+fine-step+` from `units.lisp` is the fine one. `docs/architecture.md` "Drift bounds" says the rule is `physics.lisp`''s, called by the clock.
- Tests `tests/physics.lisp`:
  - J2 against closed forms: at the equator (r = R + 400 km) the radial acceleration is −μ/r² (1 + 1.5 J2 (R/r)²), over the pole −μ/r² (1 − 3 J2 (R/r)²), each within 1e-14 relative; no tangential component at either; the acceleration is symmetric under z → −z.
  - Third bodies: the Moon''s and Sun''s terms at three points (towards, away from and across the body''s direction at 7×10⁶ m) equal the same formula evaluated in rationals within 1e-9 relative; along the Earth–Moon line the Moon''s term is the tidal 2 μ_☾ r / d³ within 2 %.
  - Step rule: 2 s at 400 km, 512 s at 2×10⁸ m (translunar coast, "Integrator"), 1 s cap when thrusting in LEO''s 2 s, 1/64 s at the clamp (r = R), 16,384 s at the upper clamp; every result a power of two.
- Docs: `docs/architecture.md` "Drift bounds" (step rule from `physics.lisp`), "Files in load order" (`physics.lisp` also holds the step rule).
- Done: the tests pass; `./sly eval ''(coarse-step (vec3 6778136.6d0 0 0) 0d0 nil)'' --package LUV-IN-SPACE` gives 2.0d0.

### 2. `physics.lisp`: the RK4 step and the drift tests
- `(rk4-step! state h t model force-body torque-body mdot inertia inertia-inverse &optional attitude-fn)`: classical RK4 on r, v, q, ω, m_p ("Integrator"): ṙ = v; v̇ = gravity + q (F_body / m) q* with m = m_dry + m_p supplied by the caller (a `mass` argument or folded into the inputs, the worker''s choice, consing nothing); q̇ = `quat-derivative`; I ω̇ = τ − ω × I ω with the full tensor and its inverse held over the step; ṁ_p = −ṁ. After the step: q renormalised in double; the odometer adds |F| / ṁ ln(m₀/m₁), or |F| h / m when ṁ = 0 (`decision 69`).
- Propellant exhaustion: when ṁ h > m_p, every thruster''s output is scaled by m_p / (ṁ h) for this step, so m_p ends at 0.0d0 exactly and the step stays on the grid; at m_p = 0 force, torque and ṁ are zero (no death, `decision 34`). The scale applies to force, torque and ṁ together.
- Kinematic attitude (`decision 69`): with `attitude-fn` (a function of t, r, v returning a quat and the frame rate ω_t in inertial axes) the stages take q from it for the thrust direction, ω and q are not integrated, and the step ends with q and ω (body axes) from the function at t + h; torque is ignored.
- Tests `tests/physics.lisp` ("Drift bounds"):
  - The table of "Drift bounds", two-body (`:point`), periapsis 6,778 km, i 51.6°, Ω 30°, ω 40°, from periapsis, attitude integrated alongside with I = diag(1, 2, 3)×10⁴ kg m² and ω = 0: circular 10 orbits at 1/64 s and e = 0.5 2 orbits at 1/64 s (ΔE/E, ΔL/L < 1e-11, position < 0.05 m), coarse step (`coarse-step`) circular 1,000 orbits (< 1e-10, 5 m), e = 0.5 1,000 orbits (< 1e-9, 100 m), e = 0.9 200 orbits (ΔE/E < 1e-8, ΔL/L < 1e-9, 5 km); errors against `orbit-state` at whole periods, the maximum kept. If the table''s runs don''t fit in ~2 s, the worker shortens the fine-step rows (5 circular orbits, 1 at e = 0.5, bounds unchanged) and says so in the test''s comment.
  - Rotation: torque-free I = diag(1, 2, 3)×10⁴ kg m², |ω| = 0.1 rad/s off the principal axes, 1,000 s at 1/64 s: rotational energy and |I ω| in inertial axes within 1e-9 relative, |q| within 1e-12 of 1; the same with I = R diag(1, 2, 3)×10⁴ Rᵀ, R 10° about body y.
  - Exhaustion: a step with ṁ h = 2 m_p ends with m_p = 0.0d0 exactly and Δv equal to the rocket equation over the burned mass within 1e-12 relative.
  - Kinematic: with an attitude function holding body x along v in a circular orbit, after 100 coarse steps q''s x axis is along v within 1e-12 rad.
  - Allocation: 10,000 steps with `:full` cons less than 1 KB; the same start and inputs twice give `equalp` states.
- Docs: `docs/architecture.md` "Integrator": the exhaustion rule''s scaling of all thrusters, the odometer, the kinematic step (`decision 69`); the cost line with this code''s measured ns per step (`:point`, `:full`, with and without attitude) replacing the stand-in measurement.
- Done: the tests pass inside the budget; the measured cost is in "Integrator".

### 3. `ship.lisp`: the Mule''s parts, mass properties, engine and RCS layout
- Parts (`decision 71`): the "Parts" table as data (name, mass, centre from R in FRD, shape and size), cabling and plumbing at 199 kg so the dry sum is 7,961 kg; precomputed dry mass, first and second moments about R.
- `(ship-mass-properties m-p &optional load)` into preallocated outputs: total mass, CoM (body, from R), tensor about the CoM (full symmetric, parallel-axis rule I = Σ (I_i + m_i (|d|² 𝟙 − d dᵀ))) and its inverse; propellant as "Inertia" says (two solid cylinders r 1.52 m from x 0.50 and 9.76 over f × 8.86 and f × 3.72 m, MR 5.5). `load` is nil in milestone 2: the argument and a `PLACEHOLDER` are not needed, milestone 3''s grapple adds it.
- Engine ("Propulsion"): F = 101,800 N, Isp 449.7 s, v_e = Isp g₀, ṁ = throttle F / v_e; throttle 0 or 0.1–1.0 (`ship-engine-force`, `ship-engine-mdot`); thrust along +x through the CoM, no torque; `(ship-trim-angle m-p)` = atan((z_cm − z_g)/(x_cm − x_g)), gimbal at (0, 0, −0.5), for the meshes of part 6 and the readout.
- RCS ("RCS forces, torques and accelerations"): the 24 thrusters as data, eight blocks at x 0.5 and 14.5, y ±1.8, z ±1.8, each with an axial nozzle (aft blocks +x, front −x) and a y and a z nozzle pushing towards the axis; 445 N, Isp 360 s; per thruster position (from R), direction; `(rcs-matrix! b com)`: the 6×24 force and torque columns about a CoM; the couples (pitch, yaw 2 × 445 × 14.0 N m, roll 4 × 445 × 1.8 N m) and `(rcs-translation-capability axis com)` of `decision 70`.
- `(ship-dv-remaining m-p)`: v_e ln(m / m_dry) ("Mass budget and Δv").
- Tests `tests/ship.lisp`:
  - Mass: dry 7,961 kg and wet 35,961 kg exactly; CoM for the rows Wet, Half (14,000 kg) and Dry of "Inertia" within 0.01 m; I_xx, I_yy, I_zz within 5 % and I_xz within 10 % of the table, the principal tilt within 1° (the table is an independent script''s, `decision 71`); the tensor symmetric, positive definite, tensor × inverse = 𝟙 within 1e-12.
  - Closed forms: a single box part gives m (b² + c²)/12 …; two point masses give the parallel-axis tensor exactly.
  - Trim angle: wet 1.6°, half 0.9°, dry −3.0° within 0.1°, all inside ±4°.
  - RCS: couples 12,460 N m and 3,204 N m; the matrix''s columns sum to zero force and torque for opposed pairs; translation capability ±x 1,780 N, ±y and ±z 1,266 N wet and 1,391 N dry within 1 N.
  - Δv: `ship-dv-remaining` at 28,000 kg is 6,650 m/s within 1 m/s.
  - Thrust ("Drift bounds", Thrust): a constant full-throttle burn in field-free space (`:none`) from wet for 100 s matches Δv = Isp g₀ ln(m₀/m₁) within 1e-9 relative and equals the odometer within 1e-12; a burn of 1,300 s reaches m_p = 0.0d0 exactly in the step containing 28,000 / ṁ = 1,213.2 s and stays there, the mass at 7,961 kg.
- Docs: `docs/design.md` "Parts": cabling and plumbing 199 kg (the dry sum then matches its 7,961 kg); "Inertia": one line naming the shapes `ship.lisp` uses (`decision 71`).
- Done: the tests pass; `./sly eval ''(ship-mass-properties 28000d0)'' --package LUV-IN-SPACE` returns 35,961 kg and CoM (10.34, 0, −0.22).

### 4. `controls.lisp`, part one: control state, the mixer, direct and translation, `flight-step`
- `controls` struct (`decision 70`): throttle lever, rotation keys (−1, 0, +1 per axis: pitch, yaw, roll), translation keys (per body axis), fine flag, attitude mode (`:direct`, `:rate-damping`, `:hold`), hold kind (`:prograde` … `:radial-in`, `:inertial`), inertial target quat, roll offset, settled flag, the last duty cycles (for the meshes'' and the readout''s use); `copy-controls` deep; `(new-controls)` gives rate damping with a prograde hold, throttle off (the start, `decision 38`).
- Commands that input (part 7), flight scripts (part 10) and the autopilot (milestone 3) call, each a function of `controls` with the rule of "Controls": `controls-set-throttle` (below 0.1 is 0), `controls-throttle-full`, `controls-throttle-cut`, `controls-set-rotation-key`, `controls-set-translation-key`, `controls-toggle-fine`; the hold and damping commands follow in piece 5. Throttle ramps in real time are input''s (part 7), refusals above ×4 the clock''s (part 3).
- Mixer (`decision 70`): `(rcs-mix! u b tau-c f-c)`: weighted BVLS with Householder QR, fixed iteration limit, duty cycles in [0, 1], zero command gives zero duty; its cost measured.
- Direct mode: a held rotation key commands its axis''s full couple (×0.1 fine). Translation: the axis''s nominal force from `rcs-translation-capability` (×0.1 fine), with the rotation command, in one mix.
- `(flight-step! state controls h t model &optional attitude-fn)`: mass properties from `m-p`, flight computer (piece 5 adds the closed loops), mixer, force, torque and ṁ of engine plus RCS (ṁ_p = −Σ F_i / (Isp_i g₀)), then `rk4-step!`; with `attitude-fn` (above ×4) no RCS and no flight computer, engine only. This is what part 3''s clock calls once per step.
- Tests `tests/controls.lisp`:
  - Mixer: for 50 commanded torques from a fixed list within 90 % of the couples (wet, half and dry CoM), the resulting torque equals the command within 1e-6 × the couple; all duties in [0, 1]; zero command gives all zeros; a pure +x force command uses only the four aft axial nozzles at 1,780 N with zero torque within 1e-6 N m; a 10 kN +y command with zero torque gives zero torque within 1e-6 N m and a force within 2 % of 1,266 N (wet).
  - Direct taps ("Control modes" table): one fine step of direct pitch from rest changes ω by I⁻¹ τ h within 1e-9 relative (0.027°/s per 1/60 s wet, 0.060 dry); one fine step of +x translation 0.77 mm/s wet within 1 %.
  - Propellant: one second of all-RCS firing burns Σ u_i 445 / (360 g₀) kg within 1e-12 relative; engine and RCS share one pool.
  - The mixer''s cost per call is measured and written in `docs/architecture.md` "Integrator" (a few tens of µs at most; above that the worker records the profile and a follow-up task).
- Docs: `docs/design.md` "RCS forces, torques and accelerations": the mixer runs per fine step, not per frame (`decision 70`); `docs/architecture.md` "Files in load order": `controls.lisp` also holds the flight computer, the mixer and `flight-step!`.
- Done: the tests pass; a `./sly eval` of 64 `flight-step!`s in direct mode with pitch held from the start state shows ω_y = 12,460 N m / I_yy × 1 s within 1e-6 relative (about 0.028 rad/s wet).

### 5. `controls.lisp`, part two: rate damping, holds 1–6 and R, fine control, kinematic holds
- Rate damping (`decision 70`, "Control modes"): τ = I (ω_c − ω) / 0.5 s + ω × I ω, clamped per axis to the couple (×0.1 fine), ω_c 5°/s (0.5°/s fine) on held axes.
- Holds: `(hold-attitude kind r v roll-offset)` → target quat and frame rate from the orbital frame ("Frames": prograde along v, normal along r × v, radial out completing the triple; default roll of `decision 70`); `:inertial` keeps the stored quat; the slew and settle law and the settled hysteresis of `decision 70`.
- Commands with the rules of "Controls": `controls-set-hold` (pressing the active hold again releases to rate damping), `controls-toggle-rate-damping` (T; releases a hold to rate damping), `controls-inertial-hold` (R: the current attitude); a pitch or yaw key releases any hold; a roll key during a hold turns the roll offset at the rate-damping rate.
- `(controls-attitude-fn controls)`: the active hold''s attitude function for `rk4-step!`''s kinematic mode above ×4 (part 3 sets an inertial hold first when there is none, `decision 19`).
- Tests `tests/controls.lisp` (the design''s numbers from "Control modes"):
  - Rate damping: from rest with pitch held, ω reaches 95 % of 5°/s within 3.5 s wet and 2.0 s dry (the saturated ramp plus the 0.5 s tail); released, |ω| < 0.02°/s within 6 s wet; fine control reaches 0.5°/s and never exceeds it by more than 1 %.
  - Holds: from a start 90° off prograde in the 400 km polar orbit (`:full` gravity), the prograde hold reports settled within 40 s wet and 35 s dry (the times measured go in the test''s comment), the angle then stays < 0.1° and the rate error < 0.02°/s for 120 s as the orbital frame turns; settling within 40 s for retrograde, normal, anti-normal, radial out and in, and R after a tumble at 2°/s; the default roll puts body −z within 0.1° of radial out (towards normal for the radial holds); a roll key held 2 s during a prograde hold changes the roll by 10° within 0.5° and the hold keeps it.
  - Key rules: pressing 1 twice gives rate damping; W releases a hold; T releases a hold to rate damping and toggles direct otherwise.
  - Kinematic burn ("Drift bounds", Thrust): a 60 s full-throttle prograde burn from the start orbit at the coarse step with the 1 s cap and the kinematic hold matches the same burn at the fine step flown by the hold within 1e-6 relative in Δv and 1 m in apoapsis radius.
  - Determinism: 120 s of a scripted key sequence (fixed list keyed by step) run twice gives `equalp` states and controls.
- Docs: `docs/design.md` "Control modes": one bullet naming the slew law, the settle hysteresis and the roll rule of `decision 70`; `docs/architecture.md` "Testing": a "Controls" bullet list (`tests/controls.lisp`) with the bounds above, beside "Drift bounds".
- Done: the tests pass; part 2''s tests together run under 3 s in `scripts/test` (measured and stated in the done note).

## What later parts take from here
- Part 3 (`clock.lisp`, `hazards.lisp`, `prediction.lisp`): `ship-state` and `controls` inside its simulation state, `flight-step!` per step, `coarse-step`, `controls-attitude-fn` and `controls-inertial-hold` for warp above ×4, the settled flag and |ω| for refusals, `copy-ship-state` and `copy-controls` for checkpoints, the odometer for `:after-dv`.
- Part 6 (`meshes.lisp`): `ship-mass-properties` (the CoM, to place R at r − q c), the parts table, `ship-trim-angle`, the last duty cycles and throttle for the engine glow.
- Part 7 (`input.lisp`): the `controls-…` commands, one per key of "Controls"; Part 9: throttle, propellant, attitude mode and settled for the readout; Part 10: the same commands for script actions.
- Milestone 3: loads (`ship-mass-properties` with a load), the RCS capability with the CoM ahead of the front blocks, target holds 7–0.', '2026-10-14T22:09:56Z', 73);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (15, 2, 'Clock, warp, hazards and events', 660, 'Part 3 of `plan 2: masterplan`: what turns the ship of part 2 into a running simulation. `clock.lisp` holds the simulation state as one copyable object, the start state, the one step function with every rule, the frame accumulator, the eight warp levels with their refusals, thrust limits, drop-outs and pause, and the start-state checkpoint; `hazards.lisp` the swept crash test against Earth and the Moon and death; `prediction.lisp` the milestone-2 events (apsides, impact, a scheduled cut-off) for drop-outs, the readout''s alarm and flight scripts. It builds on `plan 13: Simulation foundations: math, units, bodies, orbits` and `plan 14: Ship, integrator and flight computer`; parts 4 (the frame in `main.lisp`), 7 (warp and pause keys), 9 (readout, messages, LOST panel) and 10 (flight scripts) call it. Nothing here draws or reads keys.

## Sources and decisions
- Files and load order: `docs/architecture.md` "Files in load order" (`decision 12: Module layout and package split`): `clock`, `hazards`, `prediction` after `controls` and before `shaders` in `luv-in-space.asd`; tests `tests/clock.lisp`, `tests/hazards.lisp`, `tests/prediction.lisp` in the same order after `tests/controls.lisp`. `clock.lisp` calls `hazards.lisp` and `prediction.lisp` at run time (loaded later; ASDF''s compilation unit defers the undefined-function warnings).
- Clock, steps, warp: `docs/architecture.md` "The clock", "Integrator", "Time warp", "Determinism", "Checkpoints"; `decision 16: Integrator and simulation clock`, `decision 19: Time-warp rules` (with `decision 43: Warp drop-outs for contact and life support`: its events are milestones 3–4''s), `decision 31: Warp choices within the time-warp rules`; `docs/design.md` "Time warp".
- Crash and death: `docs/architecture.md` "Collisions and the Sun"; `decision 34: How death works`; `docs/design.md` "Dying and what follows" (milestone 2: Earth R + 100 km, the Moon R; the start state is the only checkpoint, Return and N the same).
- Prediction: `docs/architecture.md` "Trajectory prediction"; `decision 20: Trajectory prediction on the frame thread`. Script waits and cut-offs: "Scripted runs" (`decision 21: Physics test bounds and flight scripts`, `decision 56: Flight-script actions, start states and the quest play-through`).
- The start: `decision 38: Start orbit: polar`, `docs/design.md` "The start"; `decision 66: Calendar dates on the 1/64 s grid` (`(start-t)`).
- From part 1 (`plan 13`): `+fine-step+`, `(start-t)`, crash radii and `body-state` (`bodies.lisp`), `latitude-longitude`, `state->orbit`, `orbit-state`, `orbit-radius-crossings`, `orbit-next-apsis`, `orbit-j2-rates`, display conversions and duration fields (`units.lisp`). From part 2 (`plan 14`): `ship-state`, `copy-ship-state`, `controls`, `copy-controls`, `new-controls`, `flight-step!`, `coarse-step`, `controls-attitude-fn`, `controls-inertial-hold`, `hold-attitude`, the settled flag, the odometer, `ship-engine-force` and `ship-engine-mdot` (`decision 69: Ship state, step rule and kinematic attitude in physics.lisp`, `decision 70: Flight computer: per-step control laws, holds and the RCS mixer`).
- Recorded for this plan: `decision 72: Simulation state and the one step function in clock.lisp`, `decision 73: Warp levels above x4: entry, holds and lowering`, `decision 74: Crash test on the step''s Hermite and the death state`, `decision 75: Prediction in milestone 2: the step function on a copy`.
- Tests: `docs/architecture.md` "Testing" → "Rules for tests", "Drift bounds" (through the clock), "Rails, orbits and frames" (the polar node test), "Warp, events and collisions"; this part''s tests together stay under 1.5 s (part 2 has 3 s of the ~5 s physics budget).

## Rules for every piece
- Simulation files: no HAL, SDL or `*game*`; no wall clock except the frame''s 4 ms cap through `*frame-step-time-limit*` (tests bind it to nil, `decision 72`). The step path (`sim-step!`, the crash test, a prediction step) conses nothing (a test with `sb-ext:get-bytes-consed`, as part 2''s).
- State: the `sim` struct is the simulation state; the `clock` and the `prediction` structs are not part of it and checkpoints don''t copy them. Every double in `sim` compares bit for bit with `equalp`.
- Tunables (3 s, the levels, the limits ×10 / ×100, 0.01 rad/s, 4,096 steps, 4 ms, the horizon) are `defparameter`s citing their decision; the warp levels a list in order, no hash tables.
- Each piece adds its files to `luv-in-space.asd` (both systems), passes `scripts/test`, and keeps `AGENTS.md` "Layout" (the `src/` bullet lists the simulation files built so far) and `docs/architecture.md` "Files in load order" true.
- No luv gap is expected (nothing here touches luv); one met goes to `docs/luv-capabilities.md` and `docs/luv-wishlist.md`.

## Pieces, in the order they run

### 1. `clock.lisp`, part one: the simulation state, the start state, the step, checkpoints
- `sim` struct (`decision 72`): `t`, `body` (`:earth` in milestone 2), `ship` (`ship-state`), `controls`, `model` (`:full`; tests set `:point` or `:j2`), `cutoff` (nil or the odometer value at which the throttle is cut), `loss` (nil or the loss record of piece 2), `steps` (count); `copy-sim` deep (through `copy-ship-state`, `copy-controls`) and `sim-assign!` copying into an existing sim without consing.
- `(new-game-sim)`: `t` = `(start-t)`; r, v from the start orbit (a = 6,778,136.6 m, e 0, i 90°, Ω 270°, u 0 at `(start-t)`) through `orbit-state`; q and ω from `hold-attitude :prograde` with the default roll (ω in body axes = the orbital frame''s rate); m_p 28,000 kg; `(new-controls)` (rate damping, prograde hold, settled, throttle off). Flight scripts'' `(:start :new-game)` and N use it.
- `(sim-step! sim level &key until)`, one step with every rule (`decision 72`):
  - h: `+fine-step+` at ×1 and ×4; above, `(coarse-step r t thrusting)` with `controls-attitude-fn` (kinematic attitude, no RCS). Entering a coarse step with no hold engages an inertial hold first (`controls-inertial-hold`, `decision 73`); the warp-level checks that may refuse or lower warp are piece 5''s.
  - Landing: h shortened so that `t + h` ≤ `until` (a grid time: a script action, a date, an apsis wait) and ≤ the cut-off''s grid time; shortened steps are multiples of 1/64 s, so `t` stays on the grid.
  - Cut-off: with `cutoff` armed and the throttle on, the cut lands on the first 1/64 s grid point where the odometer reaches it: at the fine step after the step that crosses it; at a coarse step h = m₀/ṁ (1 − exp(−Δv_left ṁ / |F|)) (engine only, as above ×4) rounded up to the grid; then throttle 0 and `cutoff` nil.
  - Then `flight-step!` with `(sim-model sim)`; `t += h`, `steps += 1`. A sim with a `loss` takes no step (returns nil).
  - `sim-step!` returns h. Piece 2 adds the crash test after `flight-step!`.
- Checkpoints ("Checkpoints"): `(take-checkpoint sim)` = `copy-sim`; `(restore-checkpoint! sim checkpoint)` = `sim-assign!`; `(checkpoint-safe-p sim)`: throttle off, no loss (piece 3 adds "no impact predicted"); milestone 2 takes one checkpoint, the start state.
- Tests `tests/clock.lisp`:
  - Start state: `state->orbit` of it gives a, e, i, Ω, u within 1e-9 relative (1e-12 for e); body +x along v within 1e-12 rad and −z along radial out within 1e-12 rad; |ω| = n within 1e-12 relative; `t` = `(start-t)`; the hold settled.
  - Grid: 1,000 mixed steps (fine and coarse, with `until` on odd grid times) leave `t` an exact multiple of 1/64 s and land on every `until` exactly.
  - Cut-off: a prograde full-throttle burn with `cutoff` 25 m/s at ×1 and at ×100: each ends on the grid, Δv delivered ≥ 25 m/s and below 25 m/s + one fine step''s Δv; the two cut times differ by at most 1/64 s.
  - Checkpoint: restore then N = 2,000 steps (fine and coarse mixed) equals N steps from the original bit for bit (`equalp` on the sims).
  - The polar node (`docs/architecture.md` "Rails with J2", `decision 38`): the start state with `:j2`, 7 days at the coarse step (×1,000 through `sim-step!`): Ω stays within 1e-6° of 270°; `orbit-j2-rates` gives |Ω̇| < 1e-15 rad/s.
  - Allocation: 10,000 `sim-step!`s cons less than 1 KB.
- Docs: `docs/architecture.md` "Checkpoints" (milestone 2''s `sim` fields, `decision 72`), "The clock" (`sim-step!` as the one step; landing on scheduled times and the cut-off rule), "Files in load order" (`clock.lisp` also holds the start state).
- Done: the tests pass; `./sly eval ''(let ((s (new-game-sim))) (dotimes (i 64) (sim-step! s 1)) (sim-t s))'' --package LUV-IN-SPACE` gives `(start-t)` + 1.

### 2. `hazards.lisp`: the swept crash test and death
- `(crash-test! sim r0 v0 t0)` after each step, called by `sim-step!` with the step''s start state (kept in a preallocated buffer; nothing consed), against Earth (R + 100 km, the central body) and the Moon (R, a third body, its geocentric state from `body-state` at both ends), as `decision 74` sets: the relative cubic Hermite p(s); chord closest approach minus max(|p''''(0)|, |p''''(1)|)/8 against the crash radius; only then Newton on d|p|²/ds, and the first crossing |p(s)| = R_c by bracketed Newton to 1e-9 in s. The earliest crossing over both bodies wins.
- Death (`decision 74`): `loss` struct (cause `:crash`, body, `t`, speed relative to the body, latitude, longitude from `latitude-longitude` at the impact `t`); the sim''s ship at the Hermite state of the crossing (attitude slerped, ω and m_p of the step''s end), `t` the impact time; no step after. `(sim-lost-p sim)`.
- Tests `tests/hazards.lisp` ("Crash detection"):
  - Radial free fall from rest at 1,000 km (`:point`): impact at R + 100 km within 1e-3 s of the closed form t = √(r₀³/(2μ)) (√(x(1 − x)) + arccos √x), x = r_c/r₀, at the fine step (×1) and the coarse step (×1,000).
  - Periapsis 50 km below the crash radius (`:point`, an orbit from 400 km apoapsis), at the coarse step: the impact on the first pass, its time within 1e-2 s of `orbit-radius-crossings`, latitude and longitude within 0.01° of the closed-form point (the Kepler position at that time through `latitude-longitude`); the speed equal to the vis-viva speed at R_c within 1e-3 m/s. (The same through the clock at ×1,000,000 is piece 5''s.)
  - Grazing: periapsis 1 m above the crash radius, two orbits at the fine and the coarse step: no impact.
  - The Moon: a ship set 50,000 km from the Moon (in Earth''s frame, `:full`, with `t` 2040-01-01) at 3 km/s relative, aimed with an impact parameter of 0.9 R_☾, at the coarse step: caught, body `:moon`, |p| at the crossing = R_☾ within 1e-3 m; aimed so that the selenocentric hyperbola''s periapsis (computed in the test) is 1 km above R_☾: no impact.
  - Death stops: after the impact `sim-step!` returns nil and the sim is unchanged; a checkpoint restored brings back a sim that flies to the same impact bit for bit.
- Docs: `docs/architecture.md` "Collisions and the Sun" (the exact sag bound and the death state, `decision 74`); "Testing" → "Crash detection" with the Moon case as written.
- Done: the tests pass; the cost of the crash test per step is measured (a few tens of ns when nothing is near) and written in "Integrator"''s cost line.

### 3. `prediction.lisp`: the milestone-2 events
- `prediction` struct (`decision 75`): its own `sim` copy, a preallocated buffer of 20,000 steps (t, r, v), the horizon (one osculating period; two on request for the map), the events found, a validity state and the controls'' signature it was made with.
- `(prediction-update! pred sim)`: restart from the flown sim when the signature (throttle, mode, hold kind, roll offset, inertial target, keys, fine, cutoff) differs, while the throttle is on (each call), when the flown r is more than 1 m from the buffer''s Hermite at the flown t, when the flown t passes the buffer''s end, after a restore or a death; otherwise extend the tail to the horizon from the current t. At most `*prediction-steps-per-frame*` `sim-step!`s per call at ×1,000; `(prediction-run! pred sim)` runs it to completion (tests, scripts).
- Events: `(prediction-event pred kind)` → time (and for `:impact` the copy''s loss record) or nil; `:apoapsis`, `:periapsis` from r·v sign changes on the stored Hermite, root to 1e-6 s; `:impact` from the copy''s crash; `:cutoff` when the copy cuts the throttle. `(prediction-dropout-event pred)` → the earliest drop-out event (milestone 2: `:impact`) with its kind; `(prediction-complete-p pred)`. `checkpoint-safe-p` adds: no impact predicted.
- A wait on an event ends at the first 1/64 s grid point at or after its predicted time (`decision 75`); `(grid-ceiling t)` here, used by part 10''s waits as `sim-step!`''s `until`.
- Tests `tests/prediction.lisp` ("Prediction"):
  - Coasting at ×1,000 (`:full`): the prediction''s state one orbit ahead equals the flown sim''s at that `t` bit for bit; a prediction computed over frames of 500 steps equals one computed at once bit for bit.
  - Apsides (`:point`, e = 0.1): `:periapsis` and `:apoapsis` within 1e-3 s of `orbit-next-apsis`; the flight at ×1 then meets r·v = 0 within the fine step that contains the predicted time.
  - Impact: the deorbit case of piece 2 (periapsis 50 km below the crash radius): the predicted impact within 1e-2 s of the flight''s at ×1,000 and within 0.05 s at ×1 (fine steps under rate damping differ by millimetres).
  - Cut-off: a prograde burn with `cutoff` 25 m/s: `:cutoff` at the flight''s cut time at ×100 exactly; the apoapsis after it matches the flown one within 1 m.
  - Restart rules: a throttle change, a hold change and a 2 m displacement each restart; coasting 100 frames does not, and only the tail grows.
  - Budget: one call never takes more than `*prediction-steps-per-frame*` steps; a full LEO prediction conses nothing after the first.
- Docs: `docs/architecture.md` "Trajectory prediction" (milestone 2''s form, the step budget, `decision 75`); "Scripted runs" → "Steps": an event wait ends at the first grid point at or after the event.
- Done: the tests pass; `./sly eval` of `(prediction-run! …)` on the start state with a 100 m/s retrograde burn applied lists apoapsis, periapsis and no impact.

### 4. `clock.lisp`, part two: frames, warp levels, pause, the actual rate
- `clock` struct (`decision 72`): level index into ×1, ×4, ×10, ×100, ×1,000, ×10,000, ×100,000, ×1,000,000; accumulator (double); paused; the rate meter (real and game time of the frames in the last second, a fixed ring); the message list (piece 5); the previous ship state and `t` for interpolation (preallocated, filled before each step); `hook` (nil or a lambda called with the clock and the sim before each step, returning a grid `until` or nil: flight scripts); the `prediction`.
- `(clock-frame! clock sim elapsed)`: `prediction-update!` first; `acc += min(elapsed, 0.1) × level`; while `acc ≥` the next h: the hook, `sim-step!` with its `until`, `acc −= h`; at most 4,096 steps and `*frame-step-time-limit*` (checked every 64 steps); the rest dropped (`acc` clamped below one step). Paused: no steps, `acc` 0, the level kept. Lost: no steps.
- Commands for input (part 7) and scripts (part 10): `clock-warp-up`, `clock-warp-down`, `clock-warp-reset` (never refused), `clock-toggle-pause`; a change takes effect at the next step boundary and never touches the sim. `(clock-actual-rate clock)`, `(clock-kinematic-p clock)` (level above ×4: input ignores rotation and translation keys with decision 31''s message), `(clock-previous-state clock)` for part 4''s interpolation.
- Tests `tests/clock.lisp` ("Drift bounds", through the clock; two-body `:point`, the test orbit of "Drift bounds", direct mode with no keys so the mixer''s zero command short-cuts):
  - At every level, fed frames of 1/60 s with the time limit off: the state at each step equals bit for bit a direct `sim-step!` loop at the same level; ×1 and ×4 over 600 s, the six coarse levels over 10 circular orbits; at ×1,000,000 every frame stops at 4,096 steps and the states still match.
  - Frames of 1/144 s and a fixed list of irregular elapsed times (including 0.5 s, clamped to 0.1) give the same states at ×1, ×4 and ×1,000.
  - Warp transitions: a level change at a step boundary leaves the sim bit for bit unchanged; the next step uses the new h; ×1 → ×1,000 → ×1 over one orbit stays within 5 m of a run that never left ×1.
  - Pause: 100 paused frames change nothing in the sim; resuming restores the level.
  - Actual rate: at ×1,000,000 with the cap, the meter reads 4,096 coarse steps'' game time per frame × 60 within 1 %.
  - If the runs exceed their share of the 1.5 s, the worker shortens ×1 and ×4 to 120 s and the coarse levels to 5 orbits, says so in the test''s comment, and keeps the bounds.
- Docs: `docs/architecture.md` "The clock" (`clock-frame!`, the hook, the time-limit parameter); "Drift bounds" points at `tests/clock.lisp` for the through-the-clock rows as built.
- Done: the tests pass; `./sly eval` of 600 `clock-frame!`s of 1/60 s at ×100 from the start state advances `t` by 1,000 s.

### 5. `clock.lisp`, part three: refusals, thrust limits, drop-outs, messages, restart
- Refusals of a warp up (`decision 19`, `decision 31`, `decision 73`): the throttle on and the new level above the limit (×10, or ×100 with `cutoff` armed); above ×4 with |ω| ≥ 0.01 rad/s and no hold; the prediction''s drop-out event less than 3 s × the new level away. Each refusal leaves the level and writes its message.
- Checks before every step (`decision 73`): throttle on above the limit lowers to the limit; above ×4 with no hold lowers to ×4; with Δ = t_e − t to the drop-out event, the level becomes the highest L ≤ current with Δ ≥ 3 s × L (so ×1 below 12 s and at the event); the accumulator scales by new/old; each lowering writes its message once.
- Messages: text and a real-time stamp, shown 3 s (`(clock-messages clock)` gives the live ones; real time from the summed `elapsed`, not a wall clock), worded as `decision 73`; durations through `units.lisp`''s fields.
- Restart (`decision 34`): `(clock-restart! clock sim checkpoint)`: `restore-checkpoint!`, level ×1, unpaused, accumulator 0, messages cleared, prediction restarted; the LOST panel''s Return and N (part 9) call it with the start state.
- Tests `tests/clock.lisp` ("Warp refusals and drop-outs"):
  - Refusals: throttle on at ×10 → ×100 refused with "ENGINE ON, LIMIT ×10", allowed with `cutoff` armed and refused → ×1,000; rate damping at |ω| = 0.02 rad/s → ×10 refused ("ROTATING, SET A HOLD"), at 0.005 rad/s allowed and an inertial hold engaged; an impact 20 s ahead: ×4 → ×10 refused (30 s > 20 s).
  - Lowering: throttle up at ×100 without a cut-off lowers to ×10 at the next step; releasing the hold at ×1,000 lowers to ×4.
  - Drop-out to impact (`:point`, periapsis 50 km below the crash radius, starting 1 h before it at ×1,000,000 through `clock-frame!`): at every step Δ ≥ 3 s × level, the level is ×1 at the impact, the impact time within 1e-2 s of the closed form and the point within 0.01° (the clock case of "Crash detection").
  - Messages: each refusal and lowering writes one message, gone after 3 s of summed `elapsed`.
  - Restart: a death, then `clock-restart!` with the start state, gives a sim `equalp` to `(new-game-sim)` at ×1, and 1,000 frames after it equal 1,000 frames after a fresh start bit for bit.
- Docs: `docs/architecture.md` "Time warp" (entry with an inertial hold, the snap, release lowering to ×4, the per-step checks, `decision 73`); `docs/design.md` "Time warp" (the hold rules and the message texts of `decision 73`); "Testing" → "Warp, events and collisions" as built (milestone 2''s cases; the rest stays for milestones 3–4).
- Done: the tests pass; the part''s tests together run under 1.5 s in `scripts/test` (measured, in the done note).

## What later parts take from here
- Part 4 (`main.lisp`, `render.lisp`): `*game*` holds a `clock` and a `sim` from `(new-game-sim)`; the frame calls `clock-frame!` with the canvas''s elapsed time and renders from `clock-previous-state` and the sim, interpolated; `capture.lisp` renders a given sim.
- Part 7 (`input.lisp`): `clock-warp-up` / `-down` / `-reset`, `clock-toggle-pause`, `clock-kinematic-p`; flight inputs ignored while paused.
- Part 8: the map''s impact marker from `prediction-event :impact` (its ribbon stays the osculating orbit in milestone 2).
- Part 9 (`overlay.lisp`): level, `clock-actual-rate`, paused, `clock-messages`, the UTC date from `sim-t`, the alarm (periapsis below the crash radius, impact time from the prediction), the death view and LOST panel from `sim-loss`, `clock-restart!` for Return and N.
- Part 10 (`flights.lisp`): `new-game-sim`, the clock''s `hook` (actions at grid times, `cutoff` for `:after-dv`), `sim-step!` with `until` for headless runs, `prediction-event` and `grid-ceiling` for waits, the refusal messages as a refused action''s reason, `sim-loss` for `(:expect :lost :crash)`.
- Milestone 3: checkpoints after a plan and on berthing (`checkpoint-safe-p`), the drop-out events of burns and contacts, prediction trajectories for ribbons from the same buffer; milestone 4: SOI, Sun zone and life-support events, re-centring in `sim-step!`.', '2026-10-14T22:16:32Z', 74);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (16, 2, 'Rendering foundation and assets', 500, 'Part 4 of `plan 2: masterplan`: the renderer the later parts draw into, and the frame that runs the simulation. `gpu.lisp` holds the HAL helpers, `camera.lisp` the camera and the one projection function, `assets.lisp` finding and decoding assets (with the first asset, the Blue Marble, and its packaging), `render.lisp` the render state interpolated from the clock and the passes in order, `post.lisp` the HDR target, bloom, tone map and exposure, and `overlay.lisp` the dot-matrix pass moved out of the skeleton. `main.lisp`''s frame runs part 3''s clock; `capture.lisp` renders a given sim. The walking skeleton goes: `scene.lisp`, `tests/scene.lisp` and `PLACEHOLDER(23)`. It builds on `plan 13: Simulation foundations: math, units, bodies, orbits` and `plan 15: Clock, warp, hazards and events`; parts 5–9 (Earth, Moon, Sun and stars; the Mule''s meshes; input and the outside view; the map; the readout) draw through it.

## Sources and decisions
- Files and load order: `docs/architecture.md` "Files in load order" (`decision 12: Module layout and package split`): `assets`, `gpu`, `camera` after `shaders`; `post`, `overlay`, `render` after them in that order (stars, impostors, meshes, ribbons of parts 5, 6, 8 go between `camera` and `post`); `main`, `capture` last. Tests `tests/camera.lisp`, `tests/assets.lisp`, `tests/post.lisp`, `tests/render.lisp` in the same order, `tests/capture.lisp` last.
- Rendering: `docs/architecture.md` "Rendering precision", "Floating-origin pitfalls", "GPU tests", "Dev loop"; `decision 4: Depth strategy`, `decision 5: Precision of positions`, `decision 11: Render pipelines are luv live shader pipelines`, `decision 2: Screenshots and GPU test: offscreen capture on a hidden canvas, headless on lavapipe`, `decision 7: Placeholder labels: dot-matrix overlay quads`, `decision 61: Performance target GPU: the default Vulkan device, the Intel iGPU`.
- luv''s limits: `docs/luv-capabilities.md` "Rendering" (views outlive the submit; `set-bind-group` after each `set-pipeline`; triangle lists only; `write-texture` takes a rank-2 `(unsigned-byte 32)` array; `canvas-frame-resource-cache` for per-frame buffers), "Sun glare and brightness", "Spheres and planet surfaces", "Packaging", "Other findings" (no `atan`/`asin` in shaders).
- Assets: `docs/architecture.md` "Assets", `decision 13: Asset storage and attribution`, `decision 40: Earth''s look and drag` (January, `world.200401.3x5400x2700.jpg`, sources in `docs/design.md` "The places" → "Sources").
- Views: `decision 30: Views and cameras` (outside view 50° vertical, default 40 m; orbital frame); `docs/design.md` "Views".
- The clock and interpolation: `docs/architecture.md` "The clock"; `decision 72: Simulation state and the one step function in clock.lisp` (`clock-frame!`, `clock-previous-state`, the paused clock), `decision 34: How death works`.
- From part 1 (`plan 13`): `relative-single`, `quat-slerp`, `quat-rotate`, `mat3-from-quat`, `quat-from-basis` (`decision 65: Vectors and quaternions in math.lisp`), `body-state` (Sun, Moon at the render time), `body-fixed-matrix`, R⊕. From part 3 (`plan 15`): `sim`, `new-game-sim`, `clock`, `clock-frame!`, `clock-previous-state`, `clock-toggle-pause`, `sim-loss`.
- Recorded for this plan: `decision 76: Frame passes, radiance scale and exposure`, `decision 77: The frame in main.lisp: render state, capture of a given sim, part 4''s placeholders and first asset`.
- Tests: `docs/architecture.md` "Testing" → "Rules for tests", "Rails, orbits and frames" (the projection test), "GPU tests"; `scripts/test` stays under ~15 s.

## Rules for every piece
- Rendering files read the simulation state and never change it; the simulation files stay free of the HAL (`plan 2`''s rules). Pure parts (projection, interpolation, exposure, meshes, asset paths) are functions without a GPU, tested without a canvas.
- Precision (`decision 5`): every position reaches the GPU as `relative-single` of a double difference to the eye; no single-float cache of anything that moves; the clip rows carry directions and the focal scale only.
- Live redefinition ("Dev loop"): the renderer''s resources hang off `*game*`; every pipeline a live shader pipeline returned by `luv:application-live-artifacts`; tunables are `defparameter`s; render targets are size-cached and remade by the frame (the old ones destroyed through luv''s deferred `destroy`, not leaked as the skeleton''s `depth-view` does).
- Per-frame mutable buffers (uniforms, instance data) through luv''s `canvas-frame-resource-cache` or one buffer per frame in flight, never one buffer rewritten while the previous frame may read it.
- Placeholders (`decision 77`): what this part draws for later parts carries `PLACEHOLDER(76)` (Earth, stars: part 5), `PLACEHOLDER(77)` (the ship: part 6), `PLACEHOLDER(80)` (the readout: part 9) in code and in the on-screen label; those parts remove them.
- Each piece adds its files to `luv-in-space.asd` (both systems), passes `scripts/test` and `scripts/screenshot`, and keeps `AGENTS.md` "Layout" and `docs/architecture.md` "Files in load order" true. A luv gap met goes to `docs/luv-capabilities.md` and `docs/luv-wishlist.md`.

## Pieces, in the order they run

### 1. `gpu.lisp` and `camera.lisp`: HAL helpers, the camera, the one projection function
- `gpu.lisp`: from `scene.lisp`, generalised: `vertex-buffer`, `uniform-buffer`, `live-pipeline` (role, layout, vertex buffers, target format, depth state, blend, topology), size-cached render targets keyed by (extent, format) with their views (`depth-view` and `color-view` for swapchain and capture textures), releasing the old ones on a size change; `texture-from-pixels` (a rank-2 `(unsigned-byte 32)` array to an `:rgba8-unorm-srgb` texture, view and a linear sampler with repeat in u and clamp in v); a `renderer` class (device, canvas format, the pipelines, targets, buffers) with `renderer-live-artifacts` and `destroy-renderer`. `scene.lisp` switches to these helpers, so the picture is unchanged.
- `camera.lisp`: `camera` struct: anchor (`:ship` now; a body for part 8''s map), offset from the anchor in double (ICRF), orientation quat (FRD: x view, y right, z down, `docs/architecture.md` "Frames"), vertical field of view, near 0.1 m (`decision 4`).
- `(camera-clip-rows! out camera aspect)`: the one projection function ("Rendering precision"): clip x = (f / aspect)(p · right), clip y = s f (p · down), clip z = near, clip w = p · forward, f = 1 / tan(fov/2), s = +1 on Vulkan as the only place the sign lives; 16 singles into `out`.
- `(camera-eye camera anchor-position)` (double) and `(eye-relative-single p eye out index)` over `relative-single`; `(sphere-offset-term c r)` = h (2r + h), h = |c| − r in double, for impostors and the placeholder Earth ("Floating-origin pitfalls").
- `(outside-camera-default! camera r v)`: the outside view''s default pose (`decision 30`): in the orbital frame, 40 m from the ship, behind it along −prograde and raised 20° towards radial out, looking at the ship; vertical FOV 50°. Defparameters `*outside-distance*` 40, `*outside-elevation*` 20°; part 7 makes them camera state and adds drag, wheel, C and Home.
- Tests `tests/camera.lisp` ("Rails, orbits and frames" → Projection): a point straight ahead at d = 1, 10, 10^7 m gets depth (clip z / clip w) 0.1/d within 1e-6 relative; a point to the right has positive clip x, a point below (FRD +z) positive clip y; aspect 1.6 scales x only; `sphere-offset-term` agrees with |c|² − r² in rationals within 1e-12 relative for c at 400 km and 1 km altitude, while the single-float difference does not; the default outside pose looks at the ship within 1e-12 rad and puts radial-out above the view axis.
- Docs: `docs/architecture.md` "Rendering precision": the projection lives in `camera.lisp` (drop the sentence on the skeleton''s `uniform-data`); "Files in load order" as built.
- Done: the tests pass; `scripts/screenshot` gives the skeleton''s picture unchanged; `./sly eval ''(camera-clip-rows! …)''` on a default camera prints 16 finite singles.

### 2. `render.lisp`, `overlay.lisp`, `main.lisp`, `capture.lisp`: the frame runs the clock; the skeleton goes
- `render.lisp`: `render-state` struct, preallocated: t_r, r, v, q, ω, m_p, controls, loss, the Sun''s and the Moon''s positions relative to Earth at t_r, Earth''s `body-fixed-matrix` at t_r. `(render-state-update! out clock sim)` (`decision 77`): s = clamp(acc / (t − t_prev), 0, 1), cubic Hermite position and velocity in double from the previous and current r, v over t − t_prev, `quat-slerp` attitude, ω by linear blend; the sim''s state as it is when there is no previous state, t_prev ≥ t, or the sim is lost. Conses nothing.
