- Done: the tests pass; `nix build` succeeds and `result/share/luv-in-space/assets/earth/blue-marble-5400.jpg` exists; `nix run` opens the window with the textured Earth (the agent closes it by PID) and starts from outside the repo (`cd /tmp && nix run <repo>`), proving the variable is used; `nix flake check` passes.

### 4. `post.lisp`: HDR target, bloom, tone map, exposure
- As `decision 76`: the scene pass draws into a size-cached `:rgba16-float` target with the depth target; post runs the bloom mip chain (1/2 to 1/16, 13-tap downsample, 9-tap tent upsample, added level by level, `*bloom-strength*` 0.04) and one tone-map pass into the canvas or capture target (exposure, ACES filmic, sRGB encoding when the target format is not `-srgb`); then the overlay pass on the target without depth. Every pass a live pipeline with its own role (`decision 11`); bind groups set after each pipeline.
- Radiance scale (`decision 76`): the placeholder Earth''s lit white = 1.0; the placeholder stars at radiances visible at the deep exposure and gone at the sunlit one.
- Exposure (CPU, pure): `(exposure-target camera render-state view)`: the Sun''s direction within the half-diagonal plus 5°, or a body''s coverage × lit fraction ≥ 0.05 with coverage ≥ 5 % → `*exposure-sunlit*` 1.0; else `*exposure-deep*` 16.0; `:map` → `*exposure-map*`. `(exposure-ease current target elapsed)`: log2 exponential, τ 1.0 s of real time, also while paused; the first frame and captures snap.
- Tests `tests/post.lisp` (CPU): the start state with the default camera → sunlit; the same camera when the ship is in Earth''s shadow (at u = 180° of the start orbit) and looking away from Earth → deep; looking at the Sun → sunlit; Earth at phase 170° filling the view → deep; easing: after τ of elapsed time the log2 exposure has gone 1 − 1/e of the way within 1e-12; snapping; `:map` uses `*exposure-map*`.
- Tests `tests/render.lisp` (GPU, added to the same `with-capture-game`): a test-only quad of radiance 4×10^4 (4 px, through the stars'' pipeline in the HDR target) at the centre of a dark view lifts the pixels 20 px away above the background by more than 10 of 255, and not with `*bloom-strength*` 0; the tone map clamps its input to [0, 6×10^4] so a non-finite or negative value never reaches the target; the start frame''s mean luminance stays within a range set from the frame (lit Earth, not white-out: below 200 of 255); the overlay label''s pixels are exactly its colour (not bloomed, not tone-mapped); `report-failed-live-artifacts` 0. The whole `scripts/test` stays under ~15 s (measured, in the done note).
- Docs: `docs/architecture.md` "Rendering precision" → "Draw order" becomes the three stages of `decision 76` (scene pass into HDR, post, overlay on the target), the radiance scale and exposure rule in a short bullet list; `docs/luv-capabilities.md` "Sun glare and brightness" (what was built, the bloom chain on lavapipe, ran); `docs/luv-wishlist.md` the post-processing item''s workaround names `post.lisp` and its size in lines.
- Done: the tests pass; `scripts/screenshot` shows the textured Earth tone-mapped (no white-out, label crisp); with `./sly play`, `./sly eval ''(setf *bloom-strength* 0.3)'' --package LUV-IN-SPACE` visibly widens the glow on the next frame and a re-evaluated tone-map shader shows live; the frame rate on the default GPU at 1280×800 is noted in the done note (not a gate; `milestone 6` measures).

## What later parts take from here
- Part 5 (Earth, Moon, Sun and stars): `render-state` (Sun, Moon, Earth''s matrix at t_r), `sphere-offset-term`, `camera-clip-rows!`, the Earth texture (uploaded by the renderer), the shader `atan2`/`asin`, the radiance scale and exposure parameters of `decision 76`; it replaces the placeholder Earth and stars and removes `PLACEHOLDER(76)`.
- Part 6 (meshes): the scene pass after the impostors, `eye-relative-single`, `mat3-from-quat` of the interpolated attitude; removes `PLACEHOLDER(77)`.
- Part 7 (input and the outside view): the camera struct and `outside-camera-default!` to grow into the orbit camera; `handle-event` moves to `input.lisp`.
- Part 8 (map): a camera anchored on a body, the `:map` exposure, the ribbon pass between meshes and post.
- Part 9 (readout): `overlay.lisp` and its pass on the target after tone mapping; removes `PLACEHOLDER(80)`.
- Part 10 (flight scripts): `capture-state` for each `:screenshot`, `camera-fn` for a scripted view.', '2026-10-14T22:23:45Z', 75);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (17, 2, 'Earth, Moon, Sun and stars', 480, 'Part 5 of `plan 2: masterplan`: the sky and the bodies in it. `scripts/convert-stars` makes the HYG star table, `assets.lisp` loads it, `stars.lisp` draws it at infinity; `impostors.lisp` draws the Sun (limb darkening, glare through `post.lisp`), the Moon (grey Lambert) and Earth (the Blue Marble, the atmosphere rim and the R⊕ + 100 km halo) as ray-traced sphere impostors; `bodies.lisp` gains the Sun''s visible fraction at a point, which shades the night side and refines the exposure. Part 5 replaces part 4''s placeholder Earth and stars and removes `PLACEHOLDER(76)`. It builds on `plan 16: Rendering foundation and assets` (passes, `post.lisp`, camera, render state, the Earth texture) and `plan 13: Simulation foundations: math, units, bodies, orbits` (`body-state`, `body-fixed-matrix`, radii). It runs before the views and the map (parts 7 and 8), which draw these bodies.

## Sources and decisions
- Specification: `docs/design.md` "Scope" → "Feature list": "Earth: Blue Marble impostor (January), atmosphere rim and halo", "Stars (HYG ≤ 6.5 mag), the Sun''s disc and glare, bloom and exposure", "The Moon as a body in the sky, untextured"; the sections "Earth", "The Sun", "The Moon", "The start" (sunlit, Earth''s shadow 22 min after the start).
- `decision 13: Asset storage and attribution` (HYG ≤ 6.5 as `assets/stars/hyg-6.5.sexp` by `scripts/convert-stars`, CC BY-SA 4.0, `assets/stars/LICENSE`, `SOURCE.md`, `CREDITS.md`), `decision 40: Earth''s look and drag` (January, rim, halo at R + 100 km), `decision 34: How death works` (the crash sphere the halo shows), `decision 18: Ephemerides`, `decision 15: Coordinate frames` (ICRF axes, the texture lookup).
- Rendering: `decision 4: Depth strategy`, `decision 5: Precision of positions`, `decision 11: Render pipelines are luv live shader pipelines`, `decision 76: Frame passes, radiance scale and exposure`, `decision 77: The frame in main.lisp: render state, capture of a given sim, part 4''s placeholders and first asset`; `docs/architecture.md` "Files in load order", "Assets", "Frames", "Rendering precision" (the impostor''s stable root, `h (2r + h)`), "Floating-origin pitfalls", "GPU tests", "Dev loop".
- luv''s limits: `docs/luv-capabilities.md` "Starfield", "Spheres and planet surfaces", "Sun glare and brightness", "Other findings" (no `atan`/`asin`; `let*` only; no scalar–vector `-`). Vulkan textures in luv carry one mip level (`../luv/hal/vulkan/gpu.lisp` writes mip 0 only); `shader:sample-level` exists.
- Recorded for this plan: `decision 78: Sky in numbers: stars, Sun, Moon and Earth''s look` (every number below without another source), `decision 79: Sunlight at a point and the sunlit ship in the exposure rule` (refines `decision 76`''s exposure target).
- From `plan 16`: `render-state` (Sun and Moon relative to Earth at t_r, Earth''s `body-fixed-matrix`), `camera-clip-rows!`, `camera-eye`, `eye-relative-single`, `sphere-offset-term`, `live-pipeline`, `texture-from-pixels`, `load-image-pixels`, `assets-root`/`asset-path`, the Earth texture uploaded by the renderer, the shader `atan2`/`asin`, `exposure-target`, `with-capture-game`, `capture-state` with `camera-fn`, the HDR scene pass and `encode-frame`.

## Rules for every piece
- Load order (`decision 12: Module layout and package split`): `bodies.lisp` (extended), `assets.lisp` (extended), `stars.lisp` and `impostors.lisp` after `camera.lisp` and before `meshes`/`post`; tests `tests/bodies.lisp`, `tests/assets.lisp`, `tests/stars.lisp`, `tests/impostors.lisp` in that order; GPU checks only in `tests/render.lisp` (one `with-capture-game`, 320×200, shared with part 4''s checks).
- Precision (`decision 5`): every centre reaches the GPU as `eye-relative-single` of a double difference (Earth: −eye; Moon: Moon − eye; Sun: Sun − eye, all from Earth-relative doubles); `c·c − r²` as `sphere-offset-term` in double for every sphere; star directions are constants (single is fine, they never move).
- Live code ("Dev loop"): shaders in `shaders.lisp` (`define-frame-shader`), one live pipeline per role (`:stars`, `:sun`, `:moon`, `:earth`, `:earth-halo`) returned by the renderer''s live artifacts; per-frame uniforms through `canvas-frame-resource-cache` or one buffer per frame in flight; the static star buffer and the textures made once by the renderer and destroyed by `destroy-renderer`; tunables `defparameter` (`decision 78`).
- Each piece adds its files to `luv-in-space.asd` (both systems), passes `scripts/test` (under ~15 s; its GPU checks measured in the done note) and `scripts/screenshot`, and keeps `AGENTS.md` "Layout"/"Commands" and `docs/architecture.md` "Files in load order" true; a luv gap goes to `docs/luv-capabilities.md` and `docs/luv-wishlist.md`.

## Pieces, in the order they run

### 1. The star table: `scripts/convert-stars`, `assets/stars/`, the loader (needs web access: `--web`)
- `scripts/convert-stars [out]` (enters the dev shell like `scripts/test`): downloads the current HYG release CSV from `codeberg.org/astronexus/hyg` (the `hyg_v4x.csv` named in its README) into a new `mktemp -d` directory, checks its SHA-256 against the one written in the script, and runs `scripts/convert-stars.lisp` (`sbcl --script`, the path passed as an argument, a small quote-aware CSV reader) to keep rows with `mag` ≤ 6.5 except the Sun (`id` 0, "Sol"), sorted by magnitude then `id`, and write `assets/stars/hyg-6.5.sexp`: a header comment (source, version, date, licence CC BY-SA 4.0, credit "HYG database by David Nash (astronexus)", the command), then one list per star `(hip ra-deg dec-deg mag ci)` with RA in degrees (HYG''s hours × 15) and six decimals, `ci` the B−V or `nil`; the temporary directory is deleted.
- `assets/stars/SOURCE.md` (URL, release, date, SHA-256 of the CSV, licence, credit, derivation command), `assets/stars/LICENSE` (CC BY-SA 4.0 legal code as published), the HYG credit in `CREDITS.md`. Without web access the worker stops and records it, as `decision 45: Web access for pm workers still denied after decision 28` did.
- `assets.lisp`: `(load-star-table &optional (path (asset-path "stars/hyg-6.5.sexp")))` reads with `*read-eval*` nil and the standard readtable, checks the format (a list of five-element lists), returns a struct of specialised arrays (direction as single x, y, z from RA/Dec as ICRF: (cos δ cos α, cos δ sin α, sin δ); magnitude; colour index with nil as NaN-free white flag).
- Tests `tests/assets.lisp`: the table loads with 8,000–10,000 stars, none brighter than −1.5 but Sirius (−1.44 within 0.02, its direction RA 101.287°, Dec −16.716° within 0.01°), no Sun; Polaris (HIP 11767) within 0.75° of +z; every magnitude ≤ 6.5 and directions unit within 1e-6; loading takes under 0.5 s (measured, in the done note); the file is under 1 MB (`decision 13`).
- Docs: `docs/architecture.md` "Assets" (the table as built, its columns and size); `AGENTS.md` "Commands" (`scripts/convert-stars`: needs network, rewrites the committed table) and "Layout" (`assets/stars/`).
- Done: `scripts/convert-stars /tmp/x.sexp` run twice gives identical files, equal to the committed one; the tests pass; `nix build` succeeds and `result/share/luv-in-space/assets/stars/hyg-6.5.sexp` exists.

### 2. `stars.lisp`: the stars at infinity (`--web`: the colour-matching constants)
- Per `decision 78`: one static instance buffer (direction, linear RGB × peak radiance, σ in pixels) built from the table once at renderer start; peak L(m) = `*star-radiance-limit*` 1.25e-3 × 10^(0.2 (6.5 − m)); σ = `*star-sigma*` 0.7 px + 0.15 px per magnitude brighter than 3.0, at most 1.6 px; colour from B−V by Ballesteros (2012) T = 4600 (1/(0.92 BV + 1.7) + 1/(0.92 BV + 0.62)), then Planck''s law against the CIE 1931 2° functions (Wyman, Sloan, Shirley 2013, JCGT 2(2), multi-lobe fit, constants checked against the paper), XYZ → linear sRGB (IEC 61966-2-1 matrix), normalised to luminance 1, mixed toward white by `*star-saturation*` 0.6; precomputed in a 64-entry table over B−V −0.4 … 2.0 at load, the sources in comments.
- Pipeline `:stars` (vertex: direction through the clip rows with clip z 0, the quad expanded by σ × 4 in pixels from the viewport size; fragment: Gaussian × colour; premultiplied alpha 0, additive, compare `:always`, no depth write), drawn first in the scene pass; part 4''s placeholder stars and their `PLACEHOLDER(76)` go.
- `post.lisp`: a CPU replica of the tone map, `(tone-map-srgb x exposure)` → 0–255 (ACES Narkowicz fit, sRGB encoding, the shader''s clamp), if part 4 did not add one; the shader and it are kept in step by a test.
- Tests `tests/stars.lisp` (CPU): the B−V colours: 0.65 (the Sun) within 0.03 of white in chromaticity, −0.3 bluer than 1.5 (b/r), luminance 1 within 1e-6; through `tone-map-srgb`: a mag 6.5 star''s peak at `*exposure-deep*` ≥ 20, every star of mag ≥ 1.0 at `*exposure-sunlit*` < 8, Sirius at `*exposure-sunlit*` < 80; the instance count equals the table''s.
- Tests `tests/render.lisp` (GPU, the shared capture, the ship in Earth''s shadow at u = 180° of the start orbit so the exposure is deep): a camera at Sirius (RA 101.287°, Dec −16.716°) with celestial north up and 20° vertical FOV has its brightest pixel within 1 px of the centre; Procyon''s brightest pixel lies up and to the left (east is left on the sky) at the pixel `camera-clip-rows!` predicts within 2 px; a view of Orion (RA 84°, Dec −1°, 50°) has between 40 and 400 pixels above 30 of 255 (bounds set from the first measured frame ×0.5 and ×2, written in the test''s comment); the shader''s tone map agrees with `tone-map-srgb` within 2 of 255 on a test quad of known radiance; `report-failed-live-artifacts` 0.
- Docs: `docs/luv-capabilities.md` "Starfield" (built: the count, the pass, ran on lavapipe); `docs/architecture.md` "Rendering precision" → draw order (stars first, as built).
- Done: the tests pass; `scripts/screenshot` shows no placeholder stars (the start is sunlit: few or none visible); `./sly play` and a camera turned away at night show the Milky Way''s bright stars, Orion recognisable (a screenshot in the done note''s path); `(setf *star-radiance-limit* …)` live changes them on the next frame.

### 3. `impostors.lisp`, part one: impostor machinery, the Sun, the Moon, sunlight at a point
- `bodies.lisp` (`decision 79`): `(sun-visible-fraction p t &optional (origin :earth))`: Sun, Earth and Moon from `body-state` at `t`; for Earth and the Moon as occulting discs, the exact overlap area of two circles in angle (angular radii asin(R/d), separation from the cross and dot products for precision at small angles) over the Sun''s disc; the smaller visible fraction kept; double, conses nothing; a pure circle-overlap helper.
- `render.lisp`: `render-state` gains `sun-fraction` (the ship''s at t_r); `post.lisp`''s `exposure-target` gains the ship term of `decision 79` (`*ship-bounding-radius*` 10.0 m, threshold 0.005, outside view only).
- `impostors.lisp`: `(impostor-params! out center-rel radius offset-term camera aspect)`: the camera-relative centre (single), radius, `c·c − r²` from `sphere-offset-term`, and the draw form: a camera-facing quad over the tangent cone, or a full-screen triangle when the angular radius exceeds 45° or the eye is inside (`decision 78`). Shader functions in `shaders.lisp`: the view ray from the fragment''s NDC and the camera basis (right, down, forward, f/aspect, f), `ray-sphere-near` with the stable root t = k / (b + √(b² − k)) (`docs/architecture.md` "Rendering precision"), `ray-sphere-far`, and the reversed-Z depth near/(t d·forward) written through `:frag-depth`; misses discarded.
- The Sun (`:sun`): radius R☉ at its true distance, centre radiance 4.6e4 at every distance (radiance does not fall with distance; the disc''s size does), limb darkening I/I₀ = 1 − 0.6 (1 − μ), μ = cos of the angle between the normal and −ray, colour (1.0, 0.97, 0.94) at luminance 1; glare only from bloom (`decision 78`). The Moon (`:moon`): radius R☾, grey Lambert, albedo 0.12 × max(n·s, 0) × (1 AU / d_sun)², opaque, depth written. Order after the stars: Sun, Moon.
- Tests `tests/bodies.lisp`: `sun-visible-fraction` is 1 at the start state (within 1e-12) and 0 at u = 180°; along the start orbit at 1 s samples it falls monotonically from 1 to 0 across the shadow entry, the 0.5 point within 0.1° of the geometric shadow edge 180° − asin(R⊕/a) from the Sun (`docs/design.md` "The start"), the penumbra 5–15 s long; a point on the Sun–Moon line 380,000 km beyond the Moon sees less than 1; the circle overlap gives 0 for disjoint discs, the area ratio for a contained one, and agrees with a fixed-seed Monte Carlo of 10^6 points within 2e-3 on three crafted cases; 10,000 calls cons nothing.
- Tests `tests/impostors.lisp` (CPU): the quad covers the projected disc (the four tangent directions inside the quad) for the Moon from the start and the Sun; the full-screen form for Earth from 400 km and for an eye inside the halo; the depth formula gives 0.1/d at the disc''s centre within 1e-6 relative.
- Tests `tests/post.lisp`: the ship term: the start state with the camera turned away from Earth and Sun → sunlit at 40 m, deep at 1 km; in Earth''s shadow → deep at 40 m.
- Tests `tests/render.lisp` (GPU): with `*bloom-strength*` 0 and `*exposure-sunlit*` bound to 2e-5, a 2° camera at the Sun shows a disc whose lit width on the centre row is 2 asin(R☉/d) in pixels within 2 px and whose centre is brighter than its point at 0.8 R by more than 10 of 255 (limb darkening); with the defaults and a 50° camera, pixels 20 px from the Sun''s centre are above the background by more than 30 (glare); with Earth between the ship and the Sun (u = 180°), the Sun''s direction shows no pixel above 250 (occluded by depth); a 2° camera at the Moon at the start date shows a grey disc (|r − g|, |g − b| ≤ 4) whose lit pixel count over the disc''s is (1 + cos phase)/2 within 0.1, the phase from `body-state`.
- Docs: `docs/architecture.md` "Rendering precision" (the impostor as built, the draw form threshold), "Files in load order" (`impostors.lisp`; `bodies.lisp` gains sunlight); `docs/design.md` "The Sun" (the colour and glare as built) and "Views" (the exposure''s ship term, `decision 79`); `docs/luv-capabilities.md` "Sun glare and brightness" (the glare as seen on lavapipe).
- Done: the tests pass; with `./sly play`, a camera turned to the Sun shows a round disc with glare and the stars faded; `./sly eval ''(sun-visible-fraction …)''` on the live ship goes to 0 about 22 min after the start (or at ×100 within a minute).

### 4. `impostors.lisp`, part two: Earth, its atmosphere and halo; `PLACEHOLDER(76)` goes
- Earth (`:earth`): radius R⊕ (equatorial, "Units"), centre −eye; the fragment''s hit normal turned into body-fixed axes by the single copy of `body-fixed-matrix` at t_r, texture lookup u = 0.5 + atan2(y, x)/(2π), v = 0.5 − asin(z)/π ("Frames") with `sample-level` 0 from the Blue Marble and from its 1/8 box-filtered copy (675 × 338, made on the CPU at load by `(downsample-pixels pixels 8)` in `assets.lisp`, uploaded by the renderer), mixed by the texel footprint in pixels (`decision 78`); radiance = texture (linear) × max(n·s, 0) × (1 AU / d_sun)²; the rim term on the lit limb, `*rim-strength*`.
- Halo (`:earth-halo`): radius R⊕ + 100 km (the crash sphere, `decision 40`, `decision 34`), additive, depth-tested against Earth without depth write, drawn after Earth; the chord through the shell (to the surface when the ray hits Earth, far root when the eye is inside) over the tangent chord, to `*halo-power*` 1.5, × `*halo-radiance*` 0.35; blue on the day side, toward orange within 6° of the terminator at the ray''s closest point, zero 6° below (`decision 78`). `offset-term`s for both spheres from `sphere-offset-term` in double.
- Part 4''s placeholder Earth (UV sphere, `uv-sphere`) goes with its tests; `PLACEHOLDER(76)` leaves the code and the on-screen label (`PLACEHOLDER(77 80)` stays until parts 6 and 9); `AGENTS.md` and `docs/` mention no `PLACEHOLDER(76)`.
- Tests `tests/assets.lisp`: the 1/8 copy is 338 rows × 675 columns, its mean per channel equals the full image''s within 1 of 255.
- Tests `tests/render.lisp` (GPU, the shared capture): the nadir check of part 4 (open Pacific, blue above red) now through the impostor; a horizon camera at the start (horizontal towards the Sun''s side, down by the 19.8° dip): a column through the limb rises from space to the halo to the surface, the 2–6 px above the limb bluish (b > r) and above the background by more than 20; the night side (u = 180°, nadir) below 8 of 255 at the centre; Earth from 40,000 km on the Sun''s side (camera-fn): the lit disc''s horizontal and vertical diameters equal 2 asin(R⊕/d) in pixels within 2 px each (round); `report-failed-live-artifacts` 0; the whole `scripts/test` under ~15 s (measured, in the done note).
- Docs: `docs/design.md` "Earth" (rim, halo, the 1/8 copy as built); `docs/architecture.md` "Files in load order" (`impostors.lisp` milestone 2 as built, part 4''s placeholders gone), "GPU tests" (the sky checks); `docs/luv-capabilities.md` "Spheres and planet surfaces" (the impostor as built, one mip level on Vulkan and the workaround); `docs/luv-wishlist.md`: mip levels and their generation on Vulkan, a ray-sphere impostor helper naming `impostors.lisp` and its size in lines; `AGENTS.md` "Layout" (`src/`: no placeholder Earth or stars).
- Done: the tests pass; `scripts/screenshot` shows a round Blue Marble Earth with a blue halo along the limb, no placeholder label for 76; `./sly play` at ×100 through a night pass: the terminator''s orange band, a dark Earth, the stars coming up as the exposure eases; a re-evaluated `earth-fragment` shows on the next frame.

## What later parts take from here
- Part 6 (meshes): `sun-fraction` and the Sun''s direction at t_r to light the ship (dark in Earth''s shadow); `*ship-bounding-radius*` replaced by the stack''s computed radius.
- Part 7 (outside view): the exposure''s ship term uses the orbit camera''s distance.
- Part 8 (map): the impostors at true scale from far cameras, the 1/8 copy against shimmer, `*exposure-map*`; markers below 6 px are the map''s.
- Milestone 3: night lights and clouds extend `earth-fragment`; milestone 4: the Moon''s texture replaces the grey Lambert, planets as sprites, the Sun''s heat uses `sun-visible-fraction`.', '2026-10-14T22:29:18Z', 76);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (18, 2, 'The Mule''s meshes', 470, 'Part 6 of `plan 2: masterplan`: the player''s ship seen from outside. `meshes.lisp` generates the Mule from `docs/design.md` "How they look" and "Parts" as one static baked mesh, instanced unit primitives for the repeated parts and the engine as its own gimballed draw; shades it with the materials and light of `decision 80: The Mule''s meshes in numbers: geometry, draws, materials and lights`, with the stencilled registration M-07 on the cab; and draws its navigation lights, strobe and engine glow. It builds on `plan 16: Rendering foundation and assets` (passes, camera, render state, `live-pipeline`, `texture-from-pixels`, captures), `plan 14: Ship, integrator and flight computer` (the parts table, `ship-mass-properties`, `ship-trim-angle`, the RCS layout, controls) and `plan 17: Earth, Moon, Sun and stars` (the impostors drawn before it, `sun-fraction`, the exposure''s ship term); it removes `PLACEHOLDER(77)`.

## Sources and decisions
- Specification: `docs/design.md` "Scope" → "Feature list": "The Mule''s meshes, materials and lights; the registration M-07 stencilled on the cab" (must, 2) and "Engine glow; navigation lights and strobe" (should, 2); the sections "How they look", "Parts", "Body axes and reference point", "Propulsion" (the gimbal and trim), "RCS forces, torques and accelerations" (the blocks and nozzles), "The DIY look" (window and portholes seen from outside), `docs/story.md` "Names and terms" and Cairn Water''s livery (white cab, a grey cairn of three stones, the registration in black on both sides).
- Decisions: `decision 49: Story: names, call signs and ships` (M-07), `decision 22: The player''s ship and its propulsion`, `decision 71: The Mule''s mass model in ship.lisp`, `decision 30: Views and cameras` (the cockpit eye the portholes are placed from; the outside view''s 1.2 × bounding radius), `decision 34: How death works` (the ship drawn in the death view), `decision 15: Coordinate frames` (FRD, q body → inertial).
- Rendering: `decision 4: Depth strategy`, `decision 5: Precision of positions`, `decision 11: Render pipelines are luv live shader pipelines`, `decision 76: Frame passes, radiance scale and exposure`, `decision 77: The frame in main.lisp: render state, capture of a given sim, part 4''s placeholders and first asset`, `decision 79: Sunlight at a point and the sunlit ship in the exposure rule`; `docs/architecture.md` "Files in load order", "Rendering precision" (draw order item 3), "Floating-origin pitfalls" (blink phases on the CPU), "GPU tests", "Dev loop".
- luv''s limits: `docs/luv-capabilities.md` "Instancing many objects" (per-instance vertex buffers, `:step-mode :instance`), "Other findings" (no back-face culling: closed meshes rely on depth; `let*` only; no `atan`/`asin`), "Rendering" (`set-bind-group` after each `set-pipeline`; triangle lists; `canvas-frame-resource-cache`).
- Recorded for this plan: `decision 80: The Mule''s meshes in numbers: geometry, draws, materials and lights`: every size, position, material value, light and timing below without another source.
- Tests: `docs/architecture.md` "Testing" → "Rules for tests", "GPU tests"; `scripts/test` stays under ~15 s.

## Rules for every piece
- Load order (`decision 12: Module layout and package split`): `meshes.lisp` after `impostors.lisp` and before `ribbons`/`post` in both systems of `luv-in-space.asd`; `tests/meshes.lisp` after `tests/impostors.lisp`; GPU checks only in `tests/render.lisp`, in its one shared `with-capture-game` (320×200).
- Pure parts (generators, the Mule''s parts list, instance transforms, the decal pixels, the bounding radius, the strobe phase, the glow radiance) are functions without a GPU, tested without a canvas; the simulation files stay free of the HAL and of `meshes.lisp`.
- Geometry in ship body axes from R in single floats (the body frame spans < 20 m); the only world placement is R''s position r − q c relative to the eye, through `eye-relative-single` of a double difference (`decision 5`); the rotation from `mat3-from-quat` of the interpolated q each frame, never accumulated.
- Live code ("Dev loop"): shaders in `shaders.lisp`, one live pipeline per role (`:ship-mesh`, `:ship-instanced`, `:ship-glow`) returned by the renderer''s live artifacts; static vertex, instance and decal resources made once by the renderer and destroyed by `destroy-renderer`; per-draw uniforms through `canvas-frame-resource-cache`; the material table and every light number `defparameter`s, uploaded each frame.
- Each piece passes `scripts/test` and `scripts/screenshot`, keeps `AGENTS.md` "Layout" and `docs/architecture.md` "Files in load order" true; a luv gap goes to `docs/luv-capabilities.md` and `docs/luv-wishlist.md`.

## Pieces, in the order they run

### 1. `meshes.lisp`, part one: generators, the Mule''s parts, the decal (CPU only)
- Vertex format (`decision 80`): position, normal, material id, two parameters; 9 singles per vertex, triangle lists. Generators appending into an adjustable single-float vector with a transform (origin, unit basis, scale): `box`, `cylinder` (n sides, optional end caps), `frustum` (two radii, n sides), `hemisphere` (rings × sides), `disc`, `rect` (for decals and openings), each writing outward normals and its parameters (axial coordinate along x for tubes, cones and the bell; u, v over decals, window, hatch, portholes).
- Instance groups: unit 8-sided tube (x 0 to 1, radius 1), unit box, unit cone; an instance = origin, unit basis, scale, material (16 singles); `(tube-instance a b radius material)` builds the basis from the endpoints.
- `(mule-model)`: the parts of "How they look" with `decision 80`''s numbers, positions from "Parts" (truss, tanks with domes, cab with flat ends, window, portholes, hatch and its red outline, decal rectangles on both sides, grapple with claws, RCS blocks and nozzles placed from `ship.lisp`''s thruster table, wings and booms, radiators, equipment bay, gas conditioning, struts); returns a `model` struct: the baked vertex vector, the instance groups, the engine''s own baked mesh, the light positions; each part tagged with its name, so a part''s vertex range can be looked up.
- Engine transform: `(engine-rotation m-p)`: the rotation about body y by `ship-trim-angle` through the gimbal (0, 0, −0.5).
- `(stencil-decal-pixels)`: a 128 × 512 rank-2 `(unsigned-byte 32)` array (the format of `texture-from-pixels`): Cairn Water''s cairn and "M-07" in black stencil letters on a 5 × 7 grid with bridges, alpha 0 elsewhere (the shader mixes it over the white paint).
- `(ship-bounding-radius model m-p)`: the largest distance from the CoM at `m-p` to any vertex (instances expanded, the engine at its trim), consing nothing after the first call.
- Tests `tests/meshes.lisp`:
  - Generators: a box has 12 triangles and a closed `cylinder` 8 sides 4 n triangles; every normal unit within 1e-6; for closed primitives every triangle''s normal points away from the primitive''s centre (n · (centroid − centre) > 0); the parameters span 0 to the length along the axis.
  - Instances: `tube-instance` maps the unit tube''s (0, 0, 0) and (1, 0, 0) to a and b within 1e-6 m and its radius to `radius`; normals stay unit and perpendicular to b − a.
  - The Mule: instance counts 88 truss members, 8 RCS blocks, 24 nozzles, 3 claws; the whole model''s bounding box x [−2.2, 16.6], y [−8.3, 8.3], z [−5.0, 3.3] within 0.01 m; part boxes: cab x [10.5, 15.0], z [−5.0, −2.0]; LH2 tank x [0.50, 9.36] and LOX tank x [9.76, 13.48], both Ø 3.2; grapple x [15.0, 16.6]; engine x [−2.2, 0] at zero trim; each nozzle''s axis within 1e-6 of minus its thruster''s force direction in `ship.lisp`.
  - Trim: the engine''s axis through the gimbal, turned by `engine-rotation`, passes within 1e-6 m of the CoM for full, half and empty tanks.
  - Decal: 128 × 512; the letter cells hold 15–60 % opaque black pixels each; the "0" cell''s ring is broken (a transparent path from its inside to its outside: the stencil bridge); the cairn''s three ellipses are grey.
  - Bounding radius: equals a brute-force maximum within 1e-6 m; wet and dry values written in the test''s comment (expect ~12–13 m, set by the engine''s exit rim); the second call conses nothing.
- Docs: `docs/architecture.md` "Files in load order" (`meshes.lisp` milestone 2 as built so far).
- Done: the tests pass; `./sly eval ''(length (model-vertices (mule-model)))'' --package LUV-IN-SPACE` prints the vertex count, noted in the done note.

### 2. `meshes.lisp`, part two: the hull drawn and lit; `PLACEHOLDER(77)` goes
- Renderer: the baked vertices and the engine mesh in static vertex buffers, the unit primitives and the instance groups in static buffers (`:step-mode :instance`), the decal texture through `texture-from-pixels`, all made once and destroyed with the renderer.
- Shaders (`shaders.lisp`): the vertex stage rotates by the draw''s single mat3 (the engine''s also by its gimbal rotation, through the gimbal point), adds the eye-relative translation of R and applies the clip rows of `camera-clip-rows!`; instanced vertices take origin, basis and scale from the instance first. One fragment function for both pipelines: the material table, weld darkening near tube ends, foil noise normals, the throat band, the decal over the white paint, the hatch''s red outline, window and porthole glass; the sun term, earthshine and ambient of `decision 80` on the radiance scale of `decision 76`, with `sun-fraction` from the render state (dark in Earth''s shadow).
- `(encode-ship pass renderer render-state camera)` in the scene pass after the impostors and before the ribbons (`docs/architecture.md` "Rendering precision", item 3): opaque, depth written, compare `:greater`; three draws (baked hull, instances, engine). The per-frame emissive colour and lights-on slots of `decision 80` exist with their defaults (zero, on).
- `post.lisp`''s exposure term (`decision 79`) uses `ship-bounding-radius` at the render state''s m_p in place of `*ship-bounding-radius*` 10.0, which goes.
- `PLACEHOLDER(77)` leaves the code and the on-screen label; `AGENTS.md` and `docs/` mention it no more.
- Tests `tests/render.lisp` (GPU, the shared capture):
  - The ship in front of a known background: the start state with a camera 40 m from the ship that the test picks so the Sun is within 60° behind it and neither Earth''s nor the Moon''s disc is in the frame (checked by projecting with `camera-clip-rows!` before rendering): the pixel at the cab''s projected centre is lit white (each channel above 150, |r − b| < 30); every pixel more than 8 px outside the projected bounding sphere is below 20 of 255; the count of pixels above 40 lies within bounds set from the first measured frame (×0.5, ×2, in the test''s comment).
  - The registration: a camera 8 m out on +y, level with the decal and facing it: inside the decal''s projected rectangle the darkest pixel is below 60 and the brightest above 150 (black letters on white paint); the same on −y.
  - In Earth''s shadow (u = 180° of the start orbit), the same camera as the first check: the mean over the ship''s projected bounding box stays below 40 (no sunlight; earthshine near zero on the night side).
  - `report-failed-live-artifacts` returns 0; the whole `scripts/test` under ~15 s (measured, in the done note).
- Docs: `docs/design.md` "How they look": the parts and materials as built, with `decision 80` cited; "Views": the exposure''s ship term uses the stack''s computed bounding radius; `docs/architecture.md` "Rendering precision" item 3 (R placed at r − q c, the engine''s gimbal draw), "GPU tests" (the ship checks); `docs/luv-capabilities.md` "Instancing many objects" (built and ran: the tube instances, their count, on lavapipe); `docs/luv-wishlist.md` a mesh-primitives item (box, tube, frustum, hemisphere generators and instanced primitives) naming `meshes.lisp` and its size in lines; back-face culling''s item gains the ship''s overdraw as a reason if measured; `AGENTS.md` "Layout" (`src/`: the ship''s meshes, the placeholders left).
- Done: the tests pass; `scripts/screenshot` shows the Mule from the default outside view (truss, gold and silver tanks, white cab), no `PLACEHOLDER(77)` in the label; with `./sly play`, the ship turns with the rotation keys or a hold and stays fixed to Earth''s background without jitter; a re-evaluated `(setf (aref *ship-materials* …))` or the fragment shader shows on the next frame; `./sly screenshot` at night shows the hull dark.

### 3. Lights and engine glow ("should")
- Pipeline `:ship-glow` (`decision 80`): camera-facing Gaussian billboards, additive (premultiplied alpha 0), depth-tested against the hull and Earth without depth write, drawn after the hull in the scene pass; per instance the eye-relative centre (single), world radius, minimum 1.5 px across with the radiance scaled down by the area ratio when enlarged, and the RGB radiance.
- Lights from the model''s light positions turned by q and placed from R: red at −y and green at +y (steady), the strobe on the cab top on for `*strobe-on*` of each `*strobe-period*`, `(strobe-on-p t)` from the render time reduced in double on the CPU ("Floating-origin pitfalls"); all off when the lights-on slot is off.
- Engine glow: `(engine-glow-radiance render-state)` = `*engine-glow-radiance*` × throttle while the engine fires (throttle > 0, propellant > 0, not lost), 0 otherwise; the billboard at the nozzle exit''s centre after the trim rotation; bloom (`post.lisp`) spreads it.
- Tests `tests/meshes.lisp`: `strobe-on-p` is true at t = 0.05 and false at 0.5, and keeps its phase at t = 2^30 + 0.05 s (on) and 2^30 + 0.5 s (off); `engine-glow-radiance` is 0 at throttle 0, with empty tanks and after a loss, and proportional to the throttle from 0.1 to 1.0 within 1e-12.
- Tests `tests/render.lisp` (GPU, the shared capture): in Earth''s shadow with the first check''s camera, the red light''s projected pixel has r > 150 and r > g + 50, the green light''s g > 150 and g > r + 50; with `*strobe-on*` bound to `*strobe-period*` the strobe''s projected pixel is brighter by more than 50 than with `*strobe-on*` 0.0; a camera 30 m behind the ship on its −x axis, in shadow: the nozzle exit''s projected pixel with throttle 1.0 in the state is bluer (b > r) and brighter by more than 20 than with throttle 0; `report-failed-live-artifacts` 0; `scripts/test` under ~15 s.
- Docs: `docs/design.md` "How they look": the lights and glow as built (positions, timing, radiances, the floodlight and range-finder light moved to the grapple in `milestone 3`); `docs/architecture.md` "GPU tests" (the light checks).
- Done: the tests pass; with `./sly play` at ×100 through a night pass, the ship goes dark and the red, green and blinking white lights stay; `(setf *engine-glow-radiance* 5.0)` and full throttle show a blue glow at the nozzle on the next frame (a screenshot in the done note''s path).

## What later parts take from here
- Part 7 (input and the outside view): `ship-bounding-radius` for the orbit camera''s 1.2 × minimum distance (`decision 30`).
- Part 9 (the LOST panel): the per-frame emissive colour slot for the 3 s orange glow of an Earth impact (`docs/design.md` "Dying and what follows"); the label without `PLACEHOLDER(77)`.
- Part 10 (flight scripts): screenshots show the ship from the outside view.
- `milestone 3`: the cockpit matches the window and portholes of `decision 80`; the grapple''s floodlight and range-finder light; places and other ships built from the same generators and instances (`docs/design.md` "Other ships"); `milestone 4`: the asteroid meshes and the lights-on slot for heat and life-support deaths.', '2026-10-14T22:34:33Z', 77);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (19, 2, 'Input and the outside view', 450, 'Part 7 of `plan 2: masterplan`: how the player flies and looks. `input.lisp` turns luv''s canvas events into the input state and, once per frame, into the sim''s `controls` and the clock''s commands, with the milestone-2 key map of `docs/design.md` "Controls"; `camera.lisp` grows part 4''s default camera into the outside view''s orbit camera (orbital frame or locked with C, wheel, right-drag, Home). Escape and Control+Q quit; the window title lists the main keys. It builds on `plan 14: Ship, integrator and flight computer` (the `controls-…` commands), `plan 15: Clock, warp, hazards and events` (warp, pause, `clock-kinematic-p`, messages) and `plan 16: Rendering foundation and assets` (the `camera` struct, `outside-camera-default!`, `render-state`, `main.lisp`''s frame); `plan 2`''s parts 8 (map), 9 (LOST panel) and 10 (flight scripts) bind or press keys through it.

## Sources and decisions
- Files and load order: `docs/architecture.md` "Files in load order" (`decision 12: Module layout and package split`): `controls.lisp` (extended), `camera.lisp` (extended), `input.lisp` after `render.lisp` and before `main.lisp`; `tests/input.lisp` after `tests/render.lisp` and before `tests/capture.lisp` in `luv-in-space/tests`.
- Keys and modes: `docs/design.md` "Controls", "Control modes", "Time warp" (pause, refusals), "Dying and what follows"; `decision 29: Control scheme: key map, control modes, gamepad`, `decision 31: Warp choices within the time-warp rules` ("RCS OFF ABOVE ×4"), `decision 73: Warp levels above x4: entry, holds and lowering`, `decision 70: Flight computer: per-step control laws, holds and the RCS mixer`.
- The per-frame sample: `docs/architecture.md` "The clock", "Dev loop" (lambdas calling named functions, state in `*game*`, `defparameter` tunables); `decision 16: Integrator and simulation clock`.
- Views: `docs/design.md` "Views"; `decision 30: Views and cameras`; `docs/architecture.md` "Frames" (FRD camera), "Rendering precision" (the camera relative to the ship, `decision 5: Precision of positions`); `decision 77: The frame in main.lisp: render state, capture of a given sim, part 4''s placeholders and first asset`.
- luv: `docs/luv-capabilities.md` "Windowing and input": `canvas-key-press-event` / `-release-event` (`canvas-key-event-key-name`, `-modifiers` with `:control`, `-repeat-p`), `canvas-pointer-button-press-event` / `-release-event` (`canvas-pointer-event-button` `:right`), `canvas-pointer-motion-event` (`delta-x`, `delta-y`), `canvas-pointer-wheel-event` (`scroll-y` in notches, up positive), `canvas-window-focus-lost-event`, `canvas-window-close-request-event`, `set-canvas-relative-pointer-mode`; key names from `sdl-scancode-key-name` in `../luv/hal/sdl/canvas.lisp` (`:shift-left`, `:control-left`, `:|.|`, `:|,|`, `:/`, `:page-up`; digits and letters as SDL''s scancode keywords, checked by the worker).
- Script actions that press keys: `docs/architecture.md` "Scripted runs" → "Actions" (`(:keys (<key> …) :for <s>)`), `decision 56: Flight-script actions, start states and the quest play-through`.
- Recorded for this plan: `decision 81: Input in milestone 2: held keys per frame, taps, gating and the shared key map`, `decision 82: Outside view camera: angles in a frame, toggles that keep the picture, distance and easing`.
- Tests: `docs/architecture.md` "Testing" → "Rules for tests"; synthetic luv events (`make-instance` of the event classes) on a game without a canvas; no GPU test is needed here, the screenshots of the done checks show the views.

## Rules for every piece
- `controls.lisp` stays a simulation file (no HAL, SDL, `*game*`); `input.lisp` and `camera.lisp` read the sim and write only `controls` through its commands and the clock through its commands, never the ship state.
- Input and camera state live in the `game` (`input`, `outside-view` slots with `:initform`s), not in the `sim`: checkpoints, restarts and determinism don''t see them (`decision 81`, `decision 82`).
- Every event goes through `handle-event` (main.lisp''s lambda keeps calling it by name); the key map is a `defparameter` list of (key . command), so `./sly eval` rebinding a key works on the next press. Tunables (0.5/s ramp, 0.25°/px, ×1.2, τ 0.15 s, ±85°, 5 km) are `defparameter`s citing their decision.
- `relative pointer mode` and the window are touched only when the game has a canvas, so tests run without one.
- Each piece adds its files to `luv-in-space.asd` (both systems), passes `scripts/test` and `scripts/screenshot`, and keeps `AGENTS.md` ("Commands", "Layout") and `docs/architecture.md` "Files in load order" true. A luv gap met goes to `docs/luv-capabilities.md` and `docs/luv-wishlist.md`.

## Pieces, in the order they run

### 1. The shared held-key map and `input.lisp`''s event path: held keys, taps, focus, quit
- `controls.lisp` (extends part 2''s file, `decision 81`): `*flight-keys*`: W/S pitch −/+, A/D yaw −/+, Q/E roll −/+, H/N +x/−x, I/K −z/+z, J/L −y/+y as data (key, rotation or translation, axis, sign); `(controls-apply-held-keys! controls keys)`: from a list of held key names sets each rotation and translation key state to the sum of its keys'' signs (opposite keys cancel) through `controls-set-rotation-key` / `controls-set-translation-key`, so their rules (a pitch or yaw key releases a hold; a roll key turns the hold''s roll) apply only on a change. Part 10''s `(:keys …)` calls the same function.
- `input.lisp`: `input` struct (held keys, keys pressed since the last frame, keys released since the last frame, right-drag flag, pointer deltas summed since the last frame, wheel notches since the last frame); `(handle-event game event)` moved from `main.lisp`: key press (not repeat) adds the key to held and pressed, release removes it from held and records it; `canvas-window-focus-lost-event` clears held, pressed and drag and leaves relative pointer mode; Escape, Control+Q (key `:q` with `:control` in the modifiers) and the close request set `game-quit-requested-p`.
- `(input-frame! game elapsed)`, called by `main.lisp`''s `draw` before `clock-frame!`: held keys plus the keys pressed and released since the last frame (the tap latch: a press and release between two frames is held for this one frame) go to `controls-apply-held-keys!` on `(sim-controls (game-sim game))`; then the latches clear.
- Tests `tests/input.lisp` (a game from `(new-game-sim)`, a clock, no canvas, no renderer; a helper making key events):
  - W pressed, one `input-frame!`: pitch −1 and the start''s prograde hold released to rate damping; W released, next frame: pitch 0. W and S both held: 0. Each of the twelve flight keys moves its axis with its sign.
  - Tap: W pressed and released before one `input-frame!`: pitch −1 for that frame, 0 at the next.
  - A repeat press changes nothing; a release without a press changes nothing.
  - Focus loss with W, H held: next frame all rotation and translation keys 0; throttle unchanged.
  - Escape, Control+Q and the close request each set the quit flag; Q alone does not (it is roll left).
  - `controls-apply-held-keys!` with `(:a :d :q)`: yaw 0, roll −1 (no input or game: the function part 10 uses).
- Docs: `docs/design.md` "Controls": a bullet on the tap latch and the per-frame sample; `docs/architecture.md` "Files in load order": `controls.lisp` also holds the held-key map, `input.lisp` the input state and `handle-event`; "The clock": input writes `controls` once per frame before the steps.
- Done: the tests pass; with `./sly start`, `./sly play`, holding W in the window turns the ship and releasing it stops the turn (or, without a human, `./sly eval` of `handle-event` with a synthetic W press, two frames later `(controls … pitch)` is −1); Escape and Control+Q close the window; `./sly stop-playing`, `./sly stop`.

### 2. Commands, ramps, gating and the window title
- The key map `*key-map*` (`decision 29`, `decision 81`): F `controls-toggle-fine`; Z `controls-throttle-full`, X `controls-throttle-cut`; T `controls-toggle-rate-damping`; R `controls-inertial-hold`; 1–6 `controls-set-hold` prograde, retrograde, normal, anti-normal, radial out, radial in; `.` `clock-warp-up`, `,` `clock-warp-down`, `/` `clock-warp-reset`; P `clock-toggle-pause`; C, Home: piece 3; M, Tab, Page-up, Page-down: commands `:map`, `:map-focus`, `:map-zoom-in`, `:map-zoom-out` whose handlers do nothing yet (`PLACEHOLDER(79)`, the map''s plan; part 8 replaces them); a `game-view` slot (`:outside`; part 8 adds `:map`). Discrete commands run at the press, in `handle-event`; the refusals and lowering are the clock''s (`plan 15`), which writes their messages.
- Throttle ramp in `input-frame!`: Shift-left held raises the lever by `*throttle-ramp-rate*` 0.5 × min(elapsed, 0.1 s) of real time up to 1.0, a press from off giving 0.1; Control-left held lowers it by the same, below 0.1 off (`controls-set-throttle`''s rule). Control+Q does not move the lever by more than one frame''s ramp.
- Gating (`decision 81`): paused, flight keys (the held map, F, Z, X, T, R, 1–6, Shift, Control) are ignored and the ramp stops, while P, `.`, `,`, `/`, C, Home, M, Tab, Page keys, the mouse and quit act; above ×4 (`clock-kinematic-p`) the held rotation and translation keys are ignored (`controls-apply-held-keys!` sees none of them) and each press writes the clock''s message "RCS OFF ABOVE ×4" once, while holds, T, R, F and the throttle act; lost (`sim-lost-p`) every flight key is ignored (part 9 binds Return and N).
- Window title (`(window-title)`, pure, given to `make-sdl-canvas` in `main.lisp`): "Luv in Space · WASD QE turn · HN IK JL move · Shift/Ctrl throttle · Z/X full/cut · T damp · R 1–6 hold · F fine · . , / warp · P pause · C camera · M map · Esc quit" (QWERTY positions, `decision 81`).
- Tests `tests/input.lisp`:
  - Throttle: Shift pressed from off: 0.1 after the first frame; held for 60 frames of 1/60 s more: 0.6 within 1e-9; frames of 1/144 s over the same real second give the same within 1e-9; one frame of 0.5 s elapsed adds 0.05 (the cap); Control held from 0.15 for 0.2 s gives 0 (below 0.1); Z gives 1.0, X 0.
  - Commands: 1 engages prograde, 1 again releases to rate damping; 5 radial out; R an inertial hold at the current attitude; T from rate damping gives direct; F toggles fine; `.` twice gives ×10 (a coasting start at the prograde hold, not refused), `/` gives ×1; P pauses and P resumes the level.
  - Gating: paused, W and 1 and Shift change nothing and the ramp stops, P and `.` still act; at ×10 W changes no key state, the hold stays, and the clock holds one "RCS OFF ABOVE ×4" message per press; at ×10 the throttle ramps and 2 changes the hold; lost, every flight key is ignored.
  - Title: names every key of the milestone-2 table that `*key-map*` binds.
- Docs: `docs/design.md` "Controls" (what is ignored while paused, lost and above ×4; the title''s text); `AGENTS.md` "Commands" → Run: Escape or Control+Q quits, the title lists the keys; `docs/luv-wishlist.md`: a key-name → layout-character function exported by luv''s canvas (the title and milestone 6''s help would show the layout''s characters), its workaround here (QWERTY names).
- Done: the tests pass; `nix run` shows the title with the keys (the agent closes the window by PID); in `./sly play`, `./sly eval` of synthetic presses (`.`, `.`, `P`) changes `(clock …)`''s level and pause as the readout will show.

### 3. `camera.lisp`: the outside view''s orbit camera, C, Home, wheel and right-drag
