INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (129, 21, 'readout-lines: the milestone-2 readout rows, alarms and messages as a pure function', 'work', 'open', NULL, NULL, 'Piece 1 of `plan 21: Minimal readout, messages and the LOST panel` (read its piece 1 in full: rows, formats, colours, alarms, messages and the test list). Binding: `decision 85: Minimal readout: layout, formats, colours and alarms`, `decision 84: Map ribbons and markers: osculating orbit, adaptive segments, overlay markers and visibility` (the impact time is the readout''s, from the prediction), `decision 75: Prediction in milestone 2: the step function on a copy` (the `:impact` event), `decision 66: Calendar dates on the 1/64 s grid` (UTC truncated to whole seconds). Needs the glyphs and `format-duration` of the task before it in this plan.

## What
- `src/overlay.lisp`: `(readout-lines render-state clock real-time)` → a list of lines, each a list of runs (text, colour, style `:plain`/`:inverted`, flashing flag): `ALT`, `SPD`, `AP`/`PE` (`AP ESCAPE` when e ≥ 1), `THR`, `PROP`, `MODE`, `WARP` (with `ACT ×<rate>` amber "!" below 0.9 × nominal, `PAUSED`), `UTC`; alarms `! PE BELOW 100 KM` (amber) and `<EARTH|MOON> IMPACT IN <format-duration>` (red, inverted, flashing at 2 Hz of `real-time`, on in the first half of each 0.5 s); then up to `*readout-messages*` 3 of `clock-messages`, newest last, amber with "!", cut at 40 characters with "…".
- Colours (`defparameter`s citing `decision 85`): labels #8A8A80, values #E8E4D8, caution #FFB000, danger #FF3030.
- Values from the render state (interpolated, t_r); apsides via `state->orbit` with μ⊕. All strings upcased.
- `docs/design.md` "Instruments": a short bullet list after the table with the milestone-2 rows as `decision 85` lays them out (labels, units, decimals, alarms, where the impact time comes from).

## Tests (`tests/overlay.lisp`, CPU)
- All cases of the plan''s piece 1 "Tests": the start state (`ALT 400.0 KM`, `SPD 7.669 KM/S`, `AP`/`PE` 400.0, `THR 0% OFF`, `PROP 28000 KG 100%`, `MODE HOLD PROGRADE SETTLED`, `WARP ×1`, `UTC 2040-01-01 00:00:00`, no alarm, no message); the crafted formats (950 m/s, 1,000 m/s, e = 1.2, throttle 0.45 with and without propellant, rate damp + fine, paused ×1,000, actual rate 490,000 / 950,000 at ×1,000,000); the 400 × 50 km orbit''s PE alarm and, with the prediction run to completion, the impact alarm (duration = impact time − t_r, flashing on at 0.1 s, off at 0.35 s); a refused warp-up message present then gone after 3 s of the clock''s real time; a 60-character message cut to 40 with "…".
- `glyph-missing` nil for every string of every line above.
- One `readout-lines` call conses under 16 KB.

## Done when
- `scripts/test` passes; `./sly eval ''(readout-lines …)'' --package LUV-IN-SPACE` on the live game (`./sly start`, `./sly play`) prints the rows; stop what you started.', '2026-10-14T23:04:59Z', NULL, 90, 0);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (130, 21, 'Draw the readout in the overlay pass, capture-state :readout, remove PLACEHOLDER(80)', 'work', 'open', NULL, NULL, 'Piece 2 of `plan 21: Minimal readout, messages and the LOST panel`. Binding: `decision 85: Minimal readout: layout, formats, colours and alarms` (2 px dot pitch, 12 × 22 px cells, 16 px margin, opaque #0A0C0C box; captures take `:readout`), `decision 76: Frame passes, radiance scale and exposure` (the overlay is after the tone map, exact colours), `decision 77: The frame in main.lisp: render state, capture of a given sim, part 4''s placeholders and first asset`. Needs `readout-lines` (the task before it in this plan) and `label-at!` (`plan 20: The basic map`).

## What
- `src/overlay.lisp`: `(readout-vertices! out lines x y)`: the opaque #0A0C0C box sized to the lines, each run through `label-at!`; an inverted run draws its colour block with the text in #0A0C0C; a flashing run in its off phase draws nothing. Sizes and colours `defparameter`s.
- Overlay vertex capacity a `defparameter` sized for the readout, the map''s labels and the LOST panel together; per-frame data through part 4''s frame resource cache (never a buffer the previous frame still reads); text past capacity is cut, never a crash.
- `src/render.lisp`: the overlay pass draws the readout top left in `:outside` and `:map` (`game-view`), after the map''s markers and labels, from the frame''s render state, the clock and the game''s real time (summed elapsed, kept in `main.lisp`''s game).
- The `PLACEHOLDER(76 77 80)` label loses 80 (or goes, if 76 and 77 are gone); `PLACEHOLDER(80)` removed from code, `docs/` and `AGENTS.md` (search with `rg -n ''PLACEHOLDER\(.*80''`).
- `src/capture.lisp`: `capture-state` takes `:readout` (default t). Re-run every earlier GPU test; where a region check now falls under the readout box, that test passes `:readout nil` (say so in its comment) rather than moving its bounds.
- Docs: `AGENTS.md` "Layout" (`src/`: the readout in `overlay.lisp`) and "Commands" → Screenshot (the PNG shows the readout); `docs/architecture.md` "GPU tests" (`capture-state`''s `:readout`).

## Tests (`tests/render.lisp`, GPU, in the existing `with-capture-game`)
- The start state''s capture: inside the readout box''s computed rectangle only pixels of exactly #0A0C0C, #8A8A80 and #E8E4D8, at least one of each; with `:readout nil` that rectangle has some pixel not #0A0C0C; `report-failed-live-artifacts` 0.

## Done when
- `scripts/test` passes; `scripts/screenshot /tmp/r.png` shows the readout top left, legible (look at the PNG); in `./sly play`, `./sly eval` of `(clock-warp-up …)` twice shows `WARP ×10` in the next `./sly screenshot`; the frame time with the readout in the done note. Close every window and stop the image you started.', '2026-10-14T23:04:59Z', NULL, 90, 0);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (131, 21, 'Death record, game-restart! and the orange death glow in main.lisp', 'work', 'open', NULL, NULL, 'Piece 3, first part, of `plan 21: Minimal readout, messages and the LOST panel`. Binding: `decision 86: Death view, LOST panel and restart in milestone 2`, `decision 34: How death works`, `decision 74: Crash test on the step''s Hermite and the death state` (the loss record), `decision 80: The Mule''s meshes in numbers: geometry, draws, materials and lights` (the ship''s per-frame emissive slot), `decision 76: Frame passes, radiance scale and exposure`, `decision 81: Input in milestone 2: held keys per frame, taps, gating and the shared key map`.

## What
- `src/main.lisp`: the game keeps its summed real time (if piece 2 didn''t add it), the loss last seen and the real time of the death. On the first frame with a new `sim-loss`, `game-view` becomes `:outside` with the outside view''s offset unchanged, and the death time is recorded.
- `(game-restart! game)`: `clock-restart!` with the start-state checkpoint, view `:outside`, `outside-view-reset!`, map view closed, the input''s held keys, taps and drag cleared, the death record cleared, the exposure snapped.
- `(death-glow-radiance loss seconds-since-death)` (pure): `*death-glow-radiance*` 4.0 for t ≤ 1 s, linear to 0 at 3 s, 0 after; 0 for a Moon impact. The renderer writes `*death-glow-colour*` #FF6A1A × that ÷ the current exposure into the ship''s emissive slot. Numbers `defparameter`s citing `decision 86`.
- Docs: `docs/architecture.md` "Checkpoints": `game-restart!` is the restore the LOST panel calls; `docs/design.md` "Dying and what follows" → "What the player sees": the glow''s curve and the camera that may still be orbited.

## Tests (no GPU; `tests/input.lisp` or `tests/render.lisp`, whichever already builds a game without a canvas; `death-glow-radiance` in `tests/overlay.lisp`)
- `death-glow-radiance`: 4.0 at 0 and 1 s, 2.0 at 2 s, 0 at 3 s and 10 s within 1e-12; 0 for a Moon loss.
- A game from `plan 15: Clock, warp, hazards and events`''s deorbit state in `:map`, run through `clock-frame!` to the impact: the view becomes `:outside`, the outside view''s offset unchanged, the death time recorded.
- `game-restart!` gives a sim `equalp` to `(new-game-sim)` at ×1 unpaused, view `:outside` with the default outside camera, no held keys; 1,000 frames of 1/60 s after it equal 1,000 frames after a fresh game bit for bit.

## Done when
- `scripts/test` passes.', '2026-10-14T23:04:59Z', NULL, 90, 0);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (132, 21, 'The LOST panel, Return and N while lost, capture-state :death-elapsed', 'work', 'open', NULL, NULL, 'Piece 3, second part, of `plan 21: Minimal readout, messages and the LOST panel`. Binding: `decision 86: Death view, LOST panel and restart in milestone 2`, `decision 34: How death works` (in milestone 2 Return and N are the same restart), `decision 81: Input in milestone 2: held keys per frame, taps, gating and the shared key map`, `decision 85: Minimal readout: layout, formats, colours and alarms` (text, colours). Needs `readout-vertices!` and `game-restart!` / `death-glow-radiance` (earlier tasks of this plan).

## What
- `src/overlay.lisp`: `(lost-panel-lines loss)` (pure), in the readout''s line format: `LOST: CRASH` red inverted (steady), `<EARTH|MOON>  <UTC of the impact>`, `SPEED <as the readout''s SPD>`, `LAT <|lat|, 2 decimals>° <N|S>  LON <|lon|, 2 decimals>° <E|W>`, a blank line, `RETURN  RESTART FROM START`, `N       NEW GAME` (keys #E8E4D8, texts #8A8A80). Drawn on an opaque #0A0C0C box centred horizontally, top at `*lost-panel-top*` 0.6 of the height; the readout stays.
- `src/input.lisp`: `*key-map*` binds `:return` and `:n` to `:restart` while `sim-lost-p` (N stays translate-back otherwise; flight keys ignored while lost); `:restart` calls `game-restart!`. M, the mouse and quit still act.
- `src/capture.lisp`: `capture-state` of a lost sim draws the glow at `:death-elapsed` (default 0, the peak) and the panel.
- Docs: `docs/design.md` "Dying and what follows" → "What the player sees" (the panel''s lines and place, Return and N in milestone 2) and "Controls" (Return and N while lost); `AGENTS.md` "Commands" → Run: after a crash, Return or N restarts; `docs/architecture.md` "GPU tests" (`:death-elapsed`).

## Tests
- `tests/overlay.lisp`: a loss from `plan 15: Clock, warp, hazards and events`''s radial free fall (Earth) gives `LOST: CRASH`, `EARTH`, the speed as the readout formats it, latitude and longitude from the record with hemisphere letters; a Moon loss gives `MOON`; `glyph-missing` nil for every string.
- `tests/input.lisp` (synthetic events, no canvas): not lost, N is a held translation key and Return does nothing; lost, W and 1 change nothing and Return restarts; after a restart by N the next `input-frame!` holds no key (no translation in the controls); N and Return give the same sim.
- `tests/render.lisp` (GPU): the capture of a lost sim at the impact (plan 15''s deorbit case) has the panel box''s rectangle in exact #0A0C0C, #FF3030, #E8E4D8 and #8A8A80 only; the ship''s pixels at `:death-elapsed` 0 are redder (mean R − B) than at 3.5 s by a margin set from the frame.

## Done when
- `scripts/test` passes and stays under ~15 s (measured, in the done note); in `./sly play`, `./sly eval` setting the deorbit state (or a 200 s retrograde burn) and warping to the impact shows the orange glow and the panel in `./sly screenshot`s, and a synthetic Return brings back the start state with the readout at `ALT 400.0 KM`. Close every window and stop the image you started.', '2026-10-14T23:04:59Z', NULL, 90, 0);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (133, 22, 'clock-step!, prediction-apsis and read-flight: the safe script reader and step parser', 'work', 'open', NULL, NULL, 'Piece 1, first part, of `plan 22: Flight scripts and the Done when` (read its "Rules for every piece" and piece 1 in full). Binding: `decision 87: Flight scripts in milestone 2: additions, apsis conditions, live fly and scripts/fly` (the reader, the additions, apsis conditions on the prediction), `decision 56: Flight-script actions, start states and the quest play-through` (the vocabulary and its milestones), `decision 72: Simulation state and the one step function in clock.lisp`, `decision 73: Warp levels above x4: entry, holds and lowering`, `decision 75: Prediction in milestone 2: the step function on a copy`, `decision 66: Calendar dates on the 1/64 s grid`. Spec: `docs/architecture.md` "Scripted runs" ("Steps", "Actions", "Waits and expectations"), "Determinism", "Dev loop".

## What
- `src/clock.lisp`: if `clock-frame!` keeps the per-step body inline, factor it out as `(clock-step! clock sim)` (the lowering rules of `decision 73`, the clock''s `hook` with its `until`, `sim-step!`), called by `clock-frame!` with unchanged behaviour: `plan 15: Clock, warp, hazards and events`''s clock tests stay green bit for bit.
- `src/prediction.lisp`: `(prediction-apsis pred kind)` → time and radius of the next predicted `:apoapsis` / `:periapsis` from the stored Hermite or, with an `:impact` before it, the osculating apsis of the impact state (`state->orbit`); only if part 3 has no equivalent.
- `src/flights.lisp` (new, after `prediction.lisp` in `luv-in-space.asd`):
  - `flight-script-error` condition (message, line).
  - `(read-flight pathname-or-string)`: `*read-eval*` nil, `*read-default-float-format*` `double-float`, `*package*` a scratch package deleted after (`unwind-protect`); exactly one form; a non-keyword symbol or a second form rejected naming the line. A bare name (`"burn"`) resolves to `flights/<name>.sexp` via `asdf:system-relative-pathname`; a string starting with `(` is the script itself.
  - Parse each step into a step struct: timing (`:at` from the script''s start, `:after` from the previous step''s end, bare action = `:after 0`, `(:after 300)` alone lets time pass; every time to the 1/64 s grid with `grid-ceiling`, dates with `utc->grid-t`), the action, wait or expectation with its arguments (`:within`, default `*flight-default-within*` 30 days, a `defparameter`; `:before` on event waits; `:view :map` on `:screenshot`).
  - The milestone-2 vocabulary of the plan''s piece 1 ("Actions of milestone 2", "Waits", "Expectations") accepted; keywords of later milestones (`:target`, `:autopilot`, `:grapple`, `:release`, `:transfer`, `:translate`, `:approach`, `:ack`, `(:start :state …)` / `:after` / `:file`, `:soi`, `:dominant`, quest conditions, …) rejected with "NOT BEFORE MILESTONE <n>" (the milestone from `docs/architecture.md`''s "Actions" table and "Waits and expectations"); unknown keywords rejected naming the keyword.
- No simulation is run here; `run-flight` is the next task in this plan.

## Tests (`tests/flights.lisp`, new, CPU; last among the simulation tests in `luv-in-space.asd`, before `tests/render.lisp`)
- `#.` rejected; a non-keyword symbol rejected; two forms rejected; `(:autopilot …)` rejected with "NOT BEFORE MILESTONE 3"; `9.10` reads as a double; the error names the line.
- Parsing: `(:at 0.01 …)` gets t 0 or 1/64 by `grid-ceiling`; `:after` relative, bare action `:after 0`; `:date` on the grid equals `utc->grid-t`; the default `:within` is 30 days.
- `prediction-apsis`: from the start orbit after a small prograde burn, the apoapsis time and radius agree with `prediction-event`; with an impact predicted first (`plan 15`''s 400 × 50 km orbit), the osculating periapsis of the impact state.
- `scripts/test` stays under ~15 s.

## Docs
- `docs/architecture.md` "Files in load order": `flights.lisp` exists; "Running scripts": `clock-step!`. `AGENTS.md` "Layout": `src/` lists `flights.lisp`.

## Done when
- `scripts/test` passes (time in the done note); `./sly eval ''(read-flight "((:throttle 1.0) (:after 10 :throttle 0))")'' --package LUV-IN-SPACE` prints the parsed steps; stop what you started.', '2026-10-14T23:05:51Z', NULL, 91, 0);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (134, 22, 'run-flight headless: actions, waits, expectations, failures and the record', 'work', 'open', NULL, NULL, 'Piece 1, second part, of `plan 22: Flight scripts and the Done when` (read its "Rules for every piece" and piece 1 in full). Binding: `decision 87: Flight scripts in milestone 2: additions, apsis conditions, live fly and scripts/fly` (one per-step function for headless and live, apsis conditions on the prediction''s apsides, `(:restart)`, `:start-state`, `:impact-as-predicted`, `:before`), `decision 56: Flight-script actions, start states and the quest play-through`, `decision 72: Simulation state and the one step function in clock.lisp` (the `cutoff` slot, `until`), `decision 73: Warp levels above x4: entry, holds and lowering`, `decision 74: Crash test on the step''s Hermite and the death state`, `decision 75: Prediction in milestone 2: the step function on a copy`, `decision 66: Calendar dates on the 1/64 s grid`, `decision 21: Physics test bounds and flight scripts`. Needs `clock-step!`, `prediction-apsis` and `read-flight` (the task before it in this plan).

## What (`src/flights.lisp`)
- A `flight` struct: steps, the current step, the start state (a `copy-sim`), the record, coast flag, the last predicted impact time, failure.
- `(flight-step-hook flight clock sim)`: the per-step function run by the clock''s hook before each step (headless and, later, live): applies due actions, decides the step''s `until` for event waits, tests conditions after the step. The clock''s hook is a lambda calling the named function (live-redefinable).
- Actions: `(:start :new-game)` (also when absent), `(:warp <level>)` (repeated `clock-warp-up` / `-down`; a refusal fails the script with the clock''s message), `(:warp :coast)` (in the hook before each step raise to the highest level the clock accepts; `(:warp 1)` ends it; caps as `defparameter`s), `(:hold <mode>)` (`nil` → rate damping), `(:throttle <f>)` (0 or 0.1–1.0), `(:after-dv <m/s> :throttle 0)` (arms the sim''s `cutoff` at odometer + value), `(:keys (<key> …) :for <s>)` (`controls-apply-held-keys!` on, off after the time), `(:fine <t/nil>)`, `(:screenshot "<label>" [:view :map])` (a copy of the sim and the view into the record), `(:restart)` (only while lost; `clock-restart!` with the run''s start state).
- Waits: `:apoapsis`, `:periapsis`, `:impact` (prediction run to completion first; the event''s grid time less `:before` as `sim-step!`''s `until`; `:impact` remembers the predicted time); conditions after each step: `:altitude-below`, `:periapsis-below/-above`, `:apoapsis-below/-above` (via `prediction-apsis`), `:crashed`, `:settled`, `:rate-below <°/s>`, `:date "<UTC>"` (exact on the grid), `:propellant-above <kg>`, `:propellant-full`, `:drop-out :impact`; every wait with `:within`.
- Expectations: the same conditions now, plus `:lost :crash`, `:impact-as-predicted <s>`, `:start-state` (`equalp` with the run''s start state); a failed one is recorded and the script goes on.
- Failure: a wait out of time, a refused action, or a death not followed by `(:expect :lost :crash)` ends the script, failed, with reason and state.
- The record: entries per `:screenshot`, `:expect`, burn (throttle on → off: start, end, Δv from the odometer), failure, and the end: `t` and UTC, altitude, apoapsis, periapsis, speed, propellant, the loss. `(flight-record-lines record)` → one string per entry.
- `(run-flight script &key model)`: start state, fresh clock, then `prediction-update!` and `clock-step!` per step, no accumulator (headless warp: only the step size, thrust limits, refusals and drop-outs); `:model` overrides the gravity model (`:point` for two-body); returns the record and the end sim. No wall clock, no hash-table iteration.

## Tests (`tests/flights.lisp`, CPU, scripts as strings)
- The plan''s piece 1 tests: `(:after 300)` advances `t` by exactly 300 s; cut-off by `(:after-dv 25.0 :throttle 0)` at ×1 (odometer within the fine step''s Δv) and under `(:warp :coast)` (closed form), `t` on the grid, the apoapsis after it within 1 m between the two; `(:wait :apoapsis)` ends at the first grid point at or after the predicted time, `:before 10` 10 s earlier; `(:wait :date "2040-01-01T01:00:00")` ends at `utc->grid-t`; a `:within 10` wait that cannot hold fails naming the wait; `(:throttle 1.0) (:warp 1000)` fails with "WARP REFUSED: ENGINE ON, LIMIT x10" (the clock''s exact text); `(:keys (:w) :for 2)` from the prograde hold releases to rate damping, pitch rate non-zero after 2 s, then damped; a radial free-fall start (set in the test) then `(:wait :impact)` fails with cause `:crash`, with `(:expect :lost :crash)` next passes, `(:restart) (:expect :start-state)` passes.
- These tests together under 1.5 s (measured, in the done note).

## Docs
- `docs/architecture.md` "Scripted runs": "Actions" gains `(:restart)` (2) and `:view` on `:screenshot`; "Waits and expectations" gains `:before`, `:start-state`, `:impact-as-predicted` and the note that apsis conditions read the prediction''s apsides (`decision 87`); "Running scripts": `run-flight` and its `:model`, and that `--from` and `<name>.end.sexp` come with `saves.lisp` (milestone 5).

## Done when
- `scripts/test` passes (time in the done note); `./sly eval ''(flight-record-lines (run-flight "((:throttle 1.0) (:after 10 :throttle 0) (:expect :apoapsis-above 450e3))"))'' --package LUV-IN-SPACE` prints a passing record; stop what you started.', '2026-10-14T23:05:51Z', NULL, 91, 0);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (135, 22, 'The flight scripts burn, raise-orbit and deorbit, headless and deterministic', 'work', 'open', NULL, NULL, 'Piece 2 of `plan 22: Flight scripts and the Done when` (read it in full: each script''s steps and numbers). Binding: `decision 87: Flight scripts in milestone 2: additions, apsis conditions, live fly and scripts/fly` (the scripts'' numbers, raise-orbit''s 1 % checked two-body, full-model bands 800 ± 8 km widened with a comment if J2 needs it), `decision 38: Start orbit: polar`, `decision 21: Physics test bounds and flight scripts`, `decision 74: Crash test on the step''s Hermite and the death state`, `decision 34: How death works`. Spec: `docs/design.md` "The ship(s)" (RL10C-1 class: 101.8 kN, v_e 4,410 m/s, wet 35,961 kg), "Dying and what follows"; `docs/architecture.md` "Scripted runs", "Warp, events and collisions" (determinism). Needs `run-flight` (the task before it in this plan).

## What
- `flights/burn.sexp`: ×1 from the start state: `(:screenshot "before")`, `(:after 10 :throttle 1.0)`, `(:after 5 :screenshot "during")`, `(:after 5 :throttle 0)` (10 s, ≈ 28.4 m/s), expects apoapsis ≈ 500 km (band ±10 km, measured and set by you), periapsis 380–405 km, propellant 27,769 kg ± 5 kg; `(:screenshot "after" :view :map)`.
- `flights/raise-orbit.sexp`: Hohmann 400 → 800 km under `(:warp :coast)`: `(:screenshot "start" :view :map)`, `(:throttle 1.0)`, `(:after-dv 109.1 :throttle 0)`, expects apoapsis 792–808 km, periapsis 385–405 km, `(:screenshot "transfer" :view :map)`; `(:wait :apoapsis :before 18.5)` (half the second burn, refine from the flight), `(:throttle 1.0)`, `(:after-dv 107.6 :throttle 0)`, expects both apsides 792–808 km (widened with a comment if J2''s wobble needs it, measured), `(:screenshot "raised" :view :map)`, `(:screenshot "raised-outside")`.
- `flights/deorbit.sexp`: `(:hold :retrograde)`, `(:wait :settled :within 120)`, `(:screenshot "retrograde")`, `(:warp :coast)`, `(:throttle 1.0)`, `(:after-dv 102.3 :throttle 0)`, `(:expect :periapsis-below 100e3)`, `(:screenshot "falling" :view :map)`, `(:wait :altitude-below 150e3)`, `(:screenshot "entry")`, `(:wait :impact)`, `(:expect :lost :crash)`, `(:expect :impact-as-predicted 0.1)`, `(:screenshot "lost")`, `(:restart)`, `(:expect :start-state)`, `(:screenshot "restarted")`.
- Each file opens with a comment block: what it shows, the closed-form numbers behind its Δv and bands (μ⊕, R⊕ + 400 km = 6,778,136.6 m, v_e 4,410 m/s), and that `scripts/fly <name> <dir>` renders it.
- `flights/` must ship with the packaged build: check `nix build`''s `result` finds them through `asdf:system-relative-pathname` (adjust `flake.nix` if the source copy omits them).

## Tests (`tests/flights.lisp`)
- Each script headless: no failure, every expectation passes; the deorbit''s death and restart.
- Determinism: each script run twice gives `equalp` end sims and equal records (bit for bit).
- `raise-orbit` with `:model :point` (the test replaces the apsis bands): both final apsides 800 km ± 4 km, the transfer''s apoapsis likewise.
- The deorbit''s impact within 0.1 s of the prediction taken when `(:wait :impact)` began; latitude and longitude in the record.
- All three scripts twice plus the two-body run under 1 s (measured, in the done note); physics tests under ~5 s, `scripts/test` under ~15 s.

## Docs
- `docs/architecture.md` "Scripts by milestone" → `milestone 2`: each script''s steps and numbers as built (the raise''s two-body check, the full-model bands, the deorbit''s restart).

## Done when
- `scripts/test` passes; `./sly eval ''(flight-record-lines (run-flight "deorbit"))'' --package LUV-IN-SPACE` prints the burns, the impact and the restart; stop what you started.', '2026-10-14T23:07:01Z', NULL, 91, 0);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (136, 22, 'Live fly: the flight in the game, flight-status, flight-stop and input gating', 'work', 'open', NULL, NULL, 'Piece 3, first part, of `plan 22: Flight scripts and the Done when` (read its "Rules for every piece" and piece 3). Binding: `decision 87: Flight scripts in milestone 2: additions, apsis conditions, live fly and scripts/fly` (live `fly`, keys ignored while a flight runs), `decision 81: Input in milestone 2: held keys per frame, taps, gating and the shared key map`, `decision 86: Death view, LOST panel and restart in milestone 2` (`game-restart!`), `decision 72: Simulation state and the one step function in clock.lisp`. Spec: `docs/architecture.md` "Running scripts" (headless and live take the same states bit for bit), "Dev loop". Needs `run-flight` and the three scripts (earlier tasks of this plan).

## What
- `src/flights.lisp`: `(fly name &key (game *game*))`: restores the game to the script''s start state as `game-restart!` does, sets the view outside, keeps the `flight` in a new `flight` slot of `game` (`:initform nil`), installs the clock hook (a lambda calling `flight-step-hook`), returns at once. The script runs frame by frame through `clock-frame!` → `clock-step!`, the same steps as headless. At its end or a failure the hook is removed and the record lines printed to the image''s output. `(flight-status &key (game *game*))` → the record lines so far; `(flight-stop &key (game *game*))` removes the hook and clears the slot. `(:restart)` live calls `game-restart!`. No HAL or SDL calls in `flights.lisp`.
- `src/input.lisp`: while the game has a running flight, `input-frame!` writes no held keys and runs no throttle ramp; flight keys and warp keys are ignored (as when lost); P, C, Home, M, Tab, Page keys, the mouse and quit act.

## Tests
- `tests/flights.lisp` (CPU): `burn` flown through `clock-frame!` with a game-less clock fed simulated frames of 1/60 s, and again with a fixed list of irregular elapsed times, gives the same end sim (`equalp`) and record as `run-flight`.
- `tests/input.lisp`: with a flight running, W and Shift change no control; M still toggles the view.

## Docs
- `AGENTS.md` "Commands" → "Live image": `./sly eval ''(fly "raise-orbit")'' --package LUV-IN-SPACE`, `(flight-status)`, `(flight-stop)`, flight and warp keys ignored while a flight runs. `docs/architecture.md` "Running scripts": live `fly` as built.

## Done when
- `scripts/test` passes; in `./sly start` / `./sly play`, `./sly eval ''(fly "burn")'' --package LUV-IN-SPACE` and a `./sly screenshot` during the burn show the engine on and `THR 100% ON`, and `(flight-status)` prints the record at the end; `./sly stop`, no window left open.', '2026-10-14T23:07:01Z', NULL, 91, 0);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (137, 22, 'scripts/fly: fly-screenshots and fly-main, one PNG per checkpoint', 'work', 'open', NULL, NULL, 'Piece 3, second part, of `plan 22: Flight scripts and the Done when` (read piece 3). Binding: `decision 87: Flight scripts in milestone 2: additions, apsis conditions, live fly and scripts/fly` (what `scripts/fly` writes in milestone 2; screenshots not committed), `decision 64: Scripts always re-enter the dev shell`, `decision 10: Test and screenshot commands`, `decision 77: The frame in main.lisp: render state, capture of a given sim, part 4''s placeholders and first asset` (`capture-state` of a given sim), `decision 86: Death view, LOST panel and restart in milestone 2` (`:death-elapsed`). Needs `run-flight` and the three scripts (earlier tasks of this plan); independent of live `fly`.

## What
- `src/capture.lisp`: `(fly-screenshots record out-dir)`: inside one `with-capture-game`, each `:screenshot` checkpoint''s sim rendered by `capture-state` with its `:view`, the default outside pose, `:readout`, `:death-elapsed` 0 for a lost sim, written as `<out-dir>/<nn>-<label>.png` (`nn` two digits from 01); creates `out-dir`. `(fly-main name out-dir)`: `run-flight`, `flight-record-lines` on stdout, the PNGs; exit 1 on a failure or a missing script.
- `scripts/fly` (new, like `scripts/screenshot`): re-enters the dev shell unless `LIS_DEV_SHELL=1`; usage `scripts/fly <name> <out-dir>`, exit 2 on bad arguments; loads `luv-in-space`, calls `fly-main`; headless on lavapipe without a display. `flights.lisp` itself stays free of HAL/SDL.

## Tests
- `tests/capture.lisp` (GPU, inside the existing `with-capture-game`): `fly-screenshots` of `burn`''s record writes three PNGs of 1280 × 800 with the PNG signature and no fully black frame, into a temporary directory deleted after. `scripts/test` stays under ~15 s.

## Docs
- `AGENTS.md` "Commands": `scripts/fly <name> <out-dir>` (what it writes, exit codes 0/1/2, the time measured); "Layout": `flights/` and `scripts/fly`. `docs/architecture.md` "Running scripts" as built.

## Done when
- `scripts/test` passes; `scripts/fly raise-orbit /tmp/fly-raise` writes five PNGs and exits 0; look at them (the ring, the transfer ellipse with AP near 800 km, the raised ring, the ship outside), then delete the directory; `scripts/fly nosuch /tmp/x` exits 1, `scripts/fly` alone exits 2.', '2026-10-14T23:07:02Z', NULL, 91, 0);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (138, 22, 'Milestone 2 Done when: check from a clean checkout', 'work', 'open', NULL, NULL, 'Piece 4 of `plan 22: Flight scripts and the Done when` (read it in full: it is the checklist). Not code: the Done when of `milestone 2: Flight core: Newtonian flight in near-Earth orbit` checked by an agent, per `docs/design.md` "Each milestone against its Outcome and Done when". Binding: `decision 87: Flight scripts in milestone 2: additions, apsis conditions, live fly and scripts/fly` (screenshots not committed), `decision 64: Scripts always re-enter the dev shell`. Runs after every other task of milestone 2 (`pm status`: if any milestone-2 task is still open, it is not ready).

## Checks
- Clean checkout: `git clone` this repo into a temporary directory (no `build/`, no `result`); there `nix build` (time), `scripts/test` (passes, under ~15 s, time), `nix flake check` (passes), `scripts/fly burn`, `raise-orbit`, `deorbit` into a temporary directory (exit 0; look at every PNG: ship and Earth outside, the map''s ring and ellipses, the impact marker and red arc, the glow and `LOST: CRASH`, the restarted start), `scripts/screenshot` (the start state with the readout).
- Live: `./sly start`, `./sly play`, `./sly eval ''(fly "raise-orbit")'' --package LUV-IN-SPACE` with `./sly screenshot`s at the first burn and after the raise; `(fly "deorbit")` to the LOST panel, a synthetic Return and a screenshot of the start state; `./sly stop`. `nix run` in the background, closed by its PID (`kill $!`) after a few seconds: the window opens without error.
- By hand: in `./sly play`, synthetic key events through `./sly eval` (prograde hold, Shift to raise the throttle, X to cut, warp up and down, M and Tab), each followed by a `./sly screenshot`; the readout''s values move as expected.
- Docs against what was built: `AGENTS.md` "Commands" (every command runs as written, times true) and "Layout" (every file and directory; the `src/` bullet no longer says walking skeleton or `PLACEHOLDER(23)`); `docs/architecture.md` "Files in load order" against `luv-in-space.asd` and `src/`, "Testing" against `tests/` (each named test exists or its row names the milestone writing it), "Scripted runs" against `flights.lisp`; `docs/design.md` "Feature list": every milestone-2 must row built, each should row built or noted not built with a follow-up task; `rg -n ''PLACEHOLDER\('' src tests` lists only tasks of later milestones (each id checked with `pm show`); `docs/luv-capabilities.md` and `docs/luv-wishlist.md` list the luv gaps met in milestone 2.
- Small doc mismatches: fix them in this task. Anything else that fails: a follow-up task (`pm task add` in the plan of the part that owns it); this task stays open until those are done, or is dropped with the reason.

## Done when
- Every check passes, or each failure has a follow-up task named in the done note; the done note lists the commands and the times measured and is the milestone''s evidence. Screenshots not committed; temporary clones and directories deleted; no process left running.', '2026-10-14T23:07:02Z', NULL, 91, 0);

INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (1, 'How the game consumes luv', 'answered', '[]', 'Findings of `task 17: Build and run luv''s programs and test suite; record the runs` (docs/luv-capabilities.md, Packaging and Runs).
- luv''s flake exports apps, devShells, packages and lib; lib has only environmentFor, which returns everything the packaging needs (pkgs, lisp, applicationEnvironment/Packages, developmentEnvironmentHook, slynkSource, swashPackage, …). No overlays.
- applicationPackage (../luv/flake.nix:637) is not an output and is hard-wired to src = self, so it is copied (~120 lines) with src = this repo; noted for docs/luv-wishlist.md (export a lib.applicationPackage taking src).
- Do not set nixpkgs.follows: luv''s own pkgs keep the dependency closure identical to what luv.swa.sh/nix-cache serves (the dev shell''s Mesa, Vulkan tools, ffmpeg etc. came from it); the cache holds no x86_64-linux luv outputs at this revision, so the Lisp images are built locally (~40 s each).
- Pin the same revision as docs/luv-capabilities.md; bump deliberately, rerunning luv''s make test.
- Inherited LD_LIBRARY_PATH from the host broke Luvcraft (host alsa-lib needing glibc 2.43); our launcher and scripts unset it.
Part 3 (architecture) may supersede this.', 'Flake input github:mbrock/luv pinned to 6abc9d10ec8991c2cfe92888b84946372d6030b9; dev shell and app built from luv.lib.environmentFor (pkgs, lisp, developmentEnvironmentHook, applicationEnvironment) with a copy of luv''s applicationPackage recipe adapted here; luv''s sources on CL_SOURCE_REGISTRY from the input; luv''s binary cache in nixConfig; launchers unset LD_LIBRARY_PATH.', NULL, '2026-10-14T16:33:38Z', '2026-10-14T16:33:38Z', 17, NULL, 'rule', 17, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (2, 'Screenshots and GPU test: offscreen capture on a hidden canvas, headless on lavapipe', 'answered', '[]', 'Found by task 18 (docs/luv-capabilities.md, runs 24–28): a probe outside luv captured correct PNGs in a window, on a hidden canvas, with no DISPLAY/WAYLAND_DISPLAY (offscreen + lavapipe), and inside the nix build sandbox (no /dev/dri) in 21 s including compiling luv; Luvcraft''s full 3D frame also renders headless on lavapipe. Hidden canvases keep the requested size; visible windows get the compositor''s size, so tests should not use them. Binds task 24 (screenshot command) and task 25 (headless test); the architecture may supersede it.', 'Screenshots and the GPU test use luv''s capture-application-screenshot on a hidden SDL canvas (:visible-p nil); without a display the luv shell hook picks SDL''s offscreen driver and Mesa lavapipe; the GPU test runs as a nix flake check in the sandbox, built with lib.environmentFor''s slim development environment and hook.', NULL, '2026-10-14T16:42:58Z', '2026-10-14T16:42:58Z', 18, NULL, 'rule', 18, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (3, 'luv ASDF systems the game depends on', 'answered', '[]', 'The sphere probe of `task 19: Probe program: a lit sphere in a luv window plus a screenshot` (docs/luv-capabilities.md, "Appendix: probe program", runs 30–31) opened a window, drew a lit UV sphere with shaders in luv''s shader language, used a depth buffer and took an offscreen screenshot, visible and headless, with `:depends-on ("luv")` alone. Shader files use a package with (:use #:cl #:luv.shader) and (:shadowing-import-from #:luv.shader #:step). luvcraft/core is not loadable as a library (it pulls in Ghostty, libav and the whole game), so its sky, bloom and world-text code is copied and adapted here. luv/ghostty needs the full dev shell, not slim, which the GPU test (`decision 2: Screenshots and GPU test: offscreen capture on a hidden canvas, headless on lavapipe`) uses; adding it later is a separate choice.', 'The game system depends on "luv" only (it brings luv/shader, the SPIR-V/MSL lowerings and luv/arithmetic); tests add "luv/test-support". luv/ghostty, luv/terminal, luv/production and luvcraft/* are not dependencies until a feature needs one, and then each is recorded.', NULL, '2026-10-14T16:48:14Z', '2026-10-14T16:48:14Z', 19, NULL, 'rule', 19, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (4, 'Depth strategy', 'answered', '[]', 'Findings of task 20: Assess luv''s rendering gaps for solar-system scale, each with a workaround (docs/luv-capabilities.md, "Gaps and workarounds"). luv offers only depth32-float but any compare op and clear value. The standard projection lost Earth''s far side from orbit and drew nothing at the Moon''s distance; infinite reversed-Z with near 0.1 m drew both, and a textured Earth impostor, an orbit ribbon and stars in one pass (runs 30-33). Logarithmic depth via frag-depth costs early-Z for no gain; layered passes cost a pass per layer. A separate cockpit pass with its own depth clear stays the fallback. docs/architecture.md may supersede this.', 'Reversed-Z: depth32-float, an infinite far plane (depth = near/z, near 0.1 m), compare :greater, clear 0.0, in every pass; stars at infinity drawn first with compare :always and no depth write; planets as ray-traced impostors writing their depth through :frag-depth.', NULL, '2026-10-14T16:56:15Z', '2026-10-14T16:56:15Z', 20, NULL, 'rule', 20, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (5, 'Precision of positions', 'answered', '[]', 'Findings of task 20: Assess luv''s rendering gaps for solar-system scale, each with a workaround (docs/luv-capabilities.md, "Gaps and workarounds"). luv''s shaders have no 64-bit floats and write-buffer takes single-float arrays; base luv has no camera, and luv.arithmetic''s vec3 holds any real. Single floats give ~0.5 m at Earth''s radius and ~60 km at 1 AU. The sphere and gap probes computed the camera in double and sent camera-relative singles; Earth from 629 km and 34 Mm and the Moon from 384,400 km rendered correctly (runs 30-33). docs/architecture.md may supersede this, e.g. with per-body frames for the integrator.', 'World state in double-float on the CPU; each frame positions are made camera-relative in double and only then converted to single-float for the GPU; no world-space positions or matrices reach shaders; quantities that cancel in single float (e.g. |c|^2 - r^2 of a ray-sphere test) are computed in double on the CPU.', NULL, '2026-10-14T16:56:15Z', '2026-10-14T16:56:15Z', 20, NULL, 'rule', 20, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (6, 'Target platforms', 'answered', '[]', 'luv builds for exactly these three systems and has no Windows backend (docs/luv-capabilities.md, Platforms). Only x86_64-linux was available to run luv''s builds, tests, programs and probes (runs 1-33); the developer''s luv wiki measures on Apple Silicon, so aarch64-darwin is a target but untested here. Luft''s mesh-shader requirement failed on the Intel iGPU (runs 7, 17), so the game avoids mesh and task shaders. Clip-space y and other backend differences are kept in one projection function so a Metal check later touches one place.', 'Targets: x86_64-linux and aarch64-linux (Vulkan 1.4) and aarch64-darwin (Metal 4), the systems luv''s flake supports; no Windows. Tested: x86_64-linux only (this machine, Intel iGPU, NVIDIA and lavapipe). The others are evaluated (nix flake show/eval) until a machine runs them. No mesh or task shaders, so any Vulkan 1.4 iGPU works.', NULL, '2026-10-14T16:58:45Z', '2026-10-14T16:58:45Z', 21, NULL, 'rule', 21, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (7, 'Placeholder labels: dot-matrix overlay quads', 'answered', '[]', 'luv has no text layer a game can load without luvcraft/core (docs/luv-capabilities.md, HUD and instruments); the Slug copy costs ~400 lines, a dot-matrix overlay ~40 and reuses the triangle-list pipeline that also draws the placeholder stars. Added by task 23: Placeholder scene: lit Earth sphere and a PLACEHOLDER label.', 'On-screen PLACEHOLDER labels are 5x7 dot-matrix glyphs drawn as screen-space quads by the overlay pipeline in src/scene.lisp (glyph table *glyphs*), not Slug text; real text (instruments, HUD) waits for the Slug text copy the design plans.', NULL, '2026-10-14T17:15:05Z', '2026-10-14T17:15:05Z', 23, NULL, 'rule', 23, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (8, 'Live image: ./sly wraps luv''s client from the flake input', 'answered', '[]', 'luv''s ./sly serves only its own checkout and games (docs/luv-capabilities.md, live image). Redefining five functions on top of luv''s 2,400-line client keeps its other commands (eval, failures, resume, describe, xref, …) current with the luv pin, instead of a copy that drifts. Verified end to end in run 34 by task 26: Live image dev loop for luv-in-space.', './sly at the repo root builds build/sly-client from luv''s sly-client system (flake input) with play, screenshot, stop-playing, status and the dependency core redefined in scripts/sly-client.lisp; sly-server.lisp is luv''s, adapted to this root and the luv-in-space system.', NULL, '2026-10-14T17:23:33Z', '2026-10-14T17:23:33Z', 26, NULL, 'rule', 26, '2026-10-14, task 27: Verified from a clean clone by `task 27: AGENTS.md Commands, verified from a clean clone; skeleton decisions recorded`: ./sly start (~5 s), play, status, screenshot, eval, failures, stop-playing, stop all work; AGENTS.md Commands documents them.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (9, 'Layout of systems and directories', 'answered', '[]', 'Follows `decision 3: luv ASDF systems the game depends on` (luv only; tests add luv/test-support) and `decision 1: How the game consumes luv` (luv''s sources from the flake input on CL_SOURCE_REGISTRY, so the root .asd is found from the repo root). One .asd keeps the game and its tests together as luv''s own programs do; new modules go under src/ and are listed in the .asd. Verified from a clean clone by `task 27: AGENTS.md Commands, verified from a clean clone; skeleton decisions recorded`.', 'luv-in-space.asd at the root with systems luv-in-space (src/) and luv-in-space/tests (tests/, Parachute); scripts/ holds the agent commands (test, screenshot, build.lisp, the sly client); ./sly and sly-server.lisp at the root; docs/ the design documents.', NULL, '2026-10-14T17:25:48Z', '2026-10-14T17:25:48Z', 27, NULL, 'rule', 27, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (10, 'Test and screenshot commands', 'answered', '[]', 'Relies on `decision 2: Screenshots and GPU test: offscreen capture on a hidden canvas, headless on lavapipe`. No window opens, so an agent needs no display and leaves no process running. Ran from a clean clone (`task 27: AGENTS.md Commands, verified from a clean clone; skeleton decisions recorded`): tests 18/18 in ~5 s, screenshot ~3 s, with and without DISPLAY. The live image (`./sly`) is the interactive dev loop, `decision 8: Live image: ./sly wraps luv''s client from the flake input`.', 'scripts/test runs luv-in-space/tests (exit 1 on failure); scripts/screenshot <out.png> [--frames N] writes a 1280x800 PNG and exits; both offscreen on a hidden canvas, headless on lavapipe without a display, and enter the dev shell themselves; nix flake check runs scripts/test in the sandbox.', NULL, '2026-10-14T17:25:48Z', '2026-10-14T17:25:48Z', 27, NULL, 'rule', 27, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (11, 'Render pipelines are luv live shader pipelines', 'answered', '[]', 'So a shader re-evaluated in ./sly shows on the next frame and a failed build keeps the last good pipeline, as in Luft and Luvcraft; verified by task 30: Live shader pipelines for the scene (red oceans live).', 'Every render pipeline of the game is a luv live shader pipeline (make-live-shader-pipeline) whose role is a keyword with shader-specification-for methods returning the define-shader functions in src/shaders.lisp; the game returns them from luv:application-live-artifacts and calls luv:refresh-application-live-artifacts at the top of each frame.', NULL, '2026-10-14T17:30:44Z', '2026-10-14T17:30:44Z', 30, NULL, 'rule', 30, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (12, 'Module layout and package split', 'answered', '[]', 'One game package lets ./sly eval --package LUV-IN-SPACE reach every function while redefining live, with no export lists to keep in step; the code stays a few thousand lines, so clashes are handled with name prefixes. Shaders stay apart because luv.shader''s operators clash with CL. Keeping simulation free of HAL/SDL lets physics tests run without a canvas. A file that needs its own package is a new decision. Extends decision 9: Layout of systems and directories.', 'One ASDF system with flat files in src/ loaded serially in the order of docs/architecture.md "Module layout" (simulation files math … saves first and free of HAL/SDL calls, then shaders, assets and rendering files, then input, audio, title, main, capture); two packages: luv-in-space for all game code, luv-in-space.shaders for every define-shader; tests in luv-in-space.tests, one tests/<module>.lisp per module with logic.', NULL, '2026-10-14T17:39:07Z', '2026-10-14T17:39:07Z', 32, NULL, 'rule', 32, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (13, 'Asset storage and attribution', 'answered', '[]', 'The two first assets are small (1.8 MB texture, a few hundred KB of stars), and committing them keeps the build independent of NASA and HYG URLs moving. A size cap keeps the repo cloneable; larger textures for close views come through Nix when the design wants them. ShareAlike binds the derived star table only, so it lives in its own directory with its licence. The package deletes its source copy after the dump, hence an installed assets directory and an environment variable.', 'Runtime assets committed under assets/<kind>/ (each file <= 5 MB, assets/ <= 25 MB), each kind with a SOURCE.md (URL, date, licence, credit, derivation); larger files via Nix fetchurl with a pinned hash, never committed, with the committed version as fallback. Earth: Blue Marble NG 5400x2700 JPEG, credited ''NASA Earth Observatory (Blue Marble Next Generation)'', no NASA logo. Stars: HYG filtered to mag <= 6.5 as assets/stars/hyg-6.5.sexp, made by scripts/convert-stars, under CC BY-SA 4.0 with assets/stars/LICENSE. CREDITS.md at the root collects all credits and the game shows them. The package installs assets/ to share/luv-in-space/assets and the launcher exports LUV_IN_SPACE_ASSETS; the dev shell falls back to the system-relative assets/.', NULL, '2026-10-14T17:39:07Z', '2026-10-14T17:39:07Z', 32, NULL, 'rule', 32, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (14, 'Units and time epoch', 'answered', '[]', 'Doubles have the same relative precision in any unit, so km or AU gain nothing and add conversions: at 1 AU a double resolves ~3e-5 m, at 30 AU ~1e-3 m. SI keeps rocket and orbit formulas plain. J2000 matches JPL Horizons, DE440 and the IAU rotation models, so spot checks need no epoch shift; double seconds resolve ~1.2e-7 s at 30 years from J2000. Power-of-two steps make t + dt exact, so the clock does not drift and runs are deterministic. TDB - TT (< 2 ms) and future leap seconds are ignored.', 'SI throughout (m, kg, s, N, rad) as double-float, display units only at the instruments; constants as mu = GM in m^3/s^2 from JPL DE440 (Park et al. 2021) and radii from IAU 2015 B3 / IAU WGCCRE 2015; simulation time as double-float TDB seconds since J2000 (2000-01-01 12:00 TT), UT1 = UTC = TT - 69.184 s for display and Earth''s rotation; physics steps are power-of-two fractions of a second; the game''s start date is a calendar constant in src/units.lisp, chosen by docs/story.md (default 2040-01-01 00:00 UTC).', NULL, '2026-10-14T17:43:19Z', '2026-10-14T17:43:19Z', 33, NULL, 'rule', 33, '2026-10-14, task 51: 2026-10-14, task 51: docs/story.md keeps the start date 2040-01-01 00:00 UTC (decision 50: Story: start date and the passing rock''s date): the best lunar window of 2040 (Moon at Highport''s apogee 2040-01-26) comes ~20 game days after the start; docs/design.md ''The start'' and docs/architecture.md ''Time'' no longer call it a default. src/units.lisp does not exist yet; milestone 2 writes *start-date* with this value.

2026-10-14, task 93: 2026-10-14, task 93: the fixed TT - UTC = 69.184 s applies to past dates too, so J2000.0 is 11:58:50.816 in the game''s UTC (real UTC 11:58:55.816, TT - UTC 64.184 s in 2000); tests/units.lisp and docs/architecture.md ''Time'' use the game''s value.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (15, 'Coordinate frames', 'answered', '[]', 'Shared axes make re-centring a translation only and keep attitudes unchanged across frames. A body-centred frame keeps the ship''s coordinates small (LEO in an Earth frame resolves ~1e-9 m, against ~3e-5 m heliocentric), so roundoff stays below the integrator''s error and the drift tests of plan 9 measure the integrator; elements and instruments read straight from it. The frame''s acceleration enters as the indirect term of third bodies, so the trajectory does not depend on the frame. Ignoring precession and nutation puts Earth''s pole up to ~0.2 deg off by the 2040s: harmless for a game. FRD is the flight-dynamics convention: positive pitch is nose up, positive yaw nose right.', 'All inertial frames share ICRF (EME2000, J2000 equator and equinox) axes, no precession or nutation; the ship is integrated in the inertial frame centred on its dominant body (Sun, Earth, Moon; planets from milestone 4), the innermost body whose Laplace sphere of influence a (m/M)^(2/5) contains it, re-centred at a step boundary with 2% hysteresis; body-fixed frames from IAU WGCCRE 2015 rotation models, Earth''s by the Earth Rotation Angle about ICRF z with x at the prime meridian (the Blue Marble texture''s centre column); ship body and camera axes x forward, y right, z down (FRD), attitude a unit quaternion body -> inertial.', NULL, '2026-10-14T17:43:19Z', '2026-10-14T17:43:19Z', 33, NULL, 'rule', 33, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (16, 'Integrator and simulation clock', 'answered', '[]', 'Measured in /tmp with SBCL in the dev shell (task 34): RK4 at h = 2 s in a 400 km circular orbit keeps relative energy error at 1e-11 and position error at 0.36 m after 1000 orbits; velocity Verlet at 1 s conserves energy (2e-13) but drifts 18 km in phase, and loses its energy bound with a state-dependent step (2.4e-6 at e = 0.5). Phase error is what rendezvous, prediction and the autopilot see, so 4th order beats symplectic here; RK4 also takes thrust, mass flow and attitude in one state vector, with no separable-Hamiltonian assumption. The state-dependent step (eta = 1/256) gives ~2,800 steps per orbit at any eccentricity and distance, at ~180 ns per step with the full force model, so even x100,000 in LEO fits the per-frame cap. 1/64 s at low warp gives input latency below one frame and resolves RCS pulses and rotation. Power-of-two steps keep t exact (decision 14: Units and time epoch).', 'Classical fixed-step RK4 on the ship''s whole state (position, velocity, attitude quaternion, body angular velocity, propellant mass), controls held over a step; step 1/64 s at warp x1 and x4 and while thrusting with manual attitude; otherwise h = eta * min over gravitating bodies of sqrt(d^3/mu), eta = 1/256, rounded down to a power of two in [1/64 s, 16384 s] (2 s in LEO); scheduled events on the 1/64 s grid; the frame adds min(elapsed, 0.1 s) x warp to a double accumulator and runs at most 4096 steps per frame (excess dropped: the sim slows, the readout shows the actual rate); rendering lags one step and interpolates (cubic Hermite positions, slerp attitude).', NULL, '2026-10-14T17:50:44Z', '2026-10-14T17:50:44Z', 34, NULL, 'rule', 34, '2026-10-14, task 34: 2026-10-14, task 34: docs/architecture.md ''Time and integration'' adds two limits: under thrust above x4 the coarse step is also capped at 1 s, and the per-frame cap is 4096 steps or 4 ms of steps, whichever comes first.

2026-10-14, task 45: 2026-10-14, task 45: docs/architecture.md ''Integrator'' and ''Drift bounds'': Euler''s equations take the full symmetric inertia tensor in body axes (and its inverse) from ship.lisp, not principal axes; the rotation drift test adds a non-diagonal case (diag(1, 2, 3)x10^4 kg m^2 rotated 10 deg about y), same bounds. A refinement, not a change.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (17, 'Gravity model', 'answered', '[]', 'Integrating everything the same way makes the prediction, the autopilot''s refined burns and the flown trajectory one computation, and removes the rails/integrated jump for the ship. Third bodies cost ~40 ns each per evaluation; the Moon''s pull before its sphere of influence and Earth''s just outside its own (8% of the Sun''s) are large, which patched conics would drop. J2 is the largest perturbation in LEO (~1e-2 m/s^2, nodal regression ~5 deg/day at 51.6 deg), visible to a player who waits a day, and costs a few multiplications; the Moon''s J2 and higher terms are invisible at game scale. Drag needs an atmosphere model and only matters below ~300 km; the design may add it later as a force.', 'The ship is integrated n-body against on-rails bodies in its dominant body''s frame (decision 15: Coordinate frames): the central body plus third bodies as point masses with their indirect terms (Earth frame: Sun, Moon; Moon frame: Earth, Sun; Sun frame: Earth, Moon and, from milestone 4, the planets; planet frames: Sun); Earth''s J2 (1.08263e-3, EGM2008) is in, other bodies'' oblateness out; no drag. The ship is never on Kepler rails; patched conics and Kepler elements serve only display (osculating elements), autopilot first guesses and objects far from the ship.', NULL, '2026-10-14T17:50:44Z', '2026-10-14T17:50:44Z', 34, NULL, 'rule', 34, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (18, 'Ephemerides', 'answered', '[]', 'Both models are a few hundred lines of published coefficients with no data files, accurate to tens of arcseconds (thousands of km for planets, ~20 km for the Moon): far better than a player can see, and the game is self-consistent since autopilot, prediction and flight use the same ephemerides. A DE440 file would be exact but adds an asset and a reader for no visible gain. The Hermite cache makes a lookup cheap (the integrator asks four times per step), keeps the Moon''s interpolation error below 2 cm, and gives the same values whatever order frames ask in (determinism). Table 1 ends in 2050; the story starts in 2040 (default in docs/architecture.md), so the design keeps game time within the decade or a task switches to Table 2.', 'Sun fixed at the heliocentric origin; planets and the Earth-Moon barycentre from Standish, ''Keplerian Elements for Approximate Positions of the Major Planets'' (JPL SSD), Table 1 (J2000 ecliptic elements with secular rates, valid 1800-2050), rotated to ICRF by the obliquity; the Moon from the truncated ELP-2000/82 series in Meeus, ''Astronomical Algorithms'' (2nd ed., ch. 47), precessed to the J2000 ecliptic; Earth = barycentre - mu_Moon/(mu_Earth + mu_Moon) x geocentric Moon; all bodies read through a cache of cubic Hermite nodes (4096 s for Earth and Moon, 65536 s for the planets), each node a pure function of its index. Game time stays before 2050-01-01; beyond it Table 2a/2b replaces Table 1.', NULL, '2026-10-14T17:50:44Z', '2026-10-14T17:50:44Z', 34, NULL, 'rule', 34, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (19, 'Time-warp rules', 'answered', '[]', 'Warp is a rendering of the same integration, not another model: the step rule makes the cost of each level follow from the state (x100,000 fits in LEO at 4096 steps per frame), so no altitude table is needed for accuracy. Kinematic attitude above x4 avoids integrating fast rotations with long steps, the classic cause of warp instabilities. Thrust up to x1,000 serves long low-thrust burns of tugs. Drop-outs come from the prediction''s events, so the player is never carried past an impact or a planned burn. Promotion keeps a station and a ship nearby under the same forces (a rails station ignoring J2 and the Moon would drift metres per hour against the ship), and the switch is exact at its instant since elements reproduce the state.', 'Levels x1, x4, x10, x100, x1,000, x10,000, x100,000, x1,000,000. x1 and x4 run the fine 1/64 s step with full rotational dynamics; above x4 attitude is kinematic (held inertial or in the orbital frame, rotation rates below 0.01 rad/s or a hold required to enter) and manual RCS is off; thrust is allowed up to x1,000 under attitude hold (the design may lower it). The clock drops warp so that the next predicted event (impact or periapsis below the crash radius, sphere-of-influence change, scheduled burn minus its lead time, entering a Sun hazard zone) arrives no sooner than 3 s real time, and to x1 at the event. Levels beyond the step budget may be chosen; the readout shows the actual rate. Rails: only objects other than the ship (stations, asteroids, other ships) move on Kepler rails (with J2 secular rates around Earth); one within 100 km of the ship is promoted to integration with the ship''s force model via elements -> state at the current t, and demoted beyond 200 km via state -> osculating elements.', NULL, '2026-10-14T17:50:44Z', '2026-10-14T17:50:44Z', 34, NULL, 'rule', 34, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (20, 'Trajectory prediction on the frame thread', 'answered', '[]', 'Measured ~180 ns per step with the full force model: one LEO orbit (~2,800 steps) costs ~0.5 ms, a translunar coast a few thousand steps, so a whole prediction fits in a few frames'' budgets. On the frame thread it is deterministic, needs no locking over the ship''s state and is live-redefinable like everything else; a worker adds a thread and a dependency for no measured need. Revisit (and record luv/production) if a profile shows prediction over 2 ms per frame, e.g. many promoted objects or the autopilot''s shooting iterations.', 'Trajectory prediction runs on the frame thread, incrementally within a budget of 1 ms per frame, with the same integrator, force model and step rule as the flight (coarse steps, planned burns as scheduled controls); horizon until the first of: one osculating period (two for the map), an SOI change plus one period in the new body, an impact, 30 days in a planet''s sphere or 2 years heliocentric, 20,000 steps; every step''s state is kept (double, body-relative) for ribbons and event search. luv/production is not a dependency; decision 3: luv ASDF systems the game depends on stands.', NULL, '2026-10-14T17:50:44Z', '2026-10-14T17:50:44Z', 34, NULL, 'rule', 34, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (21, 'Physics test bounds and flight scripts', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/architecture.md ''Testing'' (task 35). Bounds come from the measured drift in ''Time and integration'' with a margin, sized so physics tests stay under ~5 s. Data scripts keyed by simulation time keep runs deterministic and replayable, and go through the same controls as input and the autopilot, so an agent can show a burn, an orbit raise and a deorbit with screenshots from a clean checkout.', 'Drift tests run two-body through the integrator and through the clock at every warp level, with bounds ~10x the measured drift: fine 1/64 s step (circular 10 orbits, e=0.5 2 orbits) dE/E and dL/L < 1e-11, position < 0.05 m; coarse step over 1,000 orbits circular < 1e-10 / 5 m, e=0.5 < 1e-9 / 100 m, and e=0.9 over 200 orbits dE/E < 1e-8, dL/L < 1e-9, < 5 km; the clock''s states equal the direct run bit for bit at every level and frame rate. Flights are driven by data scripts flights/<name>.sexp (actions and waits on the 1/64 s grid, writing controls) run by src/flights.lisp headless or live (fly); scripts/fly <name> <out-dir> writes a PNG per :screenshot checkpoint and exits 1 on a failed :expect; milestone 2 ships burn, raise-orbit and deorbit and demonstrates its Done when with them. GPU tests stay image checks on one frame (decision 2, decision 10).', NULL, '2026-10-14T17:55:32Z', '2026-10-14T17:55:32Z', 35, NULL, 'rule', 35, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (22, 'The player''s ship and its propulsion', 'answered', '[]', 'Hydrolox gives the delta-v for LEO to low lunar orbit (3.9 km/s) or a planetary departure on one tank, and it ties the ship to a water-to-propellant depot economy that gives tugs their work (moving water-bearing rock). One propellant pool keeps decision 16''s single propellant mass. Figures, formulas and sources in docs/design.md ''The ship(s)''. Recorded by task 38: Design: the game in one page and the ship(s).', 'A Mule-class tug: welded steel truss 15.0 x 3.6 x 3.6 m (2,131 kg), crew cab on top at the front (2,466 kg, crew 1 plus a jump seat, 180 person-days of consumables), grapple at the nose, LH2 and LOX tanks inside the truss; one RL10C-1-class hydrolox engine (101.8 kN, Isp 449.7 s, MR 5.5, throttle 10-100 %, gimbal trimmed through the centre of mass, unlimited restarts); 24 gaseous O2/H2 RCS thrusters of 445 N, Isp 360 s, in 8 corner blocks, fed from the same tanks (one propellant pool, as decision 16: Integrator and simulation clock integrates it); dry 7,961 kg, propellant 28,000 kg, wet 35,961 kg; delta-v 6.65 km/s empty, 1.74 km/s with a 50 t load, 1.02 km/s with 100 t; inertia as the full tensor from the parts in docs/design.md. Power, boil-off, ullage settling and gimbal cosine loss are not simulated.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:05:56Z', '2026-10-14T18:05:56Z', 38, NULL, 'rule', 38, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (23, 'Start orbit', 'answered', '[]', 'The orbit of the architecture''s drift tests and of most crewed traffic since the ISS; the delta-v table in docs/design.md is computed from it. RAAN and phase are invented; the story (docs/story.md) or the places (task 42: Design: the places, with real data and Δv checked against the ship) may change them and must keep the start sunlit. Recorded by task 38.', '400 km circular (a = 6,778,136.6 m), i = 51.6 deg, RAAN 0, argument of latitude 0 at the start date (decision 14: Units and time epoch, default 2040-01-01 00:00 UTC), tanks full, attitude prograde hold; sunlit at the start.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:05:56Z', '2026-10-14T18:05:56Z', 38, NULL, 'rule', 38, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (24, 'Grapple, not docking; towing in', 'answered', '[]', 'One mechanism serves refuelling, cargo and asteroid work and is flown the same way every time; the pilot never leaves the cab, so docking ports and crew transfer would only cost. Above 100 t delta-v falls below 1 km/s and turning a 90 deg takes over 40 s. How it is flown and its tolerances: docs/design.md ''Flight'' (task 40). Recorded by task 38.', 'The ship has one attachment mechanism, a nose grapple (capture cone with three latching claws) that latches onto a standard grapple fixture on depots, stations, cargo pods and bagged asteroids; no docking port, no crew transfer. A grappled object becomes part of one rigid stack (combined mass, centre of mass and inertia tensor), pushed by the main engine and turned by the RCS; loads are rated to 100 t.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:05:56Z', '2026-10-14T18:05:56Z', 38, NULL, 'rule', 38, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (25, 'Damage model', 'answered', '[]', 'Per-part damage needs effects, UI and repair for something a careful pilot rarely sees; the pillars ''Newton is the game'' and ''small and finished'' in docs/design.md cut it. Recorded by task 38.', 'None: the ship is intact or destroyed. Contacts within the grapple''s tolerance capture, harder ones beyond the crash rule kill (rules in docs/design.md ''Flight'', task 40), and the Sun''s heat kills past its threshold; no part is ever damaged, leaking or repaired.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:05:56Z', '2026-10-14T18:05:56Z', 38, NULL, 'rule', 38, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (26, 'Refuelling and economy', 'answered', '[]', 'An economy needs prices, a UI and balancing, and a linear quest line does not need it. Depots are named by task 42: Design: the places, with real data and Δv checked against the ship. Recorded by task 38.', 'Refuelling at depots only, grappled to the depot''s fixture: a cockpit switch transfers LH2 and LOX at 10 kg/s (full in ~47 min game time, time warp allowed) and restocks consumables. No money or economy: propellant is free, the story''s employer pays.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:05:57Z', '2026-10-14T18:05:57Z', 38, NULL, 'rule', 38, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (27, 'Other ships', 'answered', '[]', 'Traffic shows the economy the story needs at the cost of meshes only; AI pilots and other ships'' burns would cost far more than they show. Recorded by task 38.', 'Other ships are built from the tug''s parts (truss, cab, tanks, engine, grapple fixture) or a capsule from a frustum: other Mule tugs in other liveries, an uncrewed tanker, a crew capsule, a large rock mover; all move on Kepler rails only (decision 19: Time-warp rules), promoted to integration near the ship but never thrusting in view; a story ship that departs switches its elements while beyond 200 km.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:05:57Z', '2026-10-14T18:05:57Z', 38, NULL, 'rule', 38, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (28, 'Web access for task 44: Verify the sourced figures of docs/design.md "The ship(s)"', 'answered', '["Allow WebSearch and WebFetch for pm workers; task 44 runs again", "Drop task 44; the figures stay sourced by name only"]', '`task 44: Verify the sourced figures of docs/design.md "The ship(s)"` must re-read the RL10C-1 data sheet, NASA CECE reports, GO2/GH2 thruster and IVF papers, NIST densities and Al 2219 data online and record URLs and dates read. Its worker (2026-10-14) was denied WebSearch and WebFetch by the session''s permission settings, so no agent can do it. Allow WebSearch and WebFetch for pm workers (e.g. in .claude/settings.json permissions), or drop the task and keep the figures as named-but-unverified sources.', 'Allow WebSearch and WebFetch for pm workers; task 44 runs again', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:09:07Z', '2026-10-14T18:50:04Z', 44, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '2026-10-14, task 44: 2026-10-14, task 44: pm workers ran WebSearch/WebFetch; docs/design.md ''Propulsion'' now gives every ship source with URL and date read; no figure changed, decision 22 stands.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (29, 'Control scheme: key map, control modes, gamepad', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/design.md ''Flight'' (task 39). Physical keys keep the map on any layout; the WASD/QE and IJKL-HN blocks follow the convention players know. Rate damping as the default makes the heavy tug (1.6 deg/s^2 wet, 0.38 loaded) controllable by keys; holds are the flight computer the autopilot and warp need (decision 19: Time-warp rules). The gamepad costs ~80 lines that cannot be tested headless or here, and the keyboard covers every function (docs/luv-capabilities.md ''Gamepad and joystick''), so it is a late extra.', 'Keyboard and mouse on luv''s physical key names: W/S pitch, A/D yaw, Q/E roll, H/N I/K J/L translate, Shift/Ctrl throttle (ramp 50 %/s, 10 % minimum), Z full, X cut, F fine (10 %), T rate damping, R inertial hold, 1-8 orbital and target holds, 9/0 relative prograde/retrograde, ./,/ warp up/down/x1, P pause, V view, M map, C camera frame, Home reset, Tab map focus, [ ] \ target, O/arrows/Enter/Backspace autopilot, G grapple, Esc and Ctrl+Q quit (Esc opens a menu from milestone 6); right-drag looks, wheel zooms, left click presses switches and picks map targets. Modes: direct RCS, rate damping (default; up to 5 deg/s, fine 0.5 deg/s), attitude holds slewing at <= 5 deg/s with 80 % of the torque; translation always direct. Gamepad: milestone 6, could.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:12:48Z', '2026-10-14T18:12:48Z', 39, NULL, 'rule', 39, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (30, 'Views and cameras', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/design.md ''Flight'' (task 39). One renderer (decision 4: Depth strategy, decision 5: Precision of positions) draws all three; the map is the same scene from a far camera rather than a separate 2D chart, so it costs ribbons and markers only (docs/luv-capabilities.md ''Orbit lines and markers''). The eye point follows the cab of decision 22: The player''s ship and its propulsion; docs/design.md ''The cockpit'' (task 41) may move it.', 'Three views: outside (milestone 2; orbit camera around the CoM, 50 deg vertical FOV, distance from 1.2x the stack''s bounding radius to 5 km, in the orbital frame or locked to the ship with C), cockpit (milestone 3; eye at (14.1, 0, -3.6) m FRD from R, 0.9 m behind the 1.2 x 0.6 m window, 70 deg FOV zoomable 20-90 deg, head look yaw +-150 deg, pitch -60..+80 deg), map (milestone 2 Earth and osculating orbit; 3 prediction ribbon, targets, events; 4 Moon, Sun, planets): true scale, focus ship/body/target, camera 50 m or 1.2 R to 1.5e13 m, x1.2 per wheel notch, x10 per Page key; bodies below 6 px become labelled markers. All views available at every warp level and during the autopilot.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:12:48Z', '2026-10-14T18:12:48Z', 39, NULL, 'rule', 39, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (31, 'Warp choices within the time-warp rules', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/design.md ''Flight'' ''Time warp'' (task 39), within decision 19: Time-warp rules. One 60 Hz frame at x100 is 1.67 s of thrust, 4.7 m/s wet and 21 m/s dry, too coarse for a cut by key; at x10 0.5-2 m/s. A scheduled cut lands on the 1/64 s grid at any warp, so x100 is safe there and makes the longest burn (1,213 s) 12 s real. Stopping at SOI changes lets the pilot check the capture periapsis. A 180 deg slew at 80 % torque and 5 deg/s takes 40 s wet and 59 s with 100 t, so 60 s covers it and the max() covers the rest.', 'All eight levels of decision 19 offered, none greyed; the readout shows the actual rate when below 90 % of nominal. Thrust under warp: up to x10 with manual throttle, up to x100 for burns with a scheduled cut-off (autopilot, flight scripts'' :after-dv); throttling up above the limit lowers warp to it. SOI drop-out: stop (x1 at the change). Burn lead time: max(60 s, estimated slew to the burn attitude + 10 s). Rotation and translation keys above x4 are ignored with a message, not a warp drop. Refusals and drop-outs show a message with the reason for 3 s.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:12:48Z', '2026-10-14T18:12:48Z', 39, NULL, 'rule', 39, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (32, 'Autopilot scope and safety rules', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/design.md Flight, The autopilot (task 40). Same physics and fuel as the player per milestone 3; the final 100 m to a fixture stay the pilot''s work, the core of piloting.', 'The autopilot plans by patched-conic first guesses refined by shooting with the flight''s integrator, flies finite burns centred on the node with cut-off by delta-v (full throttle unless under 10 s; RCS below 0.5 m/s), and writes controls like the pilot. Milestone 3: circularise, set Ap/Pe, Hohmann to a circular altitude, plane change (inclination or a target''s plane), rendezvous with a place and stop at a range (default 100 m on the fixture axis) and keep station; milestone 4: TLI, capture, TEI and Earth capture, Lambert transfers to asteroids and planets in scope with mid-course corrections. It never changes warp and never grapples. Backspace or any flight key disengages. Safety: no planned path below a body''s crash radius plus 50 km (Earth, planets) or 10 km (Moon); propellant for the plan plus max(2 %, 20 m/s) reserve, abort below half the reserve; mid-burn cut if continuing makes the orbit unsafe, abort after a repeated attitude error over 5 deg; no plan inside 0.551 AU of the Sun or beyond the consumables.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:19:02Z', '2026-10-14T18:19:02Z', 40, NULL, 'rule', 40, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (33, 'Grapple tolerances, contact and berthing', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/design.md Flight, Grappling and berthing (task 40); extends decision 24: Grapple, not docking; towing in and decision 25: Damage model. Wider than real berthing so a careful first try works.', 'Capture when the cone''s tip touches a fixture at 0.01-0.30 m/s closing speed, lateral offset <= 0.30 m, angle <= 10 deg, relative rotation <= 0.5 deg/s: claws close in 2 s, a perfectly inelastic coupling into one stack. Other contacts at <= 1.0 m/s bounce (restitution 0.3, no damage); above 1.0 m/s the ship is destroyed. Ship collision shape: three boxes; objects spheres or boxes, tested against promoted objects. Towable fixtures carry up to 100 t; berth fixtures (depots, stations) hold the ship fixed to the station with engine and RCS locked; refuelling only berthed. Milestone 3.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:19:02Z', '2026-10-14T18:19:02Z', 40, NULL, 'rule', 40, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (34, 'How death works', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/design.md Flight, Dying and what follows (task 40), within docs/architecture.md Collisions and the Sun and decision 19: Time-warp rules (the life-support alarms are drop-out events of the same kind). Distances computed from the rule, not guessed.', 'Crash spheres: Earth R+100 km, Moon R+0, Venus R+200 km, Mars R+125 km, gas giants 1,000 km above 1 bar, Sun R_sun; contact above 1.0 m/s kills. Sun: hull temperature relaxing (tau 900 s, exact update) to T_eq = ((0.30 S + 100 W/m2)/(2*0.85*sigma))^(1/4): warning 350 K at 0.551 AU (alarm, warp drop-out, autopilot limit), danger 400 K at 0.415 AU, death 450 K at 0.326 AU. Life support (milestone 4): 180 person-days, alarms and warp drop-outs at 30 and 7 days, death at 0. Running out of propellant does not kill. On death the sim stops, the outside view shows the ship and a LOST panel with the cause; Return restarts from the last checkpoint, N a new game; no permanent death; checkpoints in memory before milestone 5 (start state; from milestone 3 also after a completed plan and on berthing); death never fails a quest, its step is flown again.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:19:02Z', '2026-10-14T18:19:02Z', 40, NULL, 'rule', 40, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (35, 'Saving and audio', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/design.md Flight, Saving and Sound (task 40); milestone 5 requires saves; audio stays at the architecture''s default milestone 6, after flight, cockpit and story, since agents cannot hear it and the game is complete without it.', 'Saving from milestone 5: checkpoint.sexp (at quest steps, berthing, a completed plan, only when coasting and safe; death restarts from it) and resume.sexp (on quit and every 5 min real time; deleted on death); no save slots, no save key; saves t, ship state, controls, grapple and load, hull temperature, consumables, target, promoted objects, quest state; not warp or autopilot plans. Audio in milestone 6 as a should: synthesised through-the-hull sounds (cab ambience, engine rumble, RCS puffs, valves and grapple, contact thud, alarms, radio static), one audio-sink and a Lisp mixer, U toggles sound, silent without a device.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:19:02Z', '2026-10-14T18:19:02Z', 40, NULL, 'rule', 40, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (36, 'Cockpit instruments: Slug screens, dot-matrix strip, gauges and lamps; Ghostty out', 'answered', '[]', 'Slug text on the screen planes stays sharp at every zoom and needs no render targets or mipmaps; the grapple camera is needed because the grapple tip is 55 deg below the window''s line of sight. Ghostty is cut: no screen runs a program, luv/ghostty needs the full dev shell (not the slim one of the sandbox test, decision 2) and copying Luvcraft''s ~2,150-line terminal wall, and decision 3 would need extending; the COMMS screen gives the terminal look with Slug text. Every instrument of ''Flight'' has a cockpit place, so the pilot can fly from the cockpit alone (milestone 3).', 'The cockpit (milestone 3) is a rack of mismatched screens, a dot-matrix strip, needle gauges, lamps and 18 illuminated push-buttons around the 1.2 x 0.6 m window, laid out in docs/design.md ''The cockpit'' relative to the eye point of decision 30 (default head pitch 16 deg down; two side portholes added). Screens: Slug text (Monaspace Neon, fixed grids) and ribbon plots drawn directly on the screen planes in the scene pass, no screen textures; the grapple camera is the only render target (480x320, every frame while armed). Dot-matrix strip: 5x7 glyphs of decision 7 as instanced amber dots. Gauges: needle meshes with 0.3 s lag; lamps flash in real time. Ghostty cut. Keys added: B propellant transfer, Y acknowledge. Readability at 1280x800: cap height >= 10 px (0.0175 d m), contrast >= 7:1 for screen text, emissive output divided by exposure; colours: white normal, green ok, amber caution, red danger, cyan own ship, magenta target, never colour alone.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:27:50Z', '2026-10-14T18:27:50Z', 41, NULL, 'rule', 41, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (37, 'Message channels the story may use', 'answered', '[]', 'A linear quest line needs orders and radio chatter, both carried by one screen and a lamp; the printer strip is unreadable at 1280x800 (a 58 mm roll holds ~6 legible characters per line at 0.6 m), voices and portraits cost far more than they show (pillar ''small and finished''). docs/design.md ''The cockpit'', ''Messages and radio''.', 'Four channels: RADIO (sender, game time, text; COMMS screen log typed out at 60 chars/s, MSG lamp until read with Y or a click, dot-matrix ''MSG <SENDER>'', first line in the outside/map overlay, light-time delay from the sender, text only, no reply choices; milestone 5), JOB (the current quest step''s job card: title and up to 3 lines at the top of COMMS and one line in the overlay; a step may require Y to acknowledge), SHIP (the flight computer''s own messages, written by the game, not the story), static texts (the ship''s name above the window, up to 3 tape notes chosen by docs/story.md). Cut: printer strip, recorded voices, video-call faces, an inbox.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:27:55Z', '2026-10-14T18:27:55Z', 41, NULL, 'rule', 41, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (38, 'Start orbit: polar', 'answered', '[]', 'task 42 (docs/design.md ''The places''): J2 (decision 17: Gravity model) turns orbit planes at -(3/2) n J2 (R/p)^2 cos i: at 51.6 deg -5.00 deg/day at 400 km and -4.09 at 800 km, so places at different altitudes drift apart ~0.9 deg/day and a rendezvous days later costs hundreds of m/s of plane change. At i = 90 deg cos i = 0: all polar orbits keep their plane at every altitude, so the places stay coplanar all game and presets cost only altitude and phasing. A polar orbit also shows every latitude. Supersedes the inclination and RAAN of decision 23: Start orbit; altitude, tanks, attitude unchanged.', '400 km circular (a = 6,778,136.6 m), i = 90 deg, RAAN 270 deg, argument of latitude 0 at the start date (decision 14: Units and time epoch, default 2040-01-01 00:00 UTC), tanks full, attitude prograde hold; sunlit at the start (25 deg from the Sun, beta -10 deg). Every Earth-orbit place shares the plane (i 90, RAAN 270).', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:41:48Z', '2026-10-14T18:41:48Z', 42, NULL, 'rule', 42, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (39, 'Places in scope', 'answered', '[]', 'task 42 (docs/design.md ''The places''): Delta-v checked against the Mule''s 6.65 km/s (1.74 with 50 t) and its 180 person-days: every place above is reachable with refuelling at Fulmar, Highport or Peary; Venus (no return), Mars (5.6 km/s, 259 d), Kamo''oalewa (cheapest round trip ~360 d, 9 km/s within 180 d), the main belt (9.7 km/s, 472 d) are not. A loaded tug cannot bring rock from Highport to LEO (2.76 km/s), hence the high water plant. Lagrange points need three-body motion; places are on Kepler rails (decision 19: Time-warp rules).', 'Earth orbit (milestone 3 presets, all invented, polar i 90 deg RAAN 270 deg): Tern Station (crew, 420 km), Fulmar Depot (LEO propellant, 500 km), Derelict K-17 (dead 2.5 t upper stage at 800 km, pushed by hand to re-entry), Highport (depot and water plant, 1,000 x 70,000 km), Skua Yard (bagged rocks 20-100 t and one 150 t beside Highport). Moon (milestone 4): Peary Depot, 100 km polar lunar orbit; landing cut. Asteroids: Skua Yard''s rocks and one invented 50 t passing rock captured into Highport''s orbit (milestone 4, should); real asteroids (Kamo''oalewa, Ceres, Vesta, Pallas, Hygiea) map markers only (could). Planets: all eight on rails, point sprites and flat-colour map impostors, no destinations, no textures. Not places: GEO, Lagrange points/NRHO, constellations.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:41:58Z', '2026-10-14T18:41:58Z', 42, NULL, 'rule', 42, '2026-10-14, task 47: 2026-10-14, task 47: asteroid elements read from JPL SBDB (epoch JD 2461200.5) and tabled in docs/design.md ''Asteroids''; Lambert re-run: Kamo''oalewa round trip 4.9 km/s within 360 d, 8.2 within 180 d; 2008 EV5''s one window of the decade opens at the start (6.4 km/s, 180 d), later >= 7 km/s: both stay cut. Moon: CGI Moon Kit 2019 4k colour (~2.0 MB JPEG) and ldem_4 (4.0 MB) fit decision 13.

2026-10-14, task 51: 2026-10-14, task 51: docs/story.md keeps every place name (decision 49: Story: names, call signs and ships); adds Peary Camp (lunar surface, a RADIO location only, never shown) and names the passing rock 2040 BQ7; docs/design.md ''Earth-orbit points of interest'' and ''What the story may use'' say the names are kept.

2026-10-14, task 55: 2026-10-14, task 55: POD 14, a standard 12 t cargo pod, added in Skua Yard (Highport''s orbit, M0 179.87587 deg, inertial, no spin; milestone 5) as a load, not a place; Skua Yard laid out as a string 666.7 m apart at apogee (decision 53: Deliveries, the stranded offer, checkpoint floors and Skua Yard''s layout); docs/design.md ''Earth-orbit points of interest'' and ''Rigid bodies'' list it.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (40, 'Earth''s look and drag', 'answered', '[]', 'task 42 (docs/design.md ''The places'', ''Earth''): half of each orbit is night, and a cloudless Earth looks like a map; each costs one texture (<= 5 MB, decision 13: Asset storage and attribution) and a few shader lines. Drag at 400 km (rho ~2.8e-12 kg/m3, USSA 1976) is 2.7e-7 m/s2 on the Mule, a 42 m altitude loss per day, invisible; it matters only below ~200 km, where the 100 km crash sphere and the autopilot''s 150 km floor already rule. Confirms decision 17: Gravity model (no drag).', 'Blue Marble NG of the start date''s month (January: 200401), one month all game (milestone 2); atmosphere rim and a halo impostor at R+100 km (milestone 2); night lights from NASA Black Marble 2016 (milestone 3, should); a static, unanimated cloud layer from NASA Visible Earth ''Blue Marble: Clouds'' (milestone 3, should); ocean glint could (milestone 6); aurorae, weather, relief, seasonal textures cut. Drag out.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:41:58Z', '2026-10-14T18:41:58Z', 42, NULL, 'rule', 42, '2026-10-14, task 47: 2026-10-14, task 47: sources verified; night lights = BlackMarble_2016_3km.jpg resampled to 5400x2700 (~1.0 MB), clouds = cloud_combined_8192.tif resampled to a 5400x2700 greyscale JPEG (~3.5 MB), Blue Marble January = world.200401.3x5400x2700.jpg (1.8 MB); URLs, credits and sizes in docs/design.md ''The places'', ''Sources''.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (41, 'Weapons: cut', 'answered', '[]', 'Pillar ''the ship is a tool'': a tug''s tools are its grapple and engine, and the game centres on piloting; the developer''s ideas allow low-tech weapons only ''if any''. Weapons need reacting targets, which rails-only other ships (decision 27: Other ships) cannot be, and damage, which the ship does not have (decision 25: Damage model). docs/story.md uses the trade''s own dangers and conflicts told by RADIO instead. Recorded by task 43 in docs/design.md ''Scope'', ''Weapons''.', 'No weapons in the game, low-tech or otherwise; no ship carries one and the quest line has no fight.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:45:48Z', '2026-10-14T18:45:48Z', 43, NULL, 'rule', 43, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (42, 'Scope: what each milestone builds', 'answered', '[]', 'Task 43 wrote the scope table from all sections of docs/design.md and the developer''s ideas of milestone 1, and checked it against each milestone''s Outcome and Done when; no real contradiction remains (milestone 4''s belt and planets are shown, not destinations, inside the milestone''s intent). Real asteroids on the map are raised from ''could'' to ''should'' so milestone 4 shows the belt, as its Outcome names it; this partly supersedes decision 39: Places in scope.', 'docs/design.md ''Scope'', ''Feature list'' is the milestones'' specification. Milestone 2: polar LEO start, integrated n-body flight with J2, the Mule rigid body with engine, RCS mixer and one propellant pool, key map, direct/rate damping/holds, eight warp levels with refusals and drop-outs, outside view, basic map, minimal readout, Blue Marble Earth with atmosphere, stars, Sun glare, untextured Moon, the Mule''s meshes, Earth crash and restart, flight scripts burn/raise-orbit/deorbit, drift tests. Milestone 3: the DIY cockpit (screens, strip, lamps, gauges, buttons, grapple camera), prediction ribbons and targets, Earth-orbit autopilot with safety rules, the five Earth-orbit places as presets on rails, grapple, berthing, refuelling, towing to 100 t, contact rules, re-entry of released objects, SHIP messages, in-memory checkpoints; should: night lights, clouds, other ships as scenery, the cab''s sunlit patch. Milestone 4: Moon texture and Peary Depot, autopilot across SOIs (TLI, capture, TEI), planets on rails as sprites and map impostors (no destinations), the Sun''s heat and death (tested), life support, asteroid meshes; should: the passing rock, real asteroids (Kamo''oalewa, Ceres, Vesta, Pallas, Hygiea) on the map; could: belt band, 16k Moon. Milestone 5: quests, RADIO and JOB, static texts, the story''s names and date, saves, headless play-through; should: story ships moving on rails. Milestone 6: title screen, introduction, F1 help and Escape menu, credits screen, README, nix run, performance 60 Hz at 1280x800 on the developer''s machine measured and documented; should: sound; could: gamepad, ocean glint, Saturn''s rings. Cut: weapons, docking ports and EVA, landing, drag and aerobraking, per-part damage, economy, electric and hypergolic drives, power/boil-off/slosh simulation, other playable ships, auto-warp, gravity assists, destinations beyond the Moon and the passing rock, dense belt field, planet and seasonal textures, Ghostty, printer strip, voices, save slots, debris simulation.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:45:57Z', '2026-10-14T18:45:57Z', 43, NULL, 'rule', 43, '2026-10-14, task 43: 2026-10-14, task 43: docs/design.md ''Scope'' written (feature list, weapons, performance target, milestone checks, what the story may use); ''Asteroids'' raises real asteroids on the map to should; the Moon untextured until milestone 4 and Skua Yard''s rocks plain spheres until milestone 4; docs/architecture.md title.lisp also holds the Escape menu and the credits screen.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (43, 'Warp drop-outs for contact and life support', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/design.md ''Time warp'' and ''Dying and what follows'' (decision 33: Grapple tolerances, contact and berthing, decision 34: How death works) add events that decision 19 did not list; they follow its rule unchanged, so this extends only its list of events. Life-support times are linear in t and need no prediction; contacts are predicted with bounding spheres because box tests at coarse steps would be pointless: the drop-out brings the ship to x1, where the fine-step box test decides.', 'The clock''s drop-out events (decision 19: Time-warp rules) also include a predicted contact with an object (the stack''s and the object''s bounding spheres meeting on the prediction, objects on rails or promoted) and the life-support alarms at 30 and 7 days and the end of consumables (times in closed form, scheduled like a burn); the Sun hazard zone is entering 0.551 AU from the Sun (the 350 K warning distance of decision 34: How death works). Same 3 s real-time rule, x1 at the event, same refusals.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:48:51Z', '2026-10-14T18:48:51Z', 46, NULL, 'rule', 46, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (44, 'Contact, capture and berthing in the simulation', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/design.md ''Grappling and berthing'', ''Towing'' and ''Dying and what follows'' (decision 33, decision 34) name contact, capture, berthing and life support but leave how the simulation does them to docs/architecture.md (task 46). Immovable berth objects keep stations on their rails and their orbits unchanged by a careless pilot; towable objects must take impulses for a capture to conserve momentum. Holding a berthed ship at its station keeps decision 19 (the ship is never on Kepler rails: the station it rides is promoted and integrated, since it is within 100 km).', 'Promoted objects are rigid bodies: towable ones (pods, rocks, K-17) integrated with torque-free rotation and finite mass; berth objects (stations, depots) immovable in contacts, their attitude kinematic (LVLH, inertial or Sun-pointing as places.lisp says). Contact in hazards.lisp after each step: swept bounding spheres on the relative Hermite, then the grapple tip against fixture plates, then oriented boxes and spheres (separating axes) on substeps of at most 0.25 m relative motion, first overlap bisected to 1e-4 s; only approaching contacts count. Response: capture (perfectly inelastic coupling into one stack at the contact pose, linear and angular momentum conserved), bounce (impulse with restitution 0.3 and both bodies'' mass and inertia; berth objects take none), death above 1.0 m/s. Berthed: the ship is not integrated but held at the fixture''s pose on the promoted station, each step; release gives it the fixture point''s velocity. Hull temperature and consumables are per-step scalars outside RK4, updated exactly. Checkpoints are copies of one simulation-state object in memory (clock.lisp), written to files by saves.lisp from milestone 5.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:48:51Z', '2026-10-14T18:48:51Z', 46, NULL, 'rule', 46, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (45, 'Web access for pm workers still denied after decision 28', 'answered', '["Permission added; task 44 runs again", "Drop task 44; the figures stay sourced by name only"]', '`decision 28: Web access for task 44: Verify the sourced figures of docs/design.md "The ship(s)"` was answered ''Allow WebSearch and WebFetch for pm workers'', but in run 40 of `task 44: Verify the sourced figures of docs/design.md "The ship(s)"` (2026-10-14) both tools were again denied by the session''s permission settings (''Claude requested permissions to use WebSearch, but you haven''t granted it yet''). The repo has no .claude/settings.json and a worker must not grant itself permissions. Add the permission where pm workers'' sessions read it (e.g. permissions.allow ["WebSearch", "WebFetch"] in .claude/settings.json or the pm worker''s launch flags), then answer; `task 47: Verify the sourced figures and asset licences of docs/design.md "The places"` needs the same.', 'Permission added; task 44 runs again', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:51:35Z', '2026-10-14T20:14:20Z', 44, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '2026-10-14, task 44: 2026-10-14, task 44: with the permission in place task 44 ran with web access and verified the RL10C-1, CECE, GO2/GH2 RCS, NIST density and Al 2219 figures (docs/design.md ''Propulsion'', Sources); the L3Harris RL10 spec-sheet URLs answer 404, read through a search engine''s copy.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (46, 'Rails across spheres, hyperbolas and re-entry', 'answered', '[]', 'task 48: docs/design.md ''The places'' needs the passing rock on a geocentric hyperbola inside Earth''s sphere and heliocentric outside it, and Derelict K-17 to burn up after its release at a 90 km perigee; decision 19: Time-warp rules named only Kepler rails with J2 rates. Segments precomputed keep rails exact, deterministic and cheap; exact boundaries need no hysteresis because nothing is switched per step.', 'Rails objects hold a list of conic segments built once per object (physics.lisp) from one element set: forward to 2050-01-01 and, for a definition, back to the start date; a segment ends at leaving its body''s Laplace sphere (closed form), entering a child''s sphere (sampled at the coarse step rule, swept-sphere test, Newton to 1e-6 s) or the crash sphere; switches at the exact boundary, no hysteresis, state carried through the ephemerides as the ship''s re-centring; at most 16 segments; place-state(t) stays a pure function of t. Hyperbolas (e > 1) stored as q, e, i, Omega, omega, t_p, propagated by the hyperbolic Kepler equation with near-parabolic forms; |e-1| < 1e-6 nudged to 1 +- 1e-6; pure two-body (J2 secular rates only for Earth-centred ellipses). An object meeting its body''s crash sphere is removed at the crossing: closed form on rails, the ship''s swept crash test when promoted; marked removed (time, body, point), a SHIP message, no warp drop-out; drag stays out.', NULL, '2026-10-14T19:23:46Z', '2026-10-14T19:23:46Z', 48, NULL, 'rule', 48, '2026-10-14, task 48: 2026-10-14, task 48: docs/architecture.md ''Hyperbolic rails'', ''Rails across spheres of influence'', ''Re-entry of objects'' and their tests; physics.lisp holds the segment builder and the shared swept-sphere test; checkpoints carry segment lists and removed objects; the polar start orbit''s node test added; docs/design.md cites the decision instead of task 48.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (47, 'Rigid bodies of the places', 'answered', '[]', 'Task 49 completes what decision 44: Contact, capture and berthing in the simulation needs from places.lisp. Spins about the fixture axis keep the pin and its axis still, inside decision 33''s 0.5 deg/s, so capture needs no matched motion; the rates grade the difficulty. The 0.3 m stand-off: at the 10 deg capture limit the grapple box''s front corners reach 0.20 m ahead of the tip and would touch a flush face (or a sphere, with the 0.30 m offset) before the tip reached the plate. Sun-pointing suits the sunshaded depots and Highport''s wings; LVLH gives Tern an R-bar approach to its zenith fixture. Fulmar''s sunshade grows from 12 to 16 m to shade its tanks; the Mule''s loaded inertia rows move the load 0.3 m forward.', 'Every place object has body axes at its CoM (FRD), a full inertia tensor from its parts, 1-5 boxes or spheres and fixtures (pin, outward axis) as tabled in docs/design.md ''The places'', ''Rigid bodies: attitude, mass, shapes and fixtures''. Attitude: Tern Station LVLH; Fulmar Depot, Highport, Peary Depot Sun-pointing (x to the Sun, z towards ecliptic south); the parked rock mover inertial, immovable like a berth object, no free fixture. Towable objects spin about their fixture axis (+x): K-17 0.3 deg/s, Skua Yard rocks 0.05-0.30 deg/s, the passing rock 0.4 deg/s, cargo pods none. Every fixture plate stands 0.3 m proud of its object''s shapes. Masses: Tern 85 t, Fulmar 60 t, Highport 300 t, Peary 42 t dry, rock mover 1,087 t with its 10 m rock, K-17 2.5 t, a standard cargo pod 12 t (4.0 x 2.5 x 2.5 m). Ships berthed at places as scenery are part of the place''s shapes.', NULL, '2026-10-14T19:32:04Z', '2026-10-14T19:32:04Z', 49, NULL, 'rule', 49, '2026-10-14, task 49: 2026-10-14, task 49: docs/design.md ''The places'' adds ''Rigid bodies: attitude, mass, shapes and fixtures'' (tables per object and rock); Fulmar''s sunshade 16 m; ''Inertia'' loaded rows, the control-feel table and the towing turn time recomputed for the 0.3 m stand-off; docs/architecture.md ''Rails'' names the rock mover among berth objects and cites the decision, the contact cost counts Skua Yard''s objects.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (48, 'Story: setting and premise', 'answered', '[]', 'task 51 per plan 11: story. A sober, small setting fits the hard sci-fi brief and decision 41 (no weapons) and decision 26 (no money): danger from the trade, people''s conflicts by radio. The ending is a hook into a longer adventure without needing any destination cut by decision 42. Full text: docs/story.md ''Setting'', ''Characters'', ''Premise''.', 'January 2040, about twenty people in one polar plane living on rock water. The pilot (no name, gender or face, addressed as ''Patience'') is Cairn Water''s new pilot of the Mule tug PATIENCE (M-07), taking over from Mirela Doru, retired at the career dose limit. Cairn Water (Highport, Skua Yard, Peary Depot and Camp, tankers, rock mover, bagger probes, four Mules) employs and pays; Boreal Orbital owns Tern Station, Fulmar Depot and the AURORA capsules; Lindgren Debris (Kiruna) hires the K-17 push. Conflicts told by RADIO only (Boreal wary of rocks near the plane). The prelude ends berthed at Highport after 2040 BQ7 is delivered (or after the lunar return if the passing rock is dropped): Cairn announces a long-haul tug and offers the pilot its seat, told by RADIO only, nothing cut is flown.', NULL, '2026-10-14T19:41:36Z', '2026-10-14T19:41:36Z', 51, NULL, 'rule', 51, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (49, 'Story: names, call signs and ships', 'answered', '[]', 'task 51 per plan 11: story. Working place names already read well and are cited across docs/design.md and docs/architecture.md, so they are kept rather than renamed. Names and full list: docs/story.md ''Names and terms''.', 'Ship PATIENCE, registration M-07, call sign ''Patience''. Companies Cairn Water, Boreal Orbital, Lindgren Debris. Speakers: CAIRN OPS Rafael Quiroga (Highport), TERN Ana Folau (Tern Station), LINDGREN Sami Lindgren (Kiruna), HIGHPORT Esther Okafor (Highport), GRINDSTONE Ruth Abara (Skua Yard), THRIFT Dag Eliassen (THRIFT), PEARY Tomasz Wierzbicki (Peary Camp on the Moon), MIRELA Mirela Doru (Tromsø); Cairn senders cyan, others white. Places keep their working names (Tern Station, Fulmar Depot, Derelict K-17, Highport, Skua Yard, Peary Depot); Peary Camp a RADIO location only. Other ships: STUBBORN M-04 and THISTLE M-05 (berthed at Highport), THRIFT M-09 and TANKER 3 (moving, milestone 5 should), TANKER 2 (at Fulmar), AURORA 11 (at Tern), GRINDSTONE (rock mover), BAGGER 3 (with the passing rock). Passing rock designation 2040 BQ7. Static texts: plate ''M-07  PATIENCE''; three tape notes as in docs/story.md ''Static texts''.', NULL, '2026-10-14T19:41:36Z', '2026-10-14T19:41:36Z', 51, NULL, 'rule', 51, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (50, 'Story: start date and the passing rock''s date', 'answered', '[]', 'task 51 per plan 11: story. Computed with Meeus ch. 47 (truncated, precessed to J2000; checked against example 47.a within 0.1 deg) and Highport''s J2 apsidal drift -0.0665 deg/day; the Earth-orbit quests take ~8 days, leaving 12 days of slack before the January TLI; the rock''s date keeps the meeting clear of the Moon and leaves time after either lunar window. Table and method: docs/story.md ''Start date''.', 'Start date stays 2040-01-01 00:00 UTC with the start orbit''s phase and lighting of decision 38 unchanged. Best lunar window of the year: the Moon at Highport''s apogee direction 2040-01-26 02:36 UTC (0.6 deg off), TLI from Highport''s perigee ~2040-01-21 (game day 20); fallback 2040-02-22 (2.2 deg). Passing rock 2040 BQ7: found 2040-01-19, perigee 2040-03-12 12:00 UTC at 7,378 km in the polar plane; the tug meets it ~2 days before perigee near 400,000 km on Highport''s apogee side, the Moon 134 deg away.', NULL, '2026-10-14T19:41:36Z', '2026-10-14T19:41:36Z', 51, NULL, 'rule', 51, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (51, 'Story ships flying free: THRIFT, TANKER 3, BAGGER 3', 'answered', '[]', 'Task 54. ''Behind'' Highport put the parked ships on the autopilot''s rendezvous aim line (1 km behind the target) and in the lane a rock towed from Skua Yard (30 km behind) uses; ''ahead'' is used by no approach. Same-orbit objects keep their time gap, so the gap in metres is smallest at apogee (2 and 5 km) and largest at perigee (20.7 and 51.8 km), always inside the 100 km promotion range near Highport. Immovable because story ships move on rails only (decision 27: Other ships) and a bump must not change the rails the story''s trips depend on.', 'THRIFT (wet Mule, 36.0 t) and TANKER 3 (24 m tanker, two LH2/LOX tank pairs, 54.6 t full) park between trips in Highport''s orbit AHEAD of it, M0 = 180.0220 deg (5 km at apogee) and 180.0088 deg (2 km), clear of the autopilot''s aim point 1 km behind Highport, the free fixture''s approach and the Skua Yard tow lane behind. BAGGER 3, a 1.5 m bagger probe (0.5 t, 1.35 km/s), rides the passing rock''s rails shifted by dt = 1 km / v(400,000 km) (0.626 s at v_inf 0.75 km/s) and flies on past perigee after the grapple; the rock''s fixture axis is held inertially against its inbound velocity at 400,000 km, so the bagger sits on the far side from the approach. All three are immovable objects with no player fixture (as the rock mover): THRIFT and TANKER 3 inertial, BAGGER 3 Sun-pointing; shapes, tensors and bounds in docs/design.md ''Rigid bodies: attitude, mass, shapes and fixtures''.', NULL, '2026-10-14T19:47:49Z', '2026-10-14T19:47:49Z', 54, NULL, 'rule', 54, '2026-10-14, task 54: 2026-10-14, task 54: docs/design.md ''Other ships'' adds the bagger probe kind; ''Earth-orbit points of interest'' places THRIFT and TANKER 3 ahead of Highport with the clearance check; ''Asteroids'' gives BAGGER 3''s time-shifted rails and the passing rock''s fixture axis; ''Rigid bodies'' adds their rows and parts; docs/story.md moves THRIFT and TANKER 3 from behind to ahead of Highport and draws BAGGER 3 with the rock; docs/architecture.md contact cost names them.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (52, 'Story: the quest line', 'answered', '[]', 'task 52 per plan 11: story. One skill per quest, each farther and heavier, every leg within the Mule''s 6.65 / 1.74 / 1.02 km/s and 180 person-days with refuelling only at Fulmar Depot, Highport, Peary Depot (running check in docs/story.md ''Running checks''; tightest: BQ7''s loaded 565 of 1,011 m/s). Completion conditions read only simulation state. Full text: docs/story.md ''The quest line''.', 'Nine quests in fixed order: 1 Shakedown (CAIRN OPS, start orbit, attitude and a 2x2.8 m/s out-and-back burn; milestone 2), 2 Home port (TERN, rendezvous and berth at Tern Station; 3), 3 Seventeen (LINDGREN, grapple K-17, push to Pe < 100 km by hand, release, raise own Pe, burn-up; 3), 4 Topping off (Fulmar Depot fixture A, refuel; 3), 5 The long climb (Highport, berth at the plant, refuel; 3), 6 Rock three (Skua Yard 3, 50 t, delivered to the plant; 3), 7 The January window (POD 14, 12 t, to Peary Depot via the TLI near 2040-01-21, refuel, back to Highport; 4), 8 The big one (Skua Yard 5, 100 t; 3, placed after 7, skipped if given after 2040-03-02 12:00), 9 BQ7 (intercept 400,000 km out, grapple, capture at perigee 2040-03-12 into Highport''s orbit, leave in Skua Yard, then the ending; 4 should, skipped if given after 2040-03-06). If the passing rock is dropped, quest 9 is replaced by the ending alone after quest 8 (assay of Skua Yard 5); no quest depends on moving story ships (if dropped, THRIFT''s messages come from Highport). The Sun is no quest (0.551 AU and back ~250 days > 180 person-days). Death restarts the step from its checkpoint; checkpoints carry a Δv floor; a stranded ship gets CAIRN OPS''s offer, Y restores the last checkpoint.', NULL, '2026-10-14T20:01:02Z', '2026-10-14T20:01:02Z', 52, NULL, 'rule', 52, '2026-10-14, task 52: 2026-10-14, task 52: docs/story.md ''The quest line'' written (nine quests, rules, running checks); Premise''s ending and Start date''s slack refer to quest 8; Names and terms add POD 14 and the quest titles; follow-ups task 55, task 56, task 57 in plan 10: game design.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (53, 'Deliveries, the stranded offer, checkpoint floors and Skua Yard''s layout', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/story.md ''Rules for every quest'' (decision 52: Story: the quest line) relies on these; task 55 puts them in docs/design.md (''Towing'', ''Dying and what follows'', ''Saving'', ''Earth-orbit points of interest'', ''Rigid bodies'') and docs/architecture.md (''Removal of objects'', ''Checkpoints'', tests). The yard''s spacing puts every autopilot aim point (1 km behind a target) mid-gap, 333 m from both neighbours, and orders the rocks as the quests take them so each tow leaves forwards into the empty lane to Highport; the offer and floors make sure no state of the quest line is a dead end and no checkpoint holds a ship that cannot finish its step.', 'Delivery: a towable load released with its CoM within 100 m of a receiving fixture''s pin (Highport''s plant, milestone 3; Peary Depot''s berth, milestone 4) at <= 0.05 m/s relative to the object, still within 150 m 600 s later, is removed with cause :delivered (architecture ''Removal of objects'', causes :burned-up, :impact, :delivered); any towable load counts. Stranded offer (milestone 5): outside a berth, Δv with the load below the step''s floor, an escape path (e >= 1 around Earth past perigee, or the Sun dominant) or the step''s object out of reach, held 10 min game time: CAIRN OPS RADIO marked Y; Y restores the last checkpoint as Return does; no warp drop-out. Checkpoint floors (milestone 5): each step has (v_f, m_L); every checkpoint is vetoed while v_e ln((m_dry+m_L+m_p)/(m_dry+m_L)) < v_f; the floor lives in the quest state, clock.lisp reads it. Skua Yard is a string on Highport''s orbit, 666.7 m apart at apogee from the front: Skua Yard 3 (+2,000 m), POD 14 (+1,333 m, M0 179.87587 deg, inertial, fixture aft at apogee, no spin), 5, 1 (the reference, M0 179.87 deg), 2, 4, 6, the rock mover at -4,000 m.', NULL, '2026-10-14T20:06:34Z', '2026-10-14T20:06:34Z', 55, NULL, 'rule', 55, '2026-10-14, task 55: 2026-10-14, task 55: docs/design.md ''Towing'' (Delivery), ''Dying and what follows'' (the stranded offer), ''Saving'' (checkpoint floors), ''Earth-orbit points of interest'' (Skua Yard''s layout, POD 14), ''Rigid bodies'' (POD 14 row), scope table and ''What the story may use''; docs/architecture.md ''Re-entry of objects'' generalised to ''Removal of objects'', ''Checkpoints'' (floors), tests for delivery, floors and the offer; docs/story.md cites them; task 56 gets the approach inside Skua Yard.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (54, 'Autopilot rendezvous: phasing, lunar chain, hyperbolic targets, clear approaches', 'answered', '[]', 'task 52''s quest line budgets <= 50 m/s phasing per rendezvous and uses presets the design did not define: a 48 h single-revolution Lambert search cannot close a large phase gap between orbits of equal period, a TLI aimed only at periapsis altitude and inclination does not reach Peary Depot''s plane, a hyperbola has no period, and the straight path from 1 km behind a Skua Yard object passes its neighbour 667 m behind. Computed cases (docs/design.md ''Rendezvous: search, phasing and the approach''): Fulmar from 400 km <= 33 m/s within 48 h, Highport from Fulmar <= 28, Skua Yard from Highport 0.2, Peary after LOI, Highport after a lunar return and Skua Yard after BQ7''s capture 0. Refines decision 32: Autopilot scope and safety rules; its safety rules apply unchanged.', 'Rendezvous searches two families within a time limit (48 h default, 12 h-10 days on the AUTOPILOT screen; least total dv, ties within 1 m/s to the earlier arrival): A direct (Lambert, departures over the limit, 0.4-1.5 target periods) and B a phasing orbit (a tangential burn, or one merged into the plan''s capture/LOI/Hohmann apsis burn, period T_t - dt/N to catch up or T_t + (T_t - dt)/N to fall back, N whole revolutions <= 32, then A); falling back merged into a capture costs nothing, so phasing after a capture or LOI is free within 2 target periods. Lunar places from Earth orbit: one chain TLI (B-plane aimed at the place''s plane, periapsis 100 km, same sense) - mid-course - LOI with phasing - aim point - terminal; ''TLI: Peary Depot'' is the same chain. Targets on a hyperbola: departures anywhere on the ship''s orbit over up to 7 days, transfers 2-10 days, meeting inbound at least 36 h before the target''s periapsis, aim point 1 km out on the fixture axis. Terminal path: straight segments >= 100 m from the target''s CoM until the stop point and >= 200 m beyond every other object''s bound; aim point candidates in order 1 km behind on the orbit, 1 km radially from the stop point (outward unless the fixture axis points inward), 1 km out of plane; none clear: refused. Skua Yard''s rocks hold their fixture axes as POD 14 (inertial, aft along the orbit at apogee); ''Rendezvous: Skua Yard'' stops the stack''s CoM at the yard''s drop point, Skua Yard 3''s slot (+2,000 m at apogee).', NULL, '2026-10-14T20:34:03Z', '2026-10-14T20:34:03Z', 56, NULL, 'rule', 56, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (55, 'Meeting 2040 BQ7 and the lunar arrival from the polar plane', 'answered', '[]', 'task 56 computed both by Lambert and patched conics. At 400,000 km BQ7 is 17.6 deg past the apse line (true anomaly -162.4 deg), not at the transfer''s apogee: meeting there costs 2,404 m/s leaving 03-05; meeting 1.5 days out leaves a day and a half to grapple before the capture. The design''s 1,193 m/s to Peary assumed v_inf 0.83 km/s, the Moon moving along the ship''s plane; from the polar plane it moves across it. Both still fit: 996 m/s with 50 t after the intercept (565 needed), 1,353 m/s with POD 14 out of 3,866.', 'The tug meets 2040 BQ7 about 1.5 days before perigee near 325,000 km (2040-03-10 ~23:00 UTC), leaving Highport''s orbit 20-60 min after a perigee pass: 2,010 m/s leaving 2040-03-05 (1,683 on 03-03, 2,289 on 03-06), not ~2 days near 400,000 km. A lunar arrival from the polar plane has v_inf 0.99-1.04 km/s (the Moon crosses the plane at right angles), LOI into 100 km 880-925 m/s, not the coplanar 823: Highport -> Peary Depot in the January window 1,273 m/s (TLI 376 from the 2040-01-20 06:01 perigee, LOI 897 with a 4.9 deg plane change), LEO -> Peary Depot 3,983 m/s.', NULL, '2026-10-14T20:34:10Z', '2026-10-14T20:34:10Z', 56, NULL, 'rule', 56, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (56, 'Flight-script actions, start states and the quest play-through', 'answered', '[]', 'task 57 (Architecture: flight-script actions for the quest play-through): milestone 5''s headless play-through needs grapple, berth, delivery, refuelling, acknowledgements and quest waits that the milestone-2 vocabulary lacked, and ''no shortcuts around piloting'' rules out setting state, so every action is a key the player has and the last metres are flown by a closed loop on the cockpit''s own readouts. Chaining by end states through the save format makes every link a save, and re-running the chain instead of caching files keeps no stale state. The play-through stays out of scripts/test for its ~15 s budget.', 'Flight scripts (decision 21: Physics test bounds and flight scripts) gain pilot actions that press the game''s keys: :target, :autopilot <preset> <goal> [:range] / :off, :grapple :arm/:stow, :release (load or berth), :transfer :on/:off, :translate <axis> :dv, :keys … :for, :ack (Y, waits for a Y message), :warp :coast (re-raise after drop-outs, <= x4 in the autopilot''s terminal phase, x1 in :approach), and :approach <fixture> :speed 0.1: a closed-loop pilot in flights.lisp that reads only the grapple range finder and the TARGET screen and writes keys at x1 (fine control, 1/16 s ticks, hold at 20 m until aligned, up to 3 tries, 2 h limit). Waits and expects take flat conditions (:quest-step "n.m" as a string, :date, :periapsis-below/above, :apoapsis-below/above, :settled, :rate-below, :removed, :on-station, :berthed, :latched, :autopilot-done, :quest-line-done) with a :within limit (default 30 days); expects add :dv-below, :burn <kind> :between, :since-burn, :elapsed-below, :date-between, :clearance-above, :aim-point; the autopilot labels its burns (:departure, :mcc, :capture, :phasing, :arrival, :terminal, :trim). Start states: :new-game, :state (date, orbit, berthed, grappled, propellant, removed, quest step; milestone 3), :after <script> (its end state round-tripped through saves.lisp; run first if not yet run in the process), :file <save>. flights.lisp loads after saves.lisp. Milestone 5: flights/quest-1 … quest-9.sexp chained by :after, test play-through in tests/flights.lisp run by scripts/play-through and nix flake check, not scripts/test; expected ~15 s for ~74 days of game time.', NULL, '2026-10-14T20:51:17Z', '2026-10-14T20:51:17Z', 57, NULL, 'rule', 57, '2026-10-14, task 57: 2026-10-14, task 57: docs/architecture.md ''Scripted runs'' rewritten (Steps, Actions, Waits and expectations, Start states, The terminal approach, Running scripts, Scripts by milestone, The quest play-through with quest-1 and quest-3 written out); flights.lisp moved after saves.lisp in ''Files in load order''; docs/design.md ''Scripted runs'' (rendezvous) points at the start states; docs/story.md follow-up marked done. Deliveries close the last ~10 m by hand after the autopilot''s 100 m station, since a 90 m stop point would fall under the design''s 100 m clearance from Highport''s and Peary Depot''s CoM.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (57, 'Story: how it is told, its texts and the story ships'' schedule', 'answered', '[]', 'Task 53 fixes every text the game shows so quests.lisp can copy them and the play-through can test them. THRIFT''s two-way Peary trip and TANKER 3''s return could never switch slots near Highport while the pilot lives there (decision 27: Other ships, 200 km), so each makes one-way moves timed when the pilot is away (the lunar trip, the BQ7 trip). The tape notes did not fit their places at the readability rule''s cap height; the introduction wrongly put everyone in one orbit and the start over the pole (the start is at the ascending node, decision 38: Start orbit: polar). Refines decision 49: Story: names, call signs and ships and decision 52: Story: the quest line (texts, quest 7''s and 9''s cards now name the autopilot chains docs/design.md and docs/architecture.md use).', 'docs/story.md ''How the story is told'' and the quest tables are binding: every RADIO message has an id, trigger, sender, Y flag and exact text (quests 1-9, dated D1-D7, the stranded offer S1); JOB cards are a title and up to 3 lines of <= 26 characters. COMMS rows: 1 card title + step id (inverted), 2-4 card lines, 5 a rule with ''Y TO COPY'' while a Y is due, 6-11 the log (header ''HH:MM SENDER [+1.3s] [Y]'', text wrapped at 26, a grey date line per UTC day); ''NO JOB'' without a quest; the log is rebuilt from the quest state''s sent messages after a restore or load. Radio: digits for values to fly, words for talk, <= 160 characters (the ending 9h excepted), Y is the pilot''s only reply. Light time from the sender''s position, rounded up to the 1/64 s grid, shown as ''+1.3s'' when >= 0.5 s. Story ships: THRIFT leaves Highport''s orbit 2040-01-21 06:00 and parks half a turn ahead of Peary Depot from 2040-01-26 06:00; TANKER 3 parks 2 km ahead of Fulmar Depot 2040-01-22 to 2040-03-07, then back ahead of Highport; a move leaves one slot and appears in the next, not drawn in transit, each switch only while the player is beyond 200 km. Tape notes get line breaks, the third moves from the gauge bezel to the rack beside it. Introduction: ''most of them in one polar orbit'', ''on an orbit over both poles''.', NULL, '2026-10-14T21:03:10Z', '2026-10-14T21:03:10Z', 53, NULL, 'rule', 53, '2026-10-14, task 53: 2026-10-14, task 53: docs/story.md ''How the story is told'' written (channels, voice, COMMS rows, light time, other ships at work); every RADIO in per-quest tables with ids, the D table and S1; quest 8''s cards written out; cards 7.1, 7.2, 7.5, 9.2, 9.4-9.6 fixed to the autopilot chains; tape notes broken into lines; introduction corrected; THRIFT and TANKER 3 schedules. docs/design.md: Skua Yard spacing 666.7 m (not 300-800 m), the story ships'' trips, S1 cited, ''Messages and radio'' points at the story. Follow-up task in plan 10 for the story ships'' slots.

2026-10-14, task 53: 2026-10-14, task 53: the follow-up is task 58: Design: parking slots and moves of the story ships THRIFT and TANKER 3, in plan 11: story (plan 10 is closed), not plan 10.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (58, 'Story ships'' slots at Fulmar Depot and Peary Depot, schedules and clear plans', 'answered', '[]', 'task 58: the slots clear the aim points and terminal paths by kilometres (TANKER 3 >= 1,878 m beyond its bound from Fulmar''s fixture A approach; THRIFT 3,670 km from Peary''s). Quest 4''s transfer from a 370 km drift orbit overshoots ahead and passes TANKER 3 258 m beyond its bound, clear but tight; the LOI''s phasing orbit could cross THRIFT''s slot at the LOI pass when the depot leads by half a period, which the January window avoids (LOI 02:37 before THRIFT''s 06:00 arrival) and the plan check covers otherwise. The deferral is evaluated on the step grid so runs stay deterministic; the until-time at Fulmar is needed for a transit gap before the 03-07 arrival.', 'TANKER 3''s Fulmar slot: Fulmar Depot''s elements, u0 180.01666 deg (2 km ahead), 2040-01-22 06:00 until 2040-03-06 06:00 (departure added), then Highport''s slot from 2040-03-07 06:00. THRIFT''s lunar slot: Peary Depot''s elements, u0 180 deg (half a turn ahead, 3,674.8 km), from 2040-01-26 06:00. Both inertial in the LVLH attitude of their slot''s elements at the epoch. A story ship''s schedule is a list of stays (slot, from, until) with transit gaps; a switch record of actual switch times lives in the simulation state (checkpoints, saves); place-state(t) is a pure function of t and the record, nil in transit. Rule after each step: a due switch happens at t if the stack is more than 200 km from the slot concerned, else waits; scheduled times end a step (no warp drop-out). D2, D3 and 7f follow the actual switches. The autopilot''s plan check drops a candidate whose shot path from its first burn to the aim point comes within 200 m beyond the bound of any object other than the target (covers THRIFT at a later month''s LOI).', NULL, '2026-10-14T21:10:19Z', '2026-10-14T21:10:19Z', 58, NULL, 'rule', 58, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (59, 'Rendezvous transfers pass the target clear', 'answered', '[]', 'Found by task 58: a Hohmann-like transfer from a lower orbit arrives slower than the target and falls back through the target''s place before the braking burn. Checked with 2D Lambert transfers and Kepler propagation (throwaway). Extending the existing plan check costs <= 3 m/s and no new mechanism; preferring arrivals from behind would need a new search rule and costs more from below.', 'Refines decision 54: Autopilot rendezvous: phasing, lunar chain, hyperbolic targets, clear approaches. The plan check of decision 58: Story ships'' slots at Fulmar Depot and Peary Depot, schedules and clear plans also holds the shot path >= 200 m beyond the target''s own bound from the first burn until the aim point (the :arrival burn''s start); the terminal path''s 100 m from the CoM applies after it. The least-dv transfer from below passes the target''s CoM 10-27 m away (K-17, Fulmar Depot, Highport at apogee: inside their bounds); the least clear one costs <= 3 m/s more (Tern 0.05, Fulmar 0.75-0.9, K-17 2.4-2.8, Highport 0.3), arriving a few degrees short of or past the Hohmann''s 180 deg. Quest 3''s transfer 205 -> 208 m/s; quest 4''s worst case 89 -> 90 m/s; scripts: rendezvous-fulmar-phasing :dv-below 114 and :clearance-above 234 :only (:fulmar-depot) :until :aim-point, preset-<place> the same with bound + 200 and 3 m/s more.', NULL, '2026-10-14T21:16:17Z', '2026-10-14T21:16:17Z', 59, NULL, 'rule', 59, '2026-10-14, task 59: 2026-10-14, task 59: docs/design.md ''The terminal path and crowded targets'' extends the plan check to the target with the table of passes and costs; ''The quest line''s rendezvous'' (Fulmar worst case 90), ''Scripted runs'' (rendezvous-fulmar-phasing <= 114 m/s, >= 234 m), TANKER 3''s Fulmar note, ''Δv checked against the ship'' (K-17 220); docs/architecture.md :clearance-above gains :only and :until :aim-point, used by rendezvous-fulmar-phasing and preset-<place>; docs/story.md quest 3 (208 m/s transfer, 622 m/s, 4,795 kg), quest 4''s start state and the running checks.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (60, 'Design pillars', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/design.md cites the pillars as the reason for many cuts in ''Scope'' (landing, gravity assists, faces, planets as destinations), but no decision recorded them; task 62: Check the documents against the decision log found the gap. They restate the milestone 1 ideas and its ''scope over realism'' constraint.', 'Five pillars that keep or cut a feature, as docs/design.md ''Pillars'' states them: the ship is a tool (every part has a visible job); Newton is the game (all of the ship''s motion integrated, no shortcuts, propellant the limit); the cockpit is home-made (bolted-on screens, gauges, lamps; flyable from it alone); real sky, real places (real bodies and stars with sources, invented places plausible); small and finished (a feature that costs far more than it shows is cut; one ship done well).', NULL, '2026-10-14T21:24:46Z', '2026-10-14T21:24:46Z', 62, NULL, 'rule', 62, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (61, 'Performance target GPU: the default Vulkan device, the Intel iGPU', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/luv-capabilities.md ''Performance'' measured Luvcraft at 24-36 Hz on the Iris Xe and recommends assuming an iGPU; luv has no device selection, so players get the first device; decision 6: Target platforms promises any Vulkan 1.4 iGPU works. Clarifies, does not change, decision 42''s ''developer''s machine''.', 'The 60 Hz at 1280x800 target of milestone 6 (decision 42: Scope: what each milestone builds) is measured on the GPU luv picks by default, the first Vulkan device: on the developer''s machine the Intel Iris Xe, not the RTX 4060; the CPU share rests on the measured ~180 ns per RK4 step.', NULL, '2026-10-14T21:27:55Z', '2026-10-14T21:27:55Z', 63, NULL, 'rule', 63, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (62, 'Scope: must rows for each milestone''s Outcome', 'answered', '[]', 'Task 64 checked each milestone''s Outcome and Done when against the must rows of docs/design.md ''Scope''. Milestone 5''s ''other ships at their work'' and milestone 4''s ''the asteroid belt ... rendered'' were covered only by should rows; both cost little (meshes from the tug''s parts that the places'' shapes already include, decision 47: Rigid bodies of the places; five markers and orbit lines), so they become must. Decision 32: Autopilot scope and safety rules gave milestone 4 Lambert transfers to asteroids and planets in scope, but decision 39: Places in scope and decision 42: Scope: what each milestone builds leave none in scope; a manoeuvre with no destination is cut (pillar ''small and finished'').', 'Other ships as scenery at the places (AURORA 11, TANKER 2, STUBBORN, THISTLE, GRINDSTONE): must, milestone 3, not should. Real asteroids on the map (Kamo''oalewa, Ceres, Vesta, Pallas, Hygiea): must, milestone 4, not should. The autopilot''s heliocentric Lambert transfers to asteroids and planets are cut: no asteroid or planet is a destination; 2040 BQ7 is reached by ''Rendezvous: 2040 BQ7'' within Earth''s sphere. A milestone''s Outcome may be covered by a must row of an earlier milestone that it keeps.', NULL, '2026-10-14T21:31:46Z', '2026-10-14T21:31:46Z', 64, NULL, 'rule', 64, '2026-10-14, task 64: 2026-10-14, task 64: docs/design.md ''Feature list'' (scenery ships must 3, real asteroids must 4, transfers to asteroids and planets cut), ''The autopilot'' (mid-course corrections kept, transfers cut), ''Asteroids'', ''Each milestone against its Outcome and Done when''; docs/architecture.md ''Files in load order'' milestones per file');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (63, 'POD 14 and Skua Yard''s layout from milestone 3', 'answered', '[]', 'Task 65 found that milestone 3''s scripts rendezvous-skua-yard and skua-yard-apogee-approach (docs/design.md ''Scripted runs'') fly past POD 14 and expect clearance from it, and milestone 4''s rendezvous-peary and return-highport start with POD 14 grappled or delivered, while the feature list, ''Earth-orbit points of interest'', ''Rigid bodies'' and docs/architecture.md places.lisp gave it to milestone 5. One rigid body of an existing kind costs little; moving it earlier makes every script runnable in its milestone and quest 7 flyable from milestone 4 as docs/story.md says. Partly supersedes decision 39: Places in scope (only POD 14''s milestone, 5 in task 55''s consequence).', 'POD 14 (the standard 12 t cargo pod in Skua Yard) and the yard''s layout of decision 53 are built in milestone 3 with the Earth-orbit places, not in milestone 5; quest 7 stays a milestone-5 quest that milestone 4 makes flyable.', NULL, '2026-10-14T21:36:25Z', '2026-10-14T21:36:25Z', 65, NULL, 'rule', 65, '2026-10-14, task 65: 2026-10-14, task 65: docs/design.md feature list row ''POD 14 in Skua Yard, the yard''s layout'' moved to milestone 3, ''Earth-orbit points of interest'' (POD 14), ''Rigid bodies'' (cargo pod) and ''What the story may use'' follow; docs/architecture.md places.lisp lists POD 14 under milestone 3.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (64, 'Scripts always re-enter the dev shell', 'answered', '[]', 'Refines decision 10: Test and screenshot commands. An inherited dev shell can be stale or carry a foreign LD_LIBRARY_PATH: an agent session''s host alsa-lib (glibc 2.43) ahead of luv''s libraries made libSDL3.so fail to load (task 68). Re-entry costs ~0.6 s; the shell hook unsets LD_LIBRARY_PATH and activates luv''s native environment.', 'scripts/test, scripts/screenshot and ./sly re-enter ''nix develop'' unless LIS_DEV_SHELL=1 (set by their own re-exec and by the flake check); an inherited LUV_DEV_SHELL or LUV_DEV_ENVIRONMENT_MODE (direnv) is not trusted.', NULL, '2026-10-14T21:42:45Z', '2026-10-14T21:42:45Z', 68, NULL, 'rule', 68, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (65, 'Vectors and quaternions in math.lisp', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/architecture.md ''Files in load order'' asks for specialised arrays but leaves the layout open. Specialised double arrays are what luv''s write-buffer and SBCL''s unboxed arithmetic handle well, print readably for saves and tests, and compare with equalp bit for bit; in-place twins let the RK4 step (decision 16: Integrator and simulation clock, ~180 ns) run without consing while tests and ./sly eval use the pure forms. Scalar-first matches the integrator''s q-dot = 1/2 q (x) (0, omega) as written. One subtract-then-convert function enforces decision 5: Precision of positions.', 'vec3 is (simple-array double-float (3)), a quaternion (simple-array double-float (4)) scalar first (w x y z), unit, body -> inertial; the plain functions return fresh arrays, and each hot one has an in-place twin with the destination first (vec3-add! out a b), all typed double-float and declaimed inline; no structs or CLOS for them, no luv.arithmetic vec3. The single-float boundary is one function, (relative-single p origin &optional out) = (coerce (- p origin) ''single-float) per component, writing into a (simple-array single-float (3)) or a slot of a larger single-float array for write-buffer.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:01:48Z', '2026-10-14T22:01:48Z', 72, NULL, 'rule', 72, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (66, 'Calendar dates on the 1/64 s grid', 'answered', '[]', 'decision 14: Units and time epoch fixes TT - UTC = 69.184 s, which is not a multiple of 1/64 s, while decision 16: Integrator and simulation clock puts steps and scheduled events on the 1/64 s grid. Rounding every date-derived time onto the grid keeps t exact under power-of-two steps and makes scheduled times land on step boundaries; the 3.5 ms offset is invisible. Keeping days and seconds apart avoids the ~1e-5 s loss of going through a double Julian date.', 'units.lisp converts UTC calendar dates to t exactly (t = (JDN - 2451545) x 86400 + seconds of day - 43200 + 69.184, integer days and seconds kept apart until the last sum) and back; every game time made from a date (the start, schedules, flight-script :date, story dates) goes through (utc->grid-t …), which rounds to the nearest multiple of 1/64 s; *start-date* is the list (2040 1 1 0 0 0) and (start-t) its grid time, 2040-01-01 00:00:00.0035 UTC. Display truncates to whole seconds, so the start shows 00:00:00.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:01:48Z', '2026-10-14T22:01:48Z', 72, NULL, 'rule', 72, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (67, 'Ephemeris cache and the Moon''s series frame', 'answered', '[]', 'decision 18: Ephemerides fixes the models and node spacing; docs/architecture.md ''Ephemerides'' put the table in the game object, but bodies.lisp loads long before main.lisp defines game and tests have no game; nodes are pure functions of their index, so a global cache cannot change a result (Determinism) and stays a cache. Caching the geocentric Moon keeps Earth-frame lookups (the only frame of milestone 2) free of 1.5e11 m cancellations (decision 5: Precision of positions). Precessing the full ecliptic instead of longitude alone removes up to ~20 arcsec of latitude error by 2049 for a few multiplications per node. Horizons vectors are geometric, so the spot checks compare like with like.', 'The Hermite node cache stores two quantities per 4,096 s node: the Earth-Moon barycentre heliocentric (Standish Table 1, analytic velocity) and the Moon geocentric (Meeus ch. 47, velocity by central difference of +-60 s); Earth heliocentric = EMB - mu_Moon/(mu_Earth+mu_Moon) x Moon, and every body''s state in an origin''s frame is built from these in double without differencing two heliocentric values (Moon from Earth = the geocentric Moon itself). The cache is a global defvar in bodies.lisp (a ''cache'' under docs/architecture.md ''Dev loop''), direct-mapped, 64 slots per quantity keyed by node index mod 64, a slot replaced when its index differs; not in the game object. Meeus''s mean geometric ecliptic of date (no nutation, no light time) goes to the J2000 ecliptic by Meeus''s rigorous ecliptic precession (eq. 21.5 with eta, Pi, p), then to ICRF by epsilon = 84381.448 arcsec.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:01:48Z', '2026-10-14T22:01:48Z', 72, NULL, 'rule', 72, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (68, 'Orbit elements: one struct for every conic', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/architecture.md stores hyperbolas as q, e, i, Omega, omega, t_p and ellipses as ''elements at an epoch''; one form for both lets the map, the readout and later rails take any osculating orbit without a branch on the type, and q and t_p stay finite as e -> 1. t_p nearest t keeps M = n (t - t_p) within pi, so no precision is lost to a large mean anomaly. The thresholds sit far below any orbit the game flies and above the roundoff of state -> elements in LEO (~1e-15 relative).', 'orbits.lisp holds one struct, orbit: mu, q (periapsis radius), e, i, Omega, omega, t_p (a periapsis passage time: for an ellipse the one nearest the state''s t), with a, p, n, period and apoapsis derived; it serves ellipses and hyperbolas alike (decision 46: Rails across spheres, hyperbolas and re-entry). Singular cases by convention: e < 1e-11 gives omega = 0 (periapsis at the node, the anomaly is the argument of latitude), i < 1e-11 or > pi - 1e-11 gives Omega = 0 (the node at ICRF +x, the anomaly the true longitude); angles in [0, 2 pi). Round trips compare the non-singular combinations (e cos(Omega+omega), e sin(Omega+omega), tan(i/2) cos Omega, tan(i/2) sin Omega, mean longitude at t) where the classical angles are undefined. Parabolas do not exist: e within 1e-6 of 1 is milestone 4''s nudge.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:01:48Z', '2026-10-14T22:01:48Z', 72, NULL, 'rule', 72, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (69, 'Ship state, step rule and kinematic attitude in physics.lisp', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 2''s part 2 (task 73) puts the state, gravity, RK4 and the step rule in physics.lisp, while docs/architecture.md ''Drift bounds'' took the step rule from clock.lisp; one place for it (physics, loaded before clock) lets tests and prediction call it without the clock. decision 19: Time-warp rules makes attitude kinematic above x4 but leaves how a burn''s direction follows a hold inside a coarse step: evaluating the hold at each RK4 stage keeps the integrator fourth order for thrust along a turning orbital frame and needs no attitude dynamics. The odometer in closed form serves :after-dv cut-offs (decision 21: Physics test bounds and flight scripts) and the dv expectations without an extra integrated variable.', 'physics.lisp holds the ship''s state as a struct ship-state (r, v: vec3 in the dominant body''s frame; q: quaternion body -> inertial; w: body angular velocity vec3; m-p: propellant kg; dv: the delta-v odometer, sum over steps of |F| / mdot x ln(m0 / m1), or |F| h / m when mdot = 0, exact per step since force and mass flow are held over a step) with a deep copy-ship-state; one RK4 step function (rk4-step! state h t inputs) takes body force, body torque, mass flow, the inertia tensor and its inverse about the CoM (held over the step) and a gravity model (:none, :point, :j2, :full = Earth with J2 plus Sun and Moon with indirect terms, Earth frame only in milestone 2) and conses nothing; the coarse step rule (coarse-step r t thrusting) lives in physics.lisp and clock.lisp calls it. Above x4 attitude is kinematic: the step is given an attitude function of (t, r, v) (the active hold''s target, inertial or orbital, with its roll offset), evaluated at each RK4 stage for the thrust direction; q and w are not integrated but set at the step''s end to the function''s value and the target frame''s rate, so a burn in a prograde hold follows the turning velocity exactly.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:07:50Z', '2026-10-14T22:07:50Z', 73, NULL, 'rule', 73, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (70, 'Flight computer: per-step control laws, holds and the RCS mixer', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/design.md ''RCS forces, torques and accelerations'' says the mixer is solved per frame, but decision 16: Integrator and simulation clock and docs/architecture.md ''The clock'' require that the frame rate never changes a trajectory; closed loops (rate damping, holds) run per frame would. At most 256 fine steps per real second (x4) make a ~30 us solve affordable. Weighted BVLS with QR is a small, deterministic, dependency-free way to get ''torque exactly, force as fits'' (design) without an LP or QP solver; the sqrt braking curve is the bang-bang slew at 80 % torque that ''Control modes'' asks for, the linear tail settles it inside 0.1 deg, and feed-forward of the target frame''s rate keeps an orbital hold within bounds as the frame turns 0.065 deg/s in LEO. The design''s roll rule (''the roll at engagement, or by default the cab away from the body'') is ambiguous; the default for orbital holds and the current attitude for R is the reading that makes holds reproducible in flight scripts. The closed-form translation capability avoids a second solve per step and matches the design''s 1,266 N wet and 1,391 N dry (decision 29: Control scheme: key map, control modes, gamepad).', 'The flight computer (controls.lisp) runs once per fine step, before it, from the state at the step''s start, and the mixer with it; key states are sampled once per frame and held, as decision 16 says for controls. Mixer: duty cycles u in [0, 1] for the 24 RCS thrusters by bounded-variable least squares (Stark-Parker BVLS, Householder QR on the active columns, a fixed iteration limit and fixed column order) on the stacked system [w B_torque; B_force; e I] u = [w tau_c; F_c; 0] with w large enough (about 1e6 per metre) that the commanded torque is met within 1e-6 of the couple whenever it is reachable, and e small (about 1e-4 N) so that among equal fits the least-propellant duty set wins; zero command gives u = 0 exactly. Couples for clamps: pitch and yaw 2 x 445 N x 14.0 m, roll 4 x 445 N x 1.8 m, from the layout; fine control x0.1. Direct: a held rotation key commands the full couple on its axis. Rate damping: tau = I (w_c - w) / 0.5 s + w x I w, clamped per body axis to the couple, w_c 5 deg/s (fine 0.5) on held axes, 0 elsewhere. Holds: target attitude q_t(t, r, v) and its frame rate w_t; error rotation vector phi from q* q_t (shortest arc); w_c = w_t + phi-hat min(5 deg/s, sqrt(2 x 0.8 alpha |phi|), 0.5 /s x |phi|), alpha the 80 % couple over the inertia about phi-hat, then the rate law above clamped to 80 % of the couple; settled when |phi| < 0.1 deg and |w - w_t| < 0.02 deg/s, unsettled when |phi| > 0.2 deg or |w - w_t| > 0.05 deg/s. Holds 1-6 engage with the design''s default roll (cab -z radial out; towards normal for the radial holds), R with the current attitude; roll keys during a hold change a roll offset about the hold axis at the rate-damping rate, kept by the hold. Translation: a held key commands the axis''s nominal torque-free force (+-x 1,780 N; +-y, +-z 890 N x (1 + min(a_f, a_r) / max(a_f, a_r)) with a_f, a_r the front and rear blocks'' arms from the CoM), x0.1 in fine control. All flight-computer state (mode, hold, roll offset, inertial target, settled) lives in the controls struct, copied with the simulation state.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:08:04Z', '2026-10-14T22:08:04Z', 73, NULL, 'rule', 73, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (71, 'The Mule''s mass model in ship.lisp', 'answered', '[]', 'Checked by a throwaway script (task 73): these shapes give the design''s CoM rows to 0.01 m and the tensor''s diagonal within 4.2 % (dry I_xx 5.16e4 against 4.95e4, others within 2.5 %), I_xz to the table''s two figures, so the ''Inertia'' table stands and the tests compare against it within 5 %. A 1 kg cut in an estimated part keeps 7,961 / 35,961 kg and every delta-v, load and test figure (85,961 kg wet + 50 t) in docs/ unchanged.', 'ship.lisp builds the Mule''s mass properties from docs/design.md ''Parts'' with these shapes: truss as rods (4 longerons 112.65 kg each at y, z = +-1.8 along x 0-15; 11 frames at x = 0, 1.5 ... 15 of 4 tubes 3.6 m, 16.06 kg each; 40 face diagonals 3.9 m, 17.39 kg, one per bay and face, centred at the bay''s middle; every member x1.15), cab a thin-walled cylinder r 1.5 x 4.5 along x, engine a solid cylinder r 0.725 x 2.2, tanks thin-walled cylinders r 1.6 over their listed lengths (domes ignored), grapple a solid cylinder r 0.8 x 1.6, RCS blocks, gas conditioning, equipment bay, radiators and wings as solid boxes of their listed sizes, cabling and plumbing a box 15 x 0.2 x 0.2; propellant as docs/design.md ''Inertia'' says (solid cylinders r 1.52 from the tanks'' aft ends at x 0.50 and 9.76, lengths f x 8.86 and f x 3.72 for the fill fraction f, LH2 1/6.5 and LOX 5.5/6.5 of the mass). The parts'' listed masses sum to 7,962 kg against the stated dry 7,961 kg of decision 22: The player''s ship and its propulsion; cabling and plumbing (an estimate) becomes 199 kg so the sum is 7,961 kg and every figure derived from it stands. Dry sums (mass, first and second moments about R) are precomputed; per step only the propellant terms and the shift to the CoM are added.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:08:15Z', '2026-10-14T22:08:15Z', 73, NULL, 'rule', 73, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (72, 'Simulation state and the one step function in clock.lisp', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 2''s part 3 (task 74). docs/architecture.md ''Checkpoints'' lists the sim state''s contents for every milestone but leaves milestone 2''s struct, the shape of the step, and how a dv cut-off lands on the grid open. One step function shared by the frame, scripts and prediction is what makes ''the clock''s states equal the direct run bit for bit'' and ''a predicted orbit equals the flown one'' true by construction. The cut-off rule gives the same grid point at fine steps and within one fine step at coarse ones (x100 burns, decision 31: Warp choices within the time-warp rules). A step budget for prediction keeps tests deterministic; decision 20: Trajectory prediction on the frame thread''s 1 ms is ~4,096 coarse steps at the measured cost.', 'clock.lisp holds a struct sim (t, central body :earth, ship-state, controls, gravity model :full by default, the armed dv cut-off or nil, the loss record or nil, the step count) with a deep copy-sim, equalp-comparable; (new-game-sim) builds the start state of decision 38: Start orbit: polar (elements -> state through orbits.lisp, q and w the prograde hold''s target and frame rate, 28,000 kg, new-controls, settled). One function (sim-step! sim level &key until) does one step with every rule: fine 1/64 s at x1 and x4, coarse-step above with the kinematic attitude function, shortened to land on until (a scheduled time on the grid: a script action, a date, an apsis wait) and on the dv cut-off, then flight-step!, then the crash test of hazards.lisp; the live frame (clock-frame!), run-flight and the prediction all call it, so they agree bit for bit. A dv cut-off ends at the first 1/64 s grid point where the odometer reaches the target: at the fine step the step that crosses it, at a coarse step the engine-only closed form h = m0/mdot (1 - exp(-dv_left mdot/|F|)) rounded up to the grid; then the throttle is cut. A separate struct clock (warp level, accumulator, paused, rate meter, messages, the previous ship state for interpolation, a per-step hook for flight scripts) is not part of the sim state and not copied by checkpoints. The frame''s 4 ms cap reads the wall clock through a defparameter (*frame-step-time-limit*, 0.004) that tests bind to nil; the prediction''s per-frame budget is counted in steps (*prediction-steps-per-frame*, 4,096, about 1 ms), so nothing else in the simulation reads a wall clock.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:14:47Z', '2026-10-14T22:14:47Z', 74, NULL, 'rule', 74, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (73, 'Warp levels above x4: entry, holds and lowering', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 2''s part 3 (task 74). decision 19: Time-warp rules and decision 31: Warp choices within the time-warp rules require a hold or low rates to enter kinematic attitude but don''t say what attitude a rate-damped ship holds there (plan 14 already expects the clock to set an inertial hold), what happens when the hold is released above x4, or how often drop-outs are checked; at x1,000,000 a frame is up to 16,667 s of game time, so a once-per-frame check could miss the 3 s rule by a whole frame. A snap keeps the documented kinematic model (no attitude dynamics above x4); the per-step check makes drop-outs exact on the step grid at every frame rate.', 'Entering a level above x4 with no hold (direct or rate damping, |w| < 0.01 rad/s) engages an inertial hold at the current attitude, as R would, shown in the readout and kept when warp comes down; entering under a hold that has not settled snaps the attitude to the hold''s target at the first coarse step (kinematic attitude, decision 69: Ship state, step rule and kinematic attitude in physics.lisp). Switching holds above x4 (1-6, R) is allowed and snaps likewise; releasing the hold (T, the active hold''s key again) lowers warp to x4 with the message ''WARP x4: HOLD RELEASED''. The clock checks before every step, not once per frame: throttle on above the thrust limit (x10 manual, x100 with a dv cut-off armed) lowers warp to the limit (''WARP x10: ENGINE ON''); above x4 without a hold lowers to x4; the next drop-out event at Delta = t_e - t lowers warp to the highest level L with Delta >= 3 s x L (nominal), so below 12 s it is x1 and x1 at the event; the frame''s remaining accumulator is scaled by new/old level. Messages are text plus a 3 s real-time stamp in the clock, worded as docs/design.md ''Time warp'': ''WARP REFUSED: ENGINE ON, LIMIT x10'' (x100), ''WARP REFUSED: ROTATING, SET A HOLD'', ''WARP REFUSED: IMPACT IN <duration>'', ''WARP x<L>: PERIAPSIS BELOW 100 KM'' (Earth; ''MOON IMPACT AHEAD'' for the Moon).', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:14:57Z', '2026-10-14T22:14:57Z', 74, NULL, 'rule', 74, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (74, 'Crash test on the step''s Hermite and the death state', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 2''s part 3 (task 74). docs/architecture.md ''Collisions and the Sun'' prescribes a chord test minus h^2 |a|max / 8 without saying where |a|max comes from; the Hermite''s own second derivative bounds its distance from the chord exactly and costs a few multiplies, so no tunnelling holds for the curve actually tested. Stopping at the exact impact state gives the death view (camera fixed, ship at the impact) and the LOST panel''s facts from one record; the start-state checkpoint makes the off-grid t harmless.', 'hazards.lisp tests each step against Earth (R + 100 km) and the Moon (R) as decision 34: How death works sets: p(s), s in [0, 1], the cubic Hermite of the ship''s position relative to the body from the two relative states (the Moon''s from body-state at both ends); the sag bound is exact for the cubic, max(|p''''(0)|, |p''''(1)|) / 8 (p'''' is linear in s), subtracted from the chord''s closest approach; only below the crash radius is |p(s)|^2 minimised by Newton from the chord''s parameter (endpoints included), and when the minimum is inside, the first crossing |p(s)| = R_c found by bracketed Newton to 1e-9 in s. Death: the sim''s ship takes the Hermite state at the crossing (position, velocity; attitude by slerp, w and m_p of the step''s end), t becomes the impact time (off the grid; the sim then takes no steps), and the loss record holds cause :crash, body, t, speed relative to the body, latitude and longitude (bodies.lisp latitude-longitude). The renderer''s previous state stays the step''s start.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:15:04Z', '2026-10-14T22:15:04Z', 74, NULL, 'rule', 74, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (75, 'Prediction in milestone 2: the step function on a copy', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 2''s part 3 (task 74). decision 20: Trajectory prediction on the frame thread fixes the method (the flight''s integrator, coarse steps, budgeted, states kept) but milestone 2 needs only events for drop-outs, alarms and script waits. Running the same step function on a copy makes the ''predicted state after one orbit equals the flown one bit for bit'' test hold at a coarse level and puts crash detection in the prediction for free; osculating Kepler events would be off by seconds under J2 and the Moon, too much for the 3 s rule at x4. Ending waits on the grid keeps scripts'' times exact (decision 21: Physics test bounds and flight scripts).', 'prediction.lisp predicts by running sim-step! on its own copy of the sim at a coarse level (x1,000: kinematic attitude, the active hold or the inertial hold the step function engages), with the armed dv cut-off as the scheduled control; it keeps every step''s t, r, v (double, Earth-relative) in a preallocated buffer of 20,000 steps, resumable across frames within *prediction-steps-per-frame*. Horizon from the current t: until the copy''s crash, one osculating period (two when asked, for the map), or 20,000 steps. Events: :apoapsis and :periapsis from sign changes of r.v on the stored Hermite, root to 1e-6 s; :impact from the copy''s loss record (time, body, point); :cutoff when the copy cuts the throttle. Restart from the flown sim when the controls'' signature changes, every frame while the throttle is on, when the flown state is more than 1 m from the prediction''s Hermite at its t, when the flown t passes the prediction''s end, and on restore or death; while coasting otherwise only the tail is extended. A flight-script wait on an event ends at the first 1/64 s grid point at or after the predicted time (passed to sim-step! as until). Milestone 2''s drop-out event is :impact; milestone 3 adds burns and contacts, milestone 4 SOI changes, the Sun zone and life support.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:15:11Z', '2026-10-14T22:15:11Z', 74, NULL, 'rule', 74, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (76, 'Frame passes, radiance scale and exposure', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 2''s part 4 (task 75). docs/luv-capabilities.md ''Sun glare and brightness'' proposes HDR, a tone curve, bloom and an exposure rule but leaves them untried; docs/design.md ''The Sun'' and ''Views'' want glare from the bloom chain, stars fading near the Sun and a fixed map exposure. A mip-chain bloom without a threshold spreads the Sun''s 4.6e4 into a wide glare while leaving a lit Earth (1.0) nearly unbloomed, and avoids threshold popping; applying exposure only at the tone map keeps the HDR target within half-float range. The overlay after tone mapping keeps text crisp and unbloomed. One stated radiance scale lets the parts that write shaders (Earth, Sun, stars, meshes) agree without tuning against each other.', 'Each frame is three stages: (1) the scene pass into an HDR :rgba16-float target of the canvas size with the reversed-Z depth of decision 4: Depth strategy (stars, impostors, meshes, ribbons in the order of docs/architecture.md ''Rendering precision''); (2) post.lisp: a bloom mip chain (downsample to 1/2, 1/4, 1/8, 1/16 with a 13-tap filter, upsample with a 9-tap tent, added level by level; no threshold, mixed in at *bloom-strength* 0.04), then one tone-map pass into the canvas or capture target: exposure multiply, ACES filmic (Narkowicz fit, after Luvcraft''s aces-filmic), sRGB encoding in the shader unless the target format is -srgb; (3) the overlay pass (dot-matrix labels, the readout) straight into the canvas target after tone mapping, compare :always, no depth. Radiance scale: a white Lambert surface lit face-on by the Sun at 1 AU has radiance 1.0; the Sun''s disc 4.6e4 at its centre (1361 W/m2 / 6.8e-5 sr against 1361/pi), kept below rgba16-float''s 65,504 because exposure is applied only in the tone-map pass. Exposure rule (post.lisp, CPU): target *exposure-sunlit* 1.0 when the Sun''s centre is within the view''s half-diagonal plus 5 deg, or when a body (Earth, Moon) covers at least 5 % of the frame (pi rho^2 over the frame''s solid angle, capped at 1) times its lit fraction (1 + cos phase)/2 at least 0.05; otherwise *exposure-deep* 16.0; the map view uses *exposure-map* (fixed, set by the map''s part). The exposure eases toward its target in log2 with a 1.0 s time constant of real elapsed time (also while paused); it snaps to the target on the game''s first frame and in captures. All values are defparameters that the Earth/Sun/stars part may retune.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:22:12Z', '2026-10-14T22:22:12Z', 75, NULL, 'rule', 75, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (77, 'The frame in main.lisp: render state, capture of a given sim, part 4''s placeholders and first asset', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 2''s part 4 (task 75). docs/architecture.md ''The clock'' says rendering lags one step and interpolates, but not how the fraction is taken from the accumulator nor what happens after a restore or a death; ''GPU tests'' wants scenes set from a state, which needs a capture of a given sim that does not advance. Removing scene.lisp before Earth, stars, ship and readout exist needs a picture in between; placeholders that cite the task which will plan their replacement keep the PLACEHOLDER rule traceable (the parts'' plans don''t exist yet). decision 13 asks the first task adding an asset to do the packaging; doing it here with the Earth texture lets the loader be tested on the real file and the packaging be seen working in nix run.', 'main.lisp''s game holds a clock and a sim from plan 15''s (new-game-sim), the start-state checkpoint, a camera and a renderer; each canvas frame computes the elapsed real time from luv''s frame time (0 on the first frame), calls clock-frame!, then fills a preallocated render-state and encodes the frame from it. render-state (render.lisp, pure, no GPU): render time t_r = t_prev + s (t - t_prev) with s = clamp(acc / (t - t_prev), 0, 1); ship position by cubic Hermite in double from the previous and current r, v; attitude by quat-slerp; m_p, controls and loss from the sim; when the clock has no previous state, its previous t is not below the sim''s t, or the sim is lost, the sim''s state as it is. Bodies (Sun, Moon, Earth''s body-fixed matrix) are taken at t_r. The camera''s position is stored relative to the ship (an offset in double) and the eye is ship + offset. capture.lisp''s (capture-state pathname sim &key camera frames width height) opens a hidden canvas on a copy of the sim with the clock paused and the exposure snapped, so the PNG shows exactly that state; capture-scene (scripts/screenshot) is capture-state of (new-game-sim). Until the parts that replace them land, part 4 draws placeholders named after the plan-writing task of their part: the Earth as a true-scale UV-sphere mesh sampling the Blue Marble through body-fixed-matrix, and screen-space stars (PLACEHOLDER(76)); no ship mesh (PLACEHOLDER(77)); a dot-matrix label in overlay.lisp (PLACEHOLDER(80)). Part 4 commits the first asset, assets/earth/blue-marble-5400.jpg (Blue Marble NG January, world.200401.3x5400x2700.jpg, decision 40: Earth''s look and drag) with assets/earth/SOURCE.md and CREDITS.md, so that the packaging of decision 13: Asset storage and attribution is checked with a real asset in nix build and nix run; part 5 uses it for the impostor.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:22:22Z', '2026-10-14T22:22:22Z', 75, NULL, 'rule', 75, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (78, 'Sky in numbers: stars, Sun, Moon and Earth''s look', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/design.md ''Earth'' and ''The Sun'' and decision 76: Frame passes, radiance scale and exposure fix the look but leave the numbers open: physical star brightness (Sirius at 6e-11 of sunlight) cannot be seen at any exposure that also shows a lit Earth, so the slope is halved as sky renderers do; Vulkan textures in luv carry one mip level (hal/vulkan/gpu.lisp writes mip 0 only), hence the 1/8 copy and sample-level 0, which also avoids the seam the polynomial atan2''s derivative jump would give. Recorded by task 76: write plan: Earth, Moon, Sun and stars.', 'Stars (stars.lisp): every HYG star of mag <= 6.5 but the Sun as one instanced additive quad at infinity (clip z 0, compare :always, no depth write), a Gaussian of sigma *star-sigma* 0.7 px (+0.15 px per magnitude brighter than 3.0, at most 1.6 px) in a quad of 4 sigma; peak radiance L(m) = *star-radiance-limit* 1.25e-3 x 10^(0.2 (6.5 - m)): half Pogson''s slope, so mag 6.5 shows at the deep exposure (16) and the stars fade at the sunlit one (1.0) except the brightest few, dim; colour from B-V by Ballesteros (2012) to a temperature, a blackbody through the CIE 1931 2-degree functions (Wyman-Sloan-Shirley 2013 fit) to linear sRGB (D65), normalised to luminance 1, mixed toward white by *star-saturation* 0.6; no colour index: white; positions J2000 RA/Dec as ICRF directions, no proper motion. Sun (impostors.lisp): ray-traced sphere at its true distance, centre radiance 4.6e4 (decision 76) with linear limb darkening I/I0 = 1 - 0.6 (1 - mu), colour (1.0, 0.97, 0.94) scaled to luminance 1; glare only from post.lisp''s bloom. Moon: ray-traced grey Lambert sphere, albedo 0.12, lit by the Sun, no earthshine, no texture until milestone 4. Earth: ray-traced sphere R_earth (equatorial) sampling the Blue Marble with sample-level 0 (Vulkan textures in luv have one mip level), mixed with a CPU box-filtered 1/8 copy (675 x 338) by the texel''s footprint in pixels so the far Earth does not shimmer; radiance = texture (linear) x max(n.s, 0) x (1 AU / d_sun)^2; an additive rim on the lit limb (*rim-strength*); a halo impostor of radius R_earth + 100 km, additive, depth-tested without depth write, its radiance the ray''s chord through the shell over the tangent chord (2 sqrt((R+100 km)^2 - R^2)) to the power *halo-power* 1.5, times *halo-radiance* 0.35, blue (0.30, 0.55, 1.0) on the day side, mixed toward (1.0, 0.45, 0.20) where the sun''s elevation at the ray''s closest point is within 6 deg of the horizon, and zero 6 deg below it. Bodies whose angular radius exceeds 45 deg, or that contain the eye, draw as a full-screen triangle (the halo then takes the far root); otherwise a camera-facing quad of the tangent cone. Draw order in the scene pass: stars, Sun, Moon, Earth, Earth''s halo. All values defparameters.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:27:55Z', '2026-10-14T22:27:55Z', 76, NULL, 'rule', 76, '2026-10-14, task 76: 2026-10-14, task 76: plan 17: Earth, Moon, Sun and stars builds it (stars.lisp, impostors.lisp); docs/design.md ''Earth'' and ''The Sun'' and docs/architecture.md take its numbers as the pieces land.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (79, 'Sunlight at a point and the sunlit ship in the exposure rule', 'answered', '[]', 'With decision 76 alone, a sunlit ship at 40 m seen against black space (Earth and Sun out of view) gets the deep exposure 16 and burns out white; the ship is in view in every outside frame, so it decides the exposure there. Earth''s shadow on the ship is needed anyway (the ship''s meshes are dark at night) and later for the Sun''s heat. Recorded by task 76: write plan: Earth, Moon, Sun and stars; partly refines decision 76: Frame passes, radiance scale and exposure (the exposure target only).', 'bodies.lisp gains (sun-visible-fraction p t &optional (origin :earth)): the fraction of the Sun''s disc seen from p, occulted by Earth (radius R_earth) and the Moon (R_moon) as discs, by the exact area of two overlapping circles in angle (no limb darkening, no refraction), the larger occultation of the two kept; pure, double, no HAL, so milestone 4''s hull heat (zero in Earth''s shadow) uses it too. render-state gains sun-fraction, the ship''s, at t_r. Refining decision 76''s exposure rule: the ship counts as a body in the outside view, its coverage pi rho^2 over the frame''s solid angle with rho = asin(*ship-bounding-radius* / d), d the camera''s distance, and its lit fraction the ship''s sun-fraction; the target is *exposure-sunlit* when that product is at least 0.005 (about 240 m for the 10 m bounding radius), as well as by the Sun and body tests of decision 76. *ship-bounding-radius* 10.0 m until the meshes part computes the stack''s.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:28:03Z', '2026-10-14T22:28:03Z', 76, NULL, 'rule', 76, '2026-10-14, task 76: 2026-10-14, task 76: plan 17: Earth, Moon, Sun and stars, piece 3, adds sun-visible-fraction to bodies.lisp, sun-fraction to render-state and the ship term to post.lisp''s exposure-target.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (80, 'The Mule''s meshes in numbers: geometry, draws, materials and lights', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 2''s part 6 (task 77). docs/design.md ''How they look'' lists the parts and materials but leaves sizes of joints, the decal, the lights'' brightness and timing, the material values and the light model open; the cockpit of milestone 3 must match the outside window and portholes. Instancing only the repeated primitives keeps a handful of draws while the unique parts stay one static buffer; the engine as its own draw lets the bell follow ship-trim-angle as plan 14 intends. The body frame''s coordinates are under 20 m, so single floats hold the mesh to 1e-6 m, and only R''s position relative to the eye goes through double (decision 5: Precision of positions). Earthshine matters in LEO (Earth covers half the sky); the term is the irradiance of a uniformly lit disc, scaled by Earth''s lit phase.', 'meshes.lisp generates the Mule in ship body axes (FRD, metres from R, single floats; the body frame spans < 20 m) from docs/design.md ''How they look'' and ''Parts''. Vertex: position, normal, material id, two parameters (axial coordinate along a tube, cone or bell; or decal u, v), 9 singles. A model is one static baked mesh (unique parts: tanks with hemispherical domes, cab, grapple, wings, radiators, boxes) plus instance groups of unit primitives (8-sided tube, box, small cone) with per-instance origin, unit basis and scale (16 singles; normal = basis (n / scale), normalised): 88 truss members (4 longerons, 11 frames of 4, 40 face diagonals), 4 cab struts at x 11.0 and 14.5 from (x, +-1.8, -1.8) to (x, +-0.9, -2.3), 4 engine thrust-frame struts in the plane x = 0 from the aft corners to 0.3 m from the gimbal, 4 grapple mount struts from the front corners to the grapple cylinder''s aft rim, 2 wing booms, 8 RCS blocks (0.3 along x), 24 RCS nozzles (cones 0.12 long, exit 0.08, pointing along each thruster''s exhaust, against its force in ship.lisp), 3 claws. The engine (bell frustum exit 1.45 at x -2.2, throat 0.3 at x -0.6; chamber cylinder 0.5 x 0.6 from x -0.6 to 0; two turbopump boxes 0.3 x 0.25 x 0.25 at y +-0.35) is its own baked mesh drawn rotated by ship-trim-angle about y through the gimbal (0, 0, -0.5), so its axis passes through the current CoM. Cab: cylinder r 1.5 from x 10.5 to 15.0 about (y 0, z -3.5), flat ends; window 1.2 x 0.6 centred (15.0, 0, -3.6) (dark glass: albedo 0.02, specular, faint emission 0.005 for the screens behind it); portholes r 0.175 centred x 14.1 on the wall 37 deg below the y axis, (14.1, +-1.198, -2.597) (where a ray 90 deg right/left and 40 deg down from the cockpit eye (14.1, 0, -3.6) of decision 30: Views and cameras meets the wall; milestone 3''s cockpit uses the same); hatch 0.8 x 1.2 at +y centred x 11.4, outlined in red hand paint (a 0.05 m border); the stencil decal on both cab sides, 2.8 x 0.7 m from x 12.1 to 14.9, centred 25 deg above the y axis, reading left to right from outside: Cairn Water''s cairn (three stacked grey ellipses 0.6, 0.45, 0.3 m wide) then ''M-07'' in black stencil letters 0.6 m tall on a 5 x 7 grid with stencil bridges, drawn on the CPU into a 512 x 128 texture (meshes.lisp, uploaded once). Grapple: cylinder 0.6 x 0.8 from x 15.0, cone from 0.6 to 1.6 over x 15.8 to 16.6, claws 0.1 x 0.1 x 0.6 at 120 deg on radius 0.85 from x 16.0. Wings: 2.0 (x) x 6.0 (y) x 0.05 at (5.0, +-5.3, 0), cells on the -z face; radiators 4.0 (x) x 1.5 (z) x 0.05 vertical fins at (8.0, +-1.2, 2.55). Materials (linear albedo; Blinn-Phong specular weight, exponent; a defparameter table uploaded each frame so ./sly retunes it): steel 0.45 0.45 0.47, 0.15, 16, darker x0.6 within 0.06 m of each tube end (weld nodes); LH2 foil gold 0.80 0.55 0.20 and LOX foil silver 0.75 0.75 0.78, 0.6, 64, normal perturbed by value noise of the body position (amplitude 0.25, cell 0.08 m); cab white paint 0.80 0.80 0.78, 0.05, 8; stencil black 0.04; cairn grey 0.35; red paint 0.50 0.06 0.04; solar cells 0.03 0.04 0.09, 0.4, 128; radiators 0.85, 0.05, 8; boxes (equipment bay, gas conditioning) aluminium 0.60 0.60 0.62; nozzle 0.08 with a throat band 0.35 0.33 0.30 over the bell''s first 0.15 m. Light on the decision 76: Frame passes, radiance scale and exposure scale: sun term = max(n.s, 0) x (1 AU / d_sun)^2 x sun-fraction (decision 79: Sunlight at a point and the sunlit ship in the exposure rule); earthshine = *earth-albedo* 0.3 x (R_earth / |r|)^2 x (1 + rhat.shat)/2 x (1 AU / d_sun)^2 x max(n.(-rhat), 0), tinted 0.85 0.92 1.0; *ship-ambient* 0.0005; no self-shadowing (cut: a shadow map for one ship costs more than it shows). Draws: after the impostors in the scene pass, opaque, depth written, compare :greater; per draw a single rotation mat3-from-quat of the interpolated q and the translation eye-relative-single of R''s position r - q c (c the CoM from ship-mass-properties at the render state''s m_p), plus the gimbal rotation for the engine. Lights, additive camera-facing Gaussian billboards depth-tested without write, at least 1.5 px across, radiance scaled down by the area when enlarged: navigation red (-y) and green (+y) at the truss''s top front corners (15.0, +-1.8, -1.8), radius 0.05 m, radiance 50; white strobe on the cab top (12.75, 0, -5.05), radius 0.08 m, radiance 400 for *strobe-on* 0.1 s every *strobe-period* 1.2 s of render time, the phase reduced in double on the CPU; engine glow at the nozzle exit''s centre, radius 0.8 m, colour 0.45 0.6 1.0 (luminance 1) x *engine-glow-radiance* 1.5 x throttle, only while the engine fires (throttle > 0, propellant > 0, not lost). The grapple''s floodlight and range-finder light come with the grapple (milestone 3). A per-frame emissive colour added to every hull fragment (zero here) and a lights-on flag are the slots plan 2''s part 9 (the death glow) and milestone 4 (lights out) drive. The stack''s bounding radius = the largest distance from the current CoM to any mesh vertex, replacing *ship-bounding-radius* 10.0 of decision 79.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:33:36Z', '2026-10-14T22:33:36Z', 77, NULL, 'rule', 77, '2026-10-14, task 77: 2026-10-14, task 77: plan for part 6 of plan 2 (The Mule''s meshes) builds meshes.lisp, the ship''s pipelines, lights and glow with these numbers; post.lisp''s *ship-bounding-radius* 10.0 is replaced by the computed radius.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (81, 'Input in milestone 2: held keys per frame, taps, gating and the shared key map', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 2''s part 7 (task 78). docs/design.md ''Controls'' and docs/architecture.md ''The clock'' fix the key map, held keys, focus loss, real-time ramps and the per-frame sample (decision 16: Integrator and simulation clock, decision 29: Control scheme: key map, control modes, gamepad) but not how a tap shorter than a frame survives the per-frame sample, what keys do while paused, lost or above x4 (where a pitch key would release the hold the warp needs, decision 73: Warp levels above x4: entry, holds and lowering), nor where the key map lives when flights.lisp, a simulation file loaded before input.lisp, must press the same keys (decision 56: Flight-script actions, start states and the quest play-through). Keeping events out of controls until the frame makes the zero-order hold exact and the whole input path testable with synthetic events and no canvas.', 'input.lisp keeps its own input state in the game (held physical keys, a tap latch, the right-drag flag); canvas events only change that state or run a discrete command, and once per frame, before clock-frame!, input-frame! writes the held rotation and translation keys into the sim''s controls and runs the real-time throttle ramp. A key pressed and released between two frames counts as held for exactly one frame (the design''s shortest tap). Repeat events (canvas-key-event-repeat-p) are ignored; focus loss clears held keys and latches, ends a drag and leaves relative pointer mode; the throttle lever keeps its value. The held-key map (W/S pitch -/+, A/D yaw -/+, Q/E roll -/+, H/N +x/-x, I/K -z/+z, J/L -y/+y; opposite keys cancel) is data in controls.lisp with (controls-apply-held-keys! controls keys), so flights.lisp''s (:keys ...) action and input share it without loading input.lisp; Shift-left/Control-left ramp the lever at 0.5 per second of real elapsed time (min(elapsed, 0.1 s)), a press from off giving 0.1, below 0.1 off. Discrete keys (F, Z, X, T, R, 1-6, . , /, P, C, Home, M, Tab, Page keys, Escape, Control+Q) act at the press. Gating: paused: only P, C, Home, M, Tab, Page keys, the mouse and quit act, flight keys are ignored (the ramp stops); above x4 (clock-kinematic-p): rotation and translation keys are ignored with the clock message ''RCS OFF ABOVE x4'' once per press, holds, T, R, F and the throttle act; lost (sim-loss): flight keys ignored, part 9 binds Return and N. The window title lists the main keys by QWERTY position (layout characters come with milestone 6''s F1 help); luv exports no key-name-to-layout-character function, a wishlist item.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:37:37Z', '2026-10-14T22:37:37Z', 78, NULL, 'rule', 78, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (82, 'Outside view camera: angles in a frame, toggles that keep the picture, distance and easing', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 2''s part 7 (task 78). decision 30: Views and cameras and docs/design.md ''Views'' set the outside view''s range, frames and default but not how the camera is parametrised, what C does to the picture, the drag direction and rate, or whether the wheel eases; angles in the chosen frame make Home, C and the frame-following behaviour one formula, and pure functions of the render state keep the camera testable without a GPU (docs/architecture.md ''Dev loop'').', 'camera.lisp''s outside-view (game state, not in the sim or its checkpoints; kept across a restart) holds the frame mode (:orbital or :locked), azimuth, elevation, distance and target distance. The eye sits at the CoM (ship-state r) plus distance x (cos el cos az, cos el sin az, -sin el) in the frame''s FRD basis: :orbital x prograde, z radial in (Earth below), from the interpolated r, v; :locked the ship''s body axes from the interpolated q. The camera looks at the CoM with the frame''s +z as down; elevation clamped to +-85 deg. Default (and Home): az 180 deg, el 20 deg, 40 m (decision 30: Views and cameras, part 4''s outside-camera-default!). C converts the current eye direction into the other frame''s angles, so the picture does not move at the toggle. Right-drag (relative pointer mode while held): az += 0.25 deg per pixel of delta-x, el -= 0.25 deg per pixel of delta-y (mouse right moves the camera right around the ship, mouse up raises it). Wheel: target distance x 1.2 per notch (scroll up nearer), clamped to [1.2 x the stack''s bounding radius, 5 km]; the distance eases to the target exponentially in log with tau 0.15 s of real time as the map''s zoom does, snapping in captures. The bounding radius is the largest distance from the current CoM to a corner of a part''s box in ship.lisp''s parts table (about 12.6 m wet, so the minimum is about 15 m; decision 30''s ''12 m'' was an estimate of the same rule). All numbers defparameters.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:37:37Z', '2026-10-14T22:37:37Z', 78, NULL, 'rule', 78, '2026-10-14, task 78: Sign correction (task 78, same day): the drag rule meant is the parenthetical one, mouse right moves the eye to the viewer''s right around the ship; with the eye direction (cos el cos az, cos el sin az, -sin el) in FRD that is az -= 0.25 deg per pixel of delta-x (from az 180 deg the eye''s y component becomes positive). el -= 0.25 deg per pixel of delta-y stands (luv''s delta-y is positive downwards, so mouse up raises the eye). plan 19: Input and the outside view uses this sign.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (83, 'Map camera: framing on opening, focus, zoom, drag and exposure', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 2''s part 8 (task 79). docs/design.md ''Views'' and decision 30: Views and cameras give the map''s range, steps and opening distance but not its basis, azimuth, field of view, what Tab keeps, what Home and C do, nor the value of decision 76''s *exposure-map*; and its two sentences on the opening focus contradict each other. A basis fixed at opening keeps the orbit still on screen while the ship goes round it; az -45 deg was checked by hand: at az -90 deg a 400 km ship seen from 2.5 radii sits 21.7 deg off Earth''s centre, behind the 22.0 deg limb. Recorded by task 79: write plan: The basic map.', 'camera.lisp''s map-view (game state, not in the sim or its checkpoints; kept across a restart) holds the focus (:earth or :ship), a basis fixed when the map opens or Home is pressed (n = unit r x v of the render state, e1 = unit r, e2 = n x e1; n = ICRF z when |r x v| < 1e-9 |r||v|), azimuth, elevation, distance and target distance. Eye = focus position + distance x (cos el cos az e1 + cos el sin az e2 + sin el n), looking at the focus with n''s projection as up; el clamped to +-85 deg; vertical FOV 50 deg, near 0.1 m; the camera is stored relative to its anchor (decision 5). M opens the map framed on the orbit: focus :earth, az -45 deg (behind the ship''s motion and on its side, so a LEO ship lies in front of Earth''s disc, not behind its limb), el 30 deg above the orbit plane on the normal side, distance 2.5 x max(apoapsis radius, |r|) (|r| for a hyperbola), clamped; M again returns to the outside view as it was. docs/design.md ''Views'' says both ''the ship (default when the map opens)'' and ''opening the map frames the ship''s orbit: focus the dominant body''; the framing rule wins, Tab reaches the ship. Tab cycles :earth -> :ship -> :earth keeping basis, az, el and distance (clamped to the new focus''s range: ship 50 m, Earth 1.2 R_earth, both up to 1.5e13 m); the anchor jumps. Home re-frames as on opening; C does nothing in the map. Wheel x1.2 per notch (up nearer), Page-up /10, Page-down x10 on the target distance; the distance eases in log with tau 0.15 s of real time (decision 82''s rule), snapping in captures. Right-drag: az -= 0.25 deg per pixel of dx, el -= 0.25 deg per pixel of dy (decision 82''s signs). Exposure: *exposure-map* 4.0 (the geometric middle of decision 76''s 1.0 and 16.0: Earth''s ocean and land below white, stars to about magnitude 4 visible), snapped to on entering the map and to the outside target on leaving (no 1 s ease across views). Flight keys act in the map with decision 81''s gating unchanged. All numbers defparameters.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:43:34Z', '2026-10-14T22:43:34Z', 79, NULL, 'rule', 79, '2026-10-14, task 79: plan 20: The basic map builds it (camera.lisp map-view, input.lisp''s map commands replacing PLACEHOLDER(79), post.lisp''s *exposure-map*); docs/design.md ''Views'' takes the opening focus and numbers as the pieces land.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (84, 'Map ribbons and markers: osculating orbit, adaptive segments, overlay markers and visibility', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 2''s part 8 (task 79). docs/design.md ''Views'' fixes the line (2 px, cyan, impacts red), the markers'' existence and the 6 px rule; docs/luv-capabilities.md ''Orbit lines and markers'' leaves adaptive segments and marker shapes ''assumed''. Uniform segments fail near the ship: 512 chords over a LEO orbit have a sagitta of ~130 m, a visible offset at the 50 m ship-focus distance, so segments are split by their error in pixels. Markers in the overlay keep their colours and edges exact (no bloom, no tone map) and need no new shader; a CPU occlusion test gives one visibility rule for markers and labels, testable without a GPU. The impact marker comes from the osculating orbit because the line it marks is osculating in milestone 2 (plan 15''s note named the prediction; the alarm keeps it). Recorded by task 79: write plan: The basic map.', 'ribbons.lisp draws, in the map view only, the ship''s osculating orbit about Earth from the render state (state->orbit with mu_earth), recomputed each frame into preallocated double buffers relative to Earth''s centre (no single-float cache, docs/architecture.md ''Floating-origin pitfalls''): an ellipse as 256 segments uniform in eccentric anomaly, each split into 2^k (k <= 8) so that its chord''s sagitta seen from the eye is at most *ribbon-max-error-px* 0.25 px, at most *ribbon-max-segments* 8192 in total (k lowered from the farthest segments first when over); a hyperbola as the branch out to Earth''s Laplace sphere radius on both sides, split by the same rule. Each segment is one instance with both endpoints eye-relative (double difference, then single), expanded to 2 px in the vertex shader, endpoints behind the eye clipped at w = near; in the scene pass after the meshes, compare :greater, no depth write, additive; radiance = linear colour x *ribbon-radiance* 1.0 / the current exposure, so the line reads the same at any exposure. Colour cyan #40D0FF; a segment whose midpoint radius is below the crash radius (R_earth + 100 km) red #FF3030. Markers and their labels are screen-space triangles in the overlay pass after the tone map (decision 76''s stage 3, decision 7''s pipeline), in exact colours: the ship a cyan ring of 12 px, always drawn, even behind Earth (findable at every zoom); apoapsis and periapsis cyan diamonds of 9 px labelled ''AP <n> KM'' / ''PE <n> KM'' (altitude above R_earth rounded to whole km; no AP on a hyperbola); the impact a red X of 12 px labelled ''IMPACT'' at the osculating orbit''s next inbound crossing of the crash radius after t_r (orbit-radius-crossings), so it sits on the drawn line (the prediction''s impact stays the readout''s alarm, part 9); Earth, the Moon and the Sun, when their projected diameter H tan(asin(R/d)) / tan(fov/2) is below 6 px, a grey #8A8A80 dot of 7 px labelled ''EARTH'', ''MOON'', ''SUN'', drawn in addition to their impostor. A marker other than the ship''s, and its label, is hidden when its point is behind the eye or the segment from the eye to it meets Earth''s or the Moon''s sphere (double, CPU). Labels in paper white #E8E4D8 dot-matrix glyphs, 8 px right of the marker''s centre, vertically centred. All numbers defparameters.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:43:48Z', '2026-10-14T22:43:48Z', 79, NULL, 'rule', 79, '2026-10-14, task 79: plan 20: The basic map builds it (ribbons.lisp, overlay.lisp markers and the completed glyph table); the map''s impact marker comes from the osculating orbit, not from prediction-event as plan 15''s closing note said.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (85, 'Minimal readout: layout, formats, colours and alarms', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 2''s part 9 (task 80). docs/design.md ''Instruments'' lists the milestone-2 rows and ''Readability at 1280 x 800'' the sizes, colours and the never-colour-alone rule, but not the layout, the units and decimals of each value, where the alarms come from, nor how the readout keeps the other parts'' GPU region checks valid. Osculating apsides match plan 20''s map markers; the impact time comes from the prediction as decision 84: Map ribbons and markers: osculating orbit, adaptive segments, overlay markers and visibility left to this part. An opaque box keeps the 7:1 contrast true over a sunlit Earth without changing the overlay pipeline. Recorded by task 80: write plan: Minimal readout, messages and the LOST panel.', 'overlay.lisp draws the milestone-2 readout top left in the outside and map views as 5x7 dot-matrix text (decision 7: Placeholder labels: dot-matrix overlay quads) with a 2 px dot pitch (cap 14 px), cells 12 x 22 px (docs/design.md ''Readability at 1280 x 800'': 0.86 x cap, 1.6 x cap), 16 px margin, on an opaque near-black #0A0C0C box sized to its rows (contrast exact, no blending in the overlay pipeline). The glyph table adds % / ! + ( ) , ° × … to plan 20''s A-Z 0-9 space - . :, and every string is upcased; a character missing from the table is a test failure, not a blank. Rows, labels grey #8A8A80, values paper white #E8E4D8: ''ALT'' altitude |r| - R_earth in km, one decimal; ''SPD'' |v| in Earth''s inertial frame, m/s with one decimal below 1,000 m/s, else km/s with three; ''AP'' and ''PE'' osculating apsis altitudes (state->orbit, mu_earth) in km with one decimal, AP ''ESCAPE'' when e >= 1; ''THR'' lever in whole % and ON (throttle > 0 and propellant > 0) or OFF; ''PROP'' whole kg and whole % of 28,000 kg; ''MODE'' DIRECT, RATE DAMP or HOLD <PROGRADE|RETROGRADE|NORMAL|ANTI-NORMAL|RADIAL OUT|RADIAL IN|INERTIAL> with SETTLED or SLEWING, then FINE when on; ''WARP'' ×<nominal level>, then ''ACT ×<rate>'' in amber with ''!'' when the clock''s actual rate is below 90 % of nominal (3 significant digits), then PAUSED; ''UTC'' format-utc of t_r truncated to seconds. Values from the render state (interpolated, t_r), so they move smoothly. Alarms below the rows: amber ''! PE BELOW 100 KM'' while the osculating periapsis radius is below R_earth + 100 km; red inverted (red block, #0A0C0C text) flashing at 2 Hz in real time ''<EARTH|MOON> IMPACT IN <duration>'' while the clock''s prediction has an :impact event (time to it from t_r, the same duration text as the clock''s warp messages). Then the clock''s live messages (clock-messages, 3 s each), newest last, at most 3, amber with ''!'', cut at 40 characters with ''…''. The readout and alarms are a pure function of the render state, the clock and the real time (a list of lines with colour and style), tested without a GPU; captures take :readout (default t) so GPU tests that check scene regions can turn it off. All sizes and colours defparameters.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:47:39Z', '2026-10-14T22:47:39Z', 80, NULL, 'rule', 80, '2026-10-14, task 80: 2026-10-14, task 80: plan 21: Minimal readout, messages and the LOST panel builds it (overlay.lisp readout-lines and readout-vertices!, the extended glyph table, capture-state''s :readout).');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (86, 'Death view, LOST panel and restart in milestone 2', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 2''s part 9 (task 80). docs/design.md ''Dying and what follows'' and decision 34: How death works fix what is shown (outside view, camera fixed, 3 s orange glow for an Earth impact, the LOST panel with body, speed, latitude, longitude, Return and N) but not the glow''s curve and brightness, where the panel sits, how the restart resets the views and input, nor whether the camera may move. A fixed camera follows from the stopped ship, so letting the mouse orbit it costs nothing and keeps decision 81''s rule that views act while lost. Clearing input on restart keeps the N press from leaking into a translation. Recorded by task 80: write plan: Minimal readout, messages and the LOST panel.', 'main.lisp notices the death on the first frame whose sim has a loss (the game keeps the loss it last saw): it switches game-view to :outside and keeps the outside view''s offset, so the camera stays where it was relative to the ship, which no longer moves (the render state takes the sim''s impact state, decision 77: The frame in main.lisp: render state, capture of a given sim, part 4''s placeholders and first asset); the mouse may still orbit it and M still opens the map (decision 81: Input in milestone 2: held keys per frame, taps, gating and the shared key map). It records the real time of the death (the game''s summed elapsed). The ship''s emissive slot of plan 18 (decision 80: The Mule''s meshes in numbers: geometry, draws, materials and lights) carries the entry glow for a :crash into Earth: orange #FF6A1A at *death-glow-radiance* 4.0 (on the radiance scale of decision 76: Frame passes, radiance scale and exposure, divided by the current exposure so it reads the same lit or in shadow), full for the first 1 s of real time after the death, then falling linearly to 0 at 3 s; a Moon impact has no glow (no atmosphere). The LOST panel appears at once, centred horizontally with its top at 60 % of the height (the ship, at the view''s centre, stays visible): an opaque #0A0C0C box in the readout''s text: ''LOST: CRASH'' as a red inverted block (steady), then ''<EARTH|MOON>  <UTC of the impact>'', ''SPEED <speed relative to the body, as the readout''s SPD>'', ''LAT <|lat|, 2 decimals>° <N|S>  LON <|lon|, 2 decimals>° <E|W>'', a blank line, ''RETURN  RESTART FROM START'', ''N       NEW GAME'' (keys paper white, texts grey). The readout stays, showing the impact state. Return and N are in *key-map* and act only while lost (flight keys are ignored then; N is translate-back otherwise); in milestone 2 both call (game-restart! game): clock-restart! with the start-state checkpoint (equalp to (new-game-sim)), the view :outside, the outside view reset as Home does, the map view closed, the input''s held keys, taps and drag cleared (so the restarting N does not translate), the death record cleared, the exposure snapped. A capture of a lost sim shows the glow at its peak and the panel (capture-state''s :death-elapsed, default 0). All numbers defparameters.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:47:52Z', '2026-10-14T22:47:52Z', 80, NULL, 'rule', 80, '2026-10-14, task 80: 2026-10-14, task 80: plan 21: Minimal readout, messages and the LOST panel builds it (main.lisp''s death record and game-restart!, death-glow-radiance into plan 18''s emissive slot, lost-panel-lines, Return and N in *key-map* while lost).');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (87, 'Flight scripts in milestone 2: additions, apsis conditions, live fly and scripts/fly', 'answered', '[]', 'task 81 (plan for part 10 of plan 2: masterplan). docs/architecture.md ''Scripted runs'' leaves open how milestone 2''s scripts restart after a crash, check the predicted impact, centre a burn on an apsis and show the map, and its ''within 1%'' for raise-orbit cannot hold on osculating apsides under J2 in a polar orbit. Driving headless and live runs through one per-step function keeps them bit-identical; ignoring the player''s flight keys during a live flight keeps input-frame! from overwriting the script''s keys.', 'flights.lisp reads a script with *read-eval* nil, double floats, a scratch package, one form, and rejects any symbol that is not a keyword; actions, waits and expects of a later milestone are rejected at read time with ''NOT BEFORE MILESTONE <n>''. Additions to decision 56''s vocabulary: (:restart) presses Return while lost (clock-restart! with the run''s start state headless, game-restart! live); (:expect :start-state) holds when the sim equals the run''s start state (equalp); (:expect :impact-as-predicted <s>) compares the loss''s t with the :impact time the prediction gave when the last (:wait :impact) began; event waits take :before <s> ((:wait :apoapsis :before 18.5) ends at the grid point at or after the predicted time less s, for burns centred on an apsis); (:screenshot "<label>" [:view :map]) captures the map instead of the outside view. Apsis conditions (:apoapsis-above/below, :periapsis-above/below) use the prediction''s apsides, the radius at the next predicted passage less R_earth, not the osculating elements the readout shows, since under J2 a polar 400 km orbit''s osculating periapsis swings 367-400 km over one orbit and the true radius only 393-400 km (a throwaway RK4 run, not committed); with an impact predicted first, the periapsis is the osculating one at the impact state. run-flight steps a clock through the clock''s one-step function (lowering rules, the hook, sim-step!; factored out of clock-frame! if plan 15 kept it inline) with no accumulator, calling prediction-update! before each step as the frame does; (:warp :coast) raises in the hook, per step, so live and headless take the same steps. run-flight takes :model (default the sim''s :full) so tests can fly a script two-body. Scripts: burn (x1: 10 s full prograde, about 28.4 m/s, apoapsis about 500 km), raise-orbit (Hohmann 400 -> 800 km: 109.1 m/s cut by :after-dv, then 107.6 m/s centred on the apoapsis with :before, both under (:warp :coast)), deorbit (retrograde hold, 102.3 m/s for a 50 km periapsis, 50 km below the crash radius as plan 15''s test, wait :impact, :lost :crash, :impact-as-predicted 0.1, (:restart), :start-state). raise-orbit''s 1% of the 400 km raise is checked exactly in a two-body run (:model :point: both apsides 800 +- 4 km); the script''s own full-model bands are 800 +- 8 km (1% of the apsis altitude), the worker measures and widens with a comment if J2 needs it. Live: (fly name) restarts the game at the script''s start state, sets the view outside, installs the hook and returns; (flight-status) prints the record so far; while a flight runs input-frame! leaves the controls alone (flight keys ignored as when lost; P, C, Home, M, the mouse and quit act); it writes no PNGs (./sly screenshot does). scripts/fly <name> <out-dir> writes <n>-<label>.png per checkpoint by capture-state and prints one line per checkpoint, burn and failure; --from and <name>.end.sexp come with saves.lisp in milestone 5. Done-when screenshots are not committed: scripts/fly regenerates them bit for bit.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:52:31Z', '2026-10-14T22:52:31Z', 81, NULL, 'rule', 81, '2026-10-14, task 81: 2026-10-14, task 81: plan 22: Flight scripts and the Done when builds on it; its pieces update docs/architecture.md ''Scripted runs'' (the additions, apsis conditions on the prediction, clock-step!, what scripts/fly writes before milestone 5).');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (88, 'Warp keys while paused', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 19: Input and the outside view lists . , / among the keys acting while paused and its tests check that . acts while paused; decision 81''s paused list omitted them. docs/design.md ''Time warp'' says only flight inputs are ignored while paused, and warp keys are not flight inputs. If the clock of plan 15 refuses a level change while paused, the clock''s rule stands and input only forwards the command.', 'While paused, . , / act: they change the nominal warp level (with the clock''s refusals and messages) that P''s resume restores; the simulation stays paused. This completes the paused list of decision 81: Input in milestone 2: held keys per frame, taps, gating and the shared key map, which named P, C, Home, M, Tab, Page keys, the mouse and quit.', NULL, '2026-10-14T23:01:04Z', '2026-10-14T23:01:04Z', 88, NULL, 'rule', 88, '2026-10-14, task 88: 2026-10-14, task 88: task 120: input.lisp part two: the key map''s commands, the throttle ramp, gating and the window title implements it.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (89, 'Ephemeris test bounds and the start Sun''s RA', 'answered', '[]', 'Measured in task 95: cubic Hermite truncation over 4,096 s reaches 2.5 cm for the Moon independent of the velocity difference step; decision 18''s 2 cm was an estimate, and halving the spacing for 1 cm buys nothing visible. Horizons astrometric ICRF at 2040-01-01 00:00 UT gives RA 280.454 deg, Dec -23.086 deg; the plan''s 281 deg +- 0.5 deg would fail by 0.05 deg. beta = -9.6 deg and 25.2 deg from the Sun stay within task 96''s 1 deg bounds.', 'The Moon''s Hermite-vs-series bound is 3 cm (2.5 cm measured at 4,096 s nodes, which decision 18: Ephemerides fixes); the Earth = EMB - f x Moon identity is tested within 1e-4 m, not 1e-6 m (one ulp of a 1.5e11 m heliocentric value is 3e-5 m); the Sun at the start is near RA 280.5 deg in ICRF (281 deg is of date), docs/design.md ''The start'' says so.', NULL, '2026-10-14T23:20:59Z', '2026-10-14T23:20:59Z', 95, NULL, 'rule', 95, '');

